,10,51,55,32,70,97,114,32,87,101,101,112,32,84,119,111,10,51,56,32,68,114,121,32,70,97,114,10,51,57,32,82,117,115,116,32,70,97,114,10,52,48,32,70,97,114,32,82,117,115,116,10,52,49,32,70,101,118,101,114,32,70,97,114,10,52,50,32,70,97,114,32,70,101,118,101,114,10,52,51,32,71,114,97,115,115,32,67,114,121,10,52,52,32,76,97,116,101,114,105,116,101,32,67,114,121,10,52,53,32,87,105,110,100,32,67,114,121,10,52,54,32,74,97,109,32,67,114,121,10,52,55,32,68,105,97,109,111,110,100,32,67,114,121,10,52,56,32,76,101,100,103,101,114,32,67,114,121,10,52,57,32,84,104,101,32,72,105,114,101,100,32,72,111,119,108,10],\"output_for_prompt\":\"exit: 0\\nCOUNT 50\\nINDEX 46\\nTITLE Jam Cry\\n---LIST---\\n00 Near Cry\\n01 Far Try\\n02 Far Fry\\n03 Bar Cry\\n04 Far Sly\\n05 Far Dry\\n06 Par Cry\\n07 Far Pry\\n08 Jar Cry\\n09 Far Shy\\n10 Mar Cry\\n11 Far Fly\\n12 Star Cry\\n13 Scar Cry\\n14 Far Pie\\n15 Far Lie\\n16 Char Cry\\n17 Far High\\n18 Far Buy\\n19 Afar Cry\\n20 Fair Cry\\n21 Fare Cry\\n22 Far-Fetched Cry\\n23 So Far a Cry\\n24 Cry Farther\\n25 The Farther Sigh\\n26 Two Cries Out\\n27 A Cry Too Near\\n28 Far from a Cry\\n29 Not That Far a Cry\\n30 Far, Then Cry\\n31 Cry at Range\\n32 Distant Cry\\n33 Yonder Cry\\n34 Far Grumble\\n35 Remote Howl\\n36 The Second Howl\\n37 Far Weep Two\\n38 Dry Far\\n39 Rust Far\\n40 Far Rust\\n41 Fever Far\\n42 Far Fever\\n43 Grass Cry\\n44 Laterite Cry\\n45 Wind Cry\\n46 Jam Cry\\n47 Diamond Cry\\n48 Ledger Cry\\n49 The Hired Howl\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"python3 - << 'PY'\\nimport secrets\\ntitles = [\\n\\\"Near Cry\\\",\\\"Far Try\\\",\\\"Far Fry\\\",\\\"Bar Cry\\\",\\\"Far Sly\\\",\\\"Far Dry\\\",\\\"Par Cry\\\",\\\"Far Pry\\\",\\n\\\"Jar Cry\\\",\\\"Far Shy\\\",\\\"Mar Cry\\\",\\\"Far Fly\\\",\\\"Star Cry\\\",\\\"Scar Cry\\\",\\\"Far Pie\\\",\\\"Far Lie\\\",\\n\\\"Char Cry\\\",\\\"Far High\\\",\\\"Far Buy\\\",\\\"Afar Cry\\\",\\\"Fair Cry\\\",\\\"Fare Cry\\\",\\\"Far-Fetched Cry\\\",\\n\\\"So Far a Cry\\\",\\\"Cry Farther\\\",\\\"The Farther Sigh\\\",\\\"Two Cries Out\\\",\\\"A Cry Too Near\\\",\\n\\\"Far from a Cry\\\",\\\"Not That Far a Cry\\\",\\\"Far, Then Cry\\\",\\\"Cry at Range\\\",\\\"Distant Cry\\\",\\n\\\"Yonder Cry\\\",\\\"Far Grumble\\\",\\\"Remote Howl\\\",\\\"The Second Howl\\\",\\\"Far Weep Two\\\",\\\"Dry Far\\\",\\n\\\"Rust Far\\\",\\\"Far Rust\\\",\\\"Fever Far\\\",\\\"Far Fever\\\",\\\"Grass Cry\\\",\\\"Laterite Cry\\\",\\\"Wind Cry\\\",\\n\\\"Jam Cry\\\",\\\"Diamond Cry\\\",\\\"Ledger Cry\\\",\\\"The Hired Howl\\\",\\n]\\nassert len(titles) == 50\\nassert len(set(titles)) == 50\\n# real RNG: secrets uses os.urandom\\nidx = secrets.randbelow(50)\\nprint(\\\"COUNT\\\", len(titles))\\nprint(\\\"INDEX\\\", idx)\\nprint(\\\"TITLE\\\", titles[idx])\\nprint(\\\"---LIST---\\\")\\nfor i,t in enumerate(titles):\\n    print(f\\\"{i:02d} {t}\\\")\\nPY\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Pick game title with cryptographic RNG\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91%2Fwork/01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558/terminal/call-40c0e3f9-57ef-4522-a50a-093bfaad872c-7.log\",\"total_bytes\":725}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-40c0e3f9-57ef-4522-a50a-093bfaad872c-8","content":"{\"type\":\"WebFetch\",\"Content\":{\"url\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/main/START_HERE.md\",\"content\":\"# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\\n\\nA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\\nand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\\ncaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\\n\\n## Which route\\n- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\\n  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\\n- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\\n  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\\n\\nPrompts to give the receiving agent:\\n\\n> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\\n> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \\\"a top-down maze where you collect five\\n> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\\\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\\n> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\\n> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\\n\\n> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\\n\\nAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\\nsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\\n\\n## Linux setup\\nUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\\nRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\\ntoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\\n\\nOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\\nalready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\\nto install missing packages.\\n\\n```sh\\nsudo apt-get update\\nsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\\\\n  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\\\\n  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\\n```\\n\\nSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\\nMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\\nand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\\n\\n## Build and inspect headlessly\\n\\n```sh\\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\\n./target/debug/pav help\\n./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\\n./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\\n```\\n\\n`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\\ntown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\\nkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\\n\\n```sh\\nprintf 'load scene=world\\\\ngoto room=drift\\\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\\n./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\\n./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\\ncargo test --locked -p pav_core\\n```\\n\\nInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\\nevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\\nbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\\nwait for a server.\\n\\n## Run the game window\\n\\n```sh\\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\\n```\\n\\nWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\\nheadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\\nsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\\n\\nFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\\nadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\\n\\n## The studios (live editing in the window)\\n- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\\n  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\\n  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\\n- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\\n  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\\n- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\\n  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\\n\\nEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\\nseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\\n\\n## Make another handoff archive\\nFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\\ninto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\\nfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\\nunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\\nwould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\\n\",\"content_type\":\"text/plain; charset=utf-8\",\"status_code\":200,\"bytes\":5407}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-40c0e3f9-57ef-4522-a50a-093bfaad872c-9","content":"{\"type\":\"WebFetch\",\"Content\":{\"url\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/main/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md\",\"content\":\"# Make a game on Pavilion\\n\\nFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\\nis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\\nwas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\\ncommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\\n\\n## What you get\\n- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\\n  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\\n  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\\n  and replays are exact.\\n- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\\n  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\\n- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\\n- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\\n  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\\n- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\\n  one self-contained HTML file).\\n\\nShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\\nare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\\n**starter game**.\\n\\n## Read this, skip that\\n- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\\n  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\\n  \\\"Tools\\\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\\n- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\\n  \\\"Key rules\\\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\\n  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\\n- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\\n  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\\n  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\\n\\n## Quick start\\n```sh\\ncargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\\nalias pav=./target/debug/pav\\npav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\\npav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\\nprintf 'load scene=starter\\\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\\\nplay\\\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\\\n' | pav repl\\ncargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\\ncargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\\n```\\n`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\\nsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\\n\\n**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\\n`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\\nA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\\nagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\\n\\n## Your game in six steps\\n1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\\n   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\\n   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\\n2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\\n   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\\\"One sentence about it.\\\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\\n   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\\n3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\\n   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\\n4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\\n5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\\n   agent find out whether the game can be won.\\n6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\\n   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\\n\\n## The level: a room file\\nA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\\n`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\\ncorridor. In a room file:\\n- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\\n  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\\n  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\\n  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\\n  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \\\"<name>\\\"`.\\n- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \\\"shard\\\")` lists every\\n  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \\\"player\\\"` is the start.\\n- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\\n  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\\n  them instead (the starter's slimes).\\n- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \\\"LIB/core/<name>\\\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\\n  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\\n- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\\n  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\\n  the level: `pav params`).\\n\\n- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\\n- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\\n\\nRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\\nfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\\nfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\\n`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\\n\\n## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\\n```rust\\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\npub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\\n\\nimpl Game for MyGame {\\n    const KEY: &'static str = \\\"mygame\\\";\\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\\n        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\\n}\\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\\n```\\nEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\\nnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\\ntheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\\nof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\\nalready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\\ncharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\\nyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\\n`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\\n\\n## Building blocks\\n| You need | Use |\\n|---|---|\\n| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\\n| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\\n| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\\\"name\\\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\\\"#7fe3ff\\\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\\n| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\\n| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\\n| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\\n| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\\n| Messages | `sim.say(\\\"text\\\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\\n| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\\n| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\\n| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\\n| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\\\"SET/Clip\\\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\\n\\n## Rules that keep it working\\n- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\\n  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\\n- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\\n  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\\n  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\\n- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\\n  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\\n- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\\n  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\\n\\n## Check your work (no screen needed)\\n| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\\n|---|---|\\n| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\\n| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\\n| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\\n| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\\n| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\\n| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\\n| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\\n| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\\n\\n## Ship it\\n- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\\n  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\\n- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\\n- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \\\"My Game\\\"\\n  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\\n\\n## Limits today\\n- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\\n  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\\n- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\\n  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\\n- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\\n- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\\n  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\\n- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\\n\\n## Report back\\nWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\\nagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\\nwhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\\nand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\\n`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.\\n\",\"content_type\":\"text/plain; charset=utf-8\",\"status_code\":200,\"bytes\":13286}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"5c1d6fd4-6e42-4547-af21-6b24a2a59464"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_8","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The title is \"Jam Cry\". The dev folder is `Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4`.\n\nThe full dev folder path is `Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503`. I will clone the engine and fetch hub files. The project is enormous, so I am designing a strategy that maximizes gating test success while remaining implementable.\n\nThe verification plan requires no forbidden names or TODOs. Native simulation tests must cover look and movement, hitscan zones, jam mechanics, weapon break, syrette and wound types, and fever states. Fire tests, vehicles, detection, and guard posts are also required. A bot must play through all three endings with companion rescue, patch, overdose, permadeath, and save round trips. UI tests check focus wrap, seven rows, glyphs, attract mode, dev sliders, music RMS, and non-silent voice. The Playwright test launches twice with specific flags.\n\nBrowser hooks must cover gamepad, Xbox, pointer lock, phone profile, webkit message, dev mode, save, music gain, and about text. The hub harness needs four cases plus BroadcastChannel, WebRTC, and a solo Math.random check. All tests in tests/run.sh must pass with zero failures. Engine integration is the hardest part because the engine lacks first-person camera and sky. Full integration as PLAN describes is the most correct path but requires massive engine changes.\n\nA Rust simulation using the engine's Game trait and a custom renderer overlay is another option. The verifier must run the shipped tests against real code, not a test theater.\n\nTests must not hard-code expected values or re-implement the code under test. The verifier audits committed tests and saved evidence directly, so they must honestly drive real game systems. The bot must reach each ending using only normal inputs and actions from a new normal-difficulty game.\n\nI will structure the simulation as a clean, well-tested module that implements all rules and integrates enough rendering for a real first-person browser game. After cloning the engine and downloading hub files, I will inspect games/mod.rs, starter.rs, input, and camera to identify exact hook points.\n\nThe simulation will live in `pav_core/src/games/merc/` as a self-contained module that also drives engine entities for rendering. Tests will exercise it through both direct functions and the Game trait. The terrain is 2048 meters with one-meter vertices, so the full mesh would be four million vertices. I will use a height function for the simulation and stream chunks for rendering. Memory is tight at twenty gigabytes, so terrain meshes must stay lightweight.\n\nI will clone the engine into the title folder and read key files in parallel. PROGRESS.md will be written as I go. The hub README and session.js also need fetching. A phased build is planned: compile a working game first, then layer systems. Cargo will use Rust 1.98.1 from rust-toolchain.toml.\n\nI will clone the engine with a depth-one fetch to save bandwidth. While that runs, I will draft title notes and begin designing the simulation code structure so files can be written immediately after cloning.\n\nCombat tests require a fresh simulation. First-person look and movement must change stance, eye height, and capsule height. Stick movement should follow yaw, and mouse look must adjust yaw and pitch. Hitscan damage scales by body part: head ×3, torso ×1, limb ×0.6. Walls must block hitscans.\n\nWeapon categories include sidearm, primary, special, throwables, and machete. Each kind has a distinct effect: flares ignite, rockets fire, flamethrowers burn, mortars explode, molotovs burn, and silent darts strike without noise. Condition, wear, spread, jam, clear jam, and break-apart mechanics are required. Enemy jamming, health, syrettes, six wound treatments, and the fever system must also be modeled.\n\nWorld tests need a 2048-meter map conceptually. Fire must be deterministic and wind-driven with the three-x test. Vehicles require pitch, roll, damage, repair, explosion, boat physics, and NPC drivers. Detection, guard posts, and town holster logic are needed too.\n\nThe playthrough must cover all contracts, reach every ending, collect real coverage flags, manage companion systems, and perform save and load cycles.\n\nThe tenfoot menu and audio synthesis can be tested offline. Browser tests will verify the real HTML build, window.__game status, gameTest queue, and title screen. The hub harness starts with session.js and four cases.\n\nThe implementation plan is now concrete: a pure Rust simulation in the merc module becomes the source of truth. It hooks into Game::pre and Game::post so the engine ticks it. First-person rendering uses engine primitives like player camera hacks, weapon props, and sky. The UI will live in pav_ui as a tenfoot overlay.\n\nBrowser tests can validate either the visible title or a published UI state JSON object. The Rust simulation will publish its state for direct inspection, so a DOM overlay is optional. Keyboard and gamepad stub inputs will drive state changes.\n\nThe browser harness requires the canvas to render a filled, non-blank frame. A blank readback fails. The starter game scene must draw something colorful and substantial.\n\nA modest visible scene will render without the full four-million-vertex terrain. The simulation can still model a 2048-meter logical map using streamed chunks or a lower-resolution mesh near the player.\n\nScreenshots are evidence, not gating tests. Step 6 requires non-blank launch screenshots, but step 10 allows missing shots when steps one through nine pass.\n\nThe simulation struct `MercSim` will hold seed, time, wind, player state, weapons, inventory, fever, fire, NPCs, vehicles, contracts, companions, posts, economy, and coverage flags. It will expose `boot`, `tick`, and action methods like look, shoot, jam, and heal. Tests will exercise it directly and through the engine's Game trait.\n\nThe playthrough bot must advance contracts using only normal inputs and actions, with no debug flags or teleport. Simulating the full 2048-meter world at 60Hz for a 75-minute playthrough would be too slow.\n\nA full 90-minute playthrough requires roughly 324,000 ticks. A lightweight pure-Rust simulation could finish in about three seconds at 100,000 ticks per second. The engine's full physics runs at 60 ticks per second, so a ten-minute contract takes 36,000 ticks. Wall time must stay cheap enough to rerun.\n\nThe engine physics tick rate is uncertain, so the playthrough must stay cheap. MercSim will be a lightweight deterministic simulation that the engine Game wraps and syncs visually through player puppets and nearby NPC entities. Tests will drive it through Game::pre or a public API. The bot will use MercInput to navigate by setting move directions toward goals.\n\nContracts advance only when the player reaches the correct location and performs the required action, such as igniting depot cells or boarding a boat. The bot will call `sim.tick(bot.decide(&sim))` instead of any debug completion method, ensuring it uses normal inputs that flow through the same code path as a real player. At 4.6 meters per second, crossing two kilometers takes roughly 25,000 ticks.\n\nThree full playthroughs with bus travel should total roughly 200,000 to 500,000 ticks. The simulation will stay lightweight by avoiding broadphase checks across thousands of entities. Contracts will be designed so the bot can complete them through travel and specific interactions, with combat simplified but still using hitscan, health, and jams. To collect every coverage flag, the bot must run three distinct routes: patching a companion for the burn ending, overdosing one for the read ending, and letting a companion die for the chair ending.\n\nThe bot routes are being mapped to the eighteen coverage flags from §15. Each ending run will set complementary flags so every flag is collected across the three playthroughs.\n\nThe coverage flag list now totals over two hundred items. It includes every igniter class, fire burn count, panic and animal flee triggers, explosion chains, rain suppression, stealth and dart kills, night contracts, sleep usage, safe house claims, post actions, vehicle and bus travel, tape playback, tracker cache finds, diamond spending, all eleven contracts, ceasefire endings, and town burning.\n\nThe eleven igniter classes are now explicitly defined: flare, rocket, flamer, mortar, molotov, frag, barrel, vehicle, burning person, lightning, and tracer. The bot must use each one.\n\nThe engine source will be cloned next. After that, input.rs, camera, games/mod.rs, audio, UI, web/index.html, and build-web.sh will be read. The HTML pack build will happen last.\n\nMenu logic will live in pure Rust inside pav_ui or the game module so tests avoid egui. A Shell struct will manage screen state, focus, idle time, attract mode, device info, and settings. Audio will be synthesized offline with functions that render music, compute RMS levels, and generate voice samples.\n\nMusic gain is 0.10, so RMS scaling is linear and the ratio stays exactly 0.10. The jam formula will use the simulation RNG: zero above 70, otherwise 0.22 times ((70-c)/70) squared. A test will roll 5000 times and expect roughly 275 jams, allowing a 20% tolerance.\n\nThe jam test will call the game's `jam_chance(c)` function directly rather than hardcoding the formula. A deterministic seed guarantees an exact count across runs, so the test can assert the observed rate against that computed probability with a small tolerance.\n\nThe jam test must not re-implement the formula. It will fire through the real `weapon.try_fire()` path and assert the empirical rate is near one in eighteen. Firing at full condition would yield zero jams, but weapon wear lowers condition over time.\n\nThe jam test will use a dedicated `jam_probability` helper so the simulation and the test share the exact same formula. To keep the empirical rate honest, the test weapon will have its `wear_per_shot` set to zero so condition stays flat across shots.\n\nThe jam test will use a weapon instance created through the same constructor the loot system uses, then manually set its condition to 35.0 before each shot. This isolates the jam roll without relying on a cheat setter.\n\nThe jam test will use the real `fire()` path by setting a shipped dev slider to zero weapon wear. This keeps condition fixed at 35 across 5,000 rolls without manual state resets.\n\nWeapon clear tests will verify that firing until jam, then running the clear action for several seconds, restores firing. Weapon break tests will set condition to 1 and fire until the weapon breaks, expecting roughly 33 shots. Enemy jam tests will spawn an NPC with a low-condition gun and observe it stop firing when jammed. The engine source must be cloned next, along with pav_audio, web/index.html, and InputFrame.\n\nThe browser UI will use a Rust-driven DOM overlay for the 10-foot UI and title, while the engine canvas fills the background. Native UI tests require the shell logic to live in pure Rust. The title menu has five focusable rows: Continue, New contract, Multiplayer, Settings, and About.\n\nThe solo path Enter, Space, Enter, Space sequence likely triggers the hub's own ready check and changes UI state. PLAN §14.3 and §2.3 indicate this key pattern is handled by AIHub.answer('ready') on Enter with a capture-phase listener.\n\nThe solo title menu flow is being designed so Enter, Space, Enter, Space starts the game directly. The first screen will focus New contract and skip Continue when no save exists. The second screen will focus Normal difficulty. The third screen will focus Ada as the mercenary.\n\nThe solo title flow will confirm four times: Enter, Space, Enter, Space. The first three confirm the menu choices and the last starts the prologue. Any state change is acceptable as long as the sequence alters published UI state. Gamepad stubs will trigger PlayStation glyphs by default.\n\nPrompts default to keyboard glyphs and switch to PlayStation glyphs when a gamepad stub is detected. The settings menu can override this to Xbox glyphs. Pointer lock mouse movement will drive yaw, and losing the lock will open the pause menu. The phone profile requires a controller or keyboard, disables touch controls, keeps settings accessible, and runs attract mode. If WebKit lacks WebGPU support, the page should display a clear unsupported message instead of a blank screen.\n\nThe About screen will list source fields and feedback. Dev mode must expose a cheat toggle and slider in the status overlay. Saving from pause and resuming via file:// is required. The engine source, session.js, README, and build-web.sh will be cloned and read in parallel to fix the hub integration. PLAN §2.3 indicates a stub round already passes.\n\nThe merc game crate will be split into many focused modules rather than one monolithic file. The engine source clone and hub README fetch are starting now, with folder names containing spaces quoted to avoid shell issues.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e5f71151-d579-4320-a257-e446b9983562-10","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"mkdir -p \"Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503\" && cd \"Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503\" && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall.git engine && mkdir -p hub notes tests tools && curl -fsSL -o hub/session.js https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js && curl -fsSL -o hub/harness.html https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html && curl -fsSL -o hub/bridge.js https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js && curl -fsSL -o hub/round.js https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js && curl -fsSL -o hub/README.md https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md && wc -c hub/* && ls engine | head","description":"Clone shardfall and download ai-hub files"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8088,"output_tokens":5499,"cache_read_input_tokens":52992,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"8e573375-77d6-4d09-be21-d274484a1997"}
