\\\"Shardfall: a level of the descent. 'level/<n>' is depth n (1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\\\"),\\n];\\n\\n/// The scenes a menu offers: the built-in ones, then the games (`crate::games::GAMES`).\\npub fn menu() -> Vec<(&'static str, &'static str)> {\\n    SCENES.iter().copied().chain(crate::games::GAMES.iter().map(|g| (g.key, g.about))).collect()\\n30→}\\n\\n/// All scene names, including standalone rooms (any room key builds just that room).\\npub fn names() -> Vec<String> {\\n    let mut v: Vec<String> = SCENES.iter().map(|s| s.0.to_string()).collect();\\n    v.extend(crate::games::GAMES.iter().map(|g| g.key.to_string()));\\n    let (rooms, _) = room::parse_all(&room::load_sources(room::rooms_dir().as_deref()));\\n    v.extend(rooms.into_iter().map(|(k, _)| k));\\n    v\\n}\\n40→\\n/// Builds one room on its own at its layout origin (fast; used by tests and agents).\\npub fn build_standalone_room(sim: &mut Sim, key: &str, def: RoomDef) {\\n    let place = Placement::new(def.layout.origin, 0);\\n    let (cols, rows) = def.layout.extent();\\n    let (min, max) = place.aabb(Vec3::new(0.0, -1.0, 0.0), Vec3::new(cols as f32, def.height.max(2.0), rows as f32));\\n    let start = def.local_start();\\n    let inward = -def.entrance.facing.dir();\\n    let def = Arc::new(def);\\n    sim.state.world = World {\\n50→        enabled: false,\",\"content_concise\":\"1→//! Scenes: the full world, small code-defined test scenes, and single rooms on their own.\\n\\nuse std::sync::Arc;\\n\\nuse anyhow::{Result, anyhow, bail};\\nuse glam::{Quat, Vec3};\\n\\nuse crate::color::Color;\\nuse crate::entity::{Behavior, BodyKind, Spawn};\\n10→use crate::level::Placement;\\nuse crate::room::{self, RoomDef};\\nuse crate::shape::{Look, Shape, Visual};\\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\\nuse crate::statics::Block;\\nuse crate::world::{RoomSlot, World};\\n\\npub const SCENES: &[(&str, &str)] = &[\\n    (\\\"world\\\", \\\"The pavilion with every room, surrounded by streaming wilderness. 'world/<room>' starts in a room.\\\"),\\n    (\\\"test\\\", \\\"Lit test scene: a small plaza, every primitive and style, props raining onto a pyramid.\\\"),\\n20→    (\\\"empty\\\", \\\"A single floor slab with a player.\\\"),\\n    (\\\"arena\\\", \\\"Shardfall: the Proving Grounds, a wave arena (combat test ground).\\\"),\\n    (\\\"town\\\", \\\"Shardfall: Emberwatch, the town (smith, stash, portal).\\\"),\\n    (\\\"lab\\\", \\\"Shardfall: the Menagerie, a gallery of designed and generated creatures.\\\"),\\n    (\\\"level\\\", \\\"Shardfall: a level of the descent. 'level/<n>' is depth n (1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\\\"),\\n];\\n\\n/// The scenes a menu offers: the built-in ones, then the games (`crate::games::GAMES`).\\npub fn menu() -> Vec<(&'static str, &'static str)> {\\n    SCENES.iter().copied().chain(crate::games::GAMES.iter().map(|g| (g.key, g.about))).collect()\\n30→}\\n\\n/// All scene names, including standalone rooms (any room key builds just that room).\\npub fn names() -> Vec<String> {\\n    let mut v: Vec<String> = SCENES.iter().map(|s| s.0.to_string()).collect();\\n    v.extend(crate::games::GAMES.iter().map(|g| g.key.to_string()));\\n    let (rooms, _) = room::parse_all(&room::load_sources(room::rooms_dir().as_deref()));\\n    v.extend(rooms.into_iter().map(|(k, _)| k));\\n    v\\n}\\n40→\\n/// Builds one room on its own at its layout origin (fast; used by tests and agents).\\npub fn build_standalone_room(sim: &mut Sim, key: &str, def: RoomDef) {\\n    let place = Placement::new(def.layout.origin, 0);\\n    let (cols, rows) = def.layout.extent();\\n    let (min, max) = place.aabb(Vec3::new(0.0, -1.0, 0.0), Vec3::new(cols as f32, def.height.max(2.0), rows as f32));\\n    let start = def.local_start();\\n    let inward = -def.entrance.facing.dir();\\n    let def = Arc::new(def);\\n    sim.state.world = World {\\n50→        enabled: false,\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/scenes.rs\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"//! Scenes: the full world, small code-defined test scenes, and single rooms on their own.\\n\\nuse std::sync::Arc;\\n\\nuse anyhow::{Result, anyhow, bail};\\nuse glam::{Quat, Vec3};\\n\\nuse crate::color::Color;\\nuse crate::entity::{Behavior, BodyKind, Spawn};\\nuse crate::level::Placement;\\nuse crate::room::{self, RoomDef};\\nuse crate::shape::{Look, Shape, Visual};\\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\\nuse crate::statics::Block;\\nuse crate::world::{RoomSlot, World};\\n\\npub const SCENES: &[(&str, &str)] = &[\\n    (\\\"world\\\", \\\"The pavilion with every room, surrounded by streaming wilderness. 'world/<room>' starts in a room.\\\"),\\n    (\\\"test\\\", \\\"Lit test scene: a small plaza, every primitive and style, props raining onto a pyramid.\\\"),\\n    (\\\"empty\\\", \\\"A single floor slab with a player.\\\"),\\n    (\\\"arena\\\", \\\"Shardfall: the Proving Grounds, a wave arena (combat test ground).\\\"),\\n    (\\\"town\\\", \\\"Shardfall: Emberwatch, the town (smith, stash, portal).\\\"),\\n    (\\\"lab\\\", \\\"Shardfall: the Menagerie, a gallery of designed and generated creatures.\\\"),\\n    (\\\"level\\\", \\\"Shardfall: a level of the descent. 'level/<n>' is depth n (1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\\\"),\\n];\\n\\n/// The scenes a menu offers: the built-in ones, then the games (`crate::games::GAMES`).\\npub fn menu() -> Vec<(&'static str, &'static str)> {\\n    SCENES.iter().copied().chain(crate::games::GAMES.iter().map(|g| (g.key, g.about))).collect()\\n}\\n\\n/// All scene names, including standalone rooms (any room key builds just that room).\\npub fn names() -> Vec<String> {\\n    let mut v: Vec<String> = SCENES.iter().map(|s| s.0.to_string()).collect();\\n    v.extend(crate::games::GAMES.iter().map(|g| g.key.to_string()));\\n    let (rooms, _) = room::parse_all(&room::load_sources(room::rooms_dir().as_deref()));\\n    v.extend(rooms.into_iter().map(|(k, _)| k));\\n    v\\n}\\n\\n/// Builds one room on its own at its layout origin (fast; used by tests and agents).\\npub fn build_standalone_room(sim: &mut Sim, key: &str, def: RoomDef) {\\n    let place = Placement::new(def.layout.origin, 0);\\n    let (cols, rows) = def.layout.extent();\\n    let (min, max) = place.aabb(Vec3::new(0.0, -1.0, 0.0), Vec3::new(cols as f32, def.height.max(2.0), rows as f32));\\n    let start = def.local_start();\\n    let inward = -def.entrance.facing.dir();\\n    let def = Arc::new(def);\\n    sim.state.world = World {\\n        enabled: false,\\n\",\"total_lines\":263}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"372c3c9b-859e-4447-9da7-961f4e52ee94"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_41","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"\"merc\" is not a scene name. Room keys are located in the rooms directory.\n\nA valid merc.toml will be created in games/ with view and layout fields. Sim::new(\"merc\") should build the room and game quickly.\n\nHistory will be set to zero before many steps. The test uses World rather than the engine character. A goal enum will drive the bot campaign. `progress()` advances goals at the end of each tick. The bot generates inputs only for the current goal. Stuck goals cause test failure.\n\nCompletion radii will be generous. The bot campaign begins at the Title screen and confirms through menus to reach the Playing state. Goals include raising the map, setting a waypoint town, and walking toward it until a fever warning triggers or the gate is reached. Flow A0 starts at the airstrip at night.\n\nA0 will take roughly 76 seconds to walk the road. Fever triggers at town, and the bot will either burn pills or wait for blackout before waking with the companion and flagging contract_a0.\n\nA1 covers GP1: clear enemies, scripted jam, rescue, wound treatment, loot rifle, claim pump house, shop purchase, sleep, and flag a1.\n\nCombat is mechanical: enemies have 30 health, the player rifle does 26 damage, so two shots kill them. Perfect accuracy is assumed when the bot aims directly.\n\nThe bot will aim at enemies before firing. A scripted jam is triggered by setting a story flag on the first shot of A1, forcing the weapon to jam once; the bot then clears it. A separate natural jam test uses the standard formula.\n\nAfter the jam, a story beat applies lethal damage to drop the player to zero health while the companion is alive. The rescue sequence plays if the companion survives. The bot waits through this and flags player_rescued.\n\nA graze will drop the player to 15 health and set the wound cause. The bot holds heal, and treatment restores 40 health.\n\nWorld-side consequences such as spawning enemies or applying damage are triggered when a goal starts. This is legitimate game scripting rather than a test cheat. The campaign progress module handles these events automatically as the player reaches each point.\n\nUnit tests will use `World::fresh` to spawn a sandbox player in a field with weapons. They will exercise combat rules through `tick` and public actions only, keeping campaign logic separate. The public API exposes position, yaw, eye height, capsule height, and tick actions.\n\nA `Boot` struct will parameterize the new-game constructor, exposing seed, weapon condition, wear scale, and enemy state. `World::sandbox(seed)` is being considered as a dedicated test constructor for quick weapon and combat setup.\n\nWeapon condition is game state that changes during combat. A debug setter will be gated behind `cfg(test)` so tests can force a condition of 35 for the jam test without exposing it in shipped code.\n\nA `#[cfg(test)]` setter is unavailable to external integration tests. The jam rate unit test will instead use the shipped `dev_give_weapon` command or a fresh weapon specification. This avoids adding a debug toggle and keeps the jam measurement clean.\n\n`World::pickup_weapon` will be the public path for both loot and tests to set a weapon condition. A `weapon_wear` Tunable slider will default to 1.0 and can be zeroed in tests to keep condition at 35. Jam clearing will take 1.8 seconds, with bolt-slap reducing that by 40%; tests will simply hold reload for two seconds without slapping.\n\nA weapon will break once its condition drops below 8. The test will set a weapon condition of 5 and zero wear, then fire repeatedly until the weapon breaks; with a fixed seed this is deterministic, so it expects roughly 33 shots and asserts the slot switches to the next weapon. A hash function will be added for deterministic testing. A watchdog will fail the playthrough if the player position stops moving and the goal does not advance for 30 seconds of simulation time.\n\nA `steer_to` helper will compute yaw from the player's current position to a target coordinate. It returns no input when the player is within three units of the destination, letting `progress()` advance the goal automatically.\n\nThe `steer_to` helper clamps horizontal look to ±0.5 and triggers sprint once the yaw error drops below 0.4 radians. A data-driven `Goal` enum is being defined to drive the campaign, with variants for reaching coordinates, holding the map, setting waypoints, following GPS, taking pills, waiting for blackout, clearing posts, forcing jam clears, rescuing, treating wounds, looting weapons, and claiming houses.\n\nThe Goal enum now includes vehicle driving, mounted combat, igniting devices, waiting for cells, boat travel, sleep, stealth, dart use, repair, tape application, cache management, spending resources, patching, overdosing, abandoning the contract, ending firefights, burning towns, story endings, weapon breakage, weather events, animal encounters, panic, chain reactions, reman effects, night vision, and monocular use.\n\n`on_enter` in `begin_goal` handles world setup when a goal starts, such as spawning enemies or setting the time to night. The bot does not call this directly; `tick` triggers it automatically when the goal index changes.\n\nThe three campaign routes share early goals but diverge at specific points. Route 1 burns pills and handles most of the early content. Route 2 waits for blackout, overdoses, reads the ending, and may trigger lightning or cigarette events if route 1 missed them. Route 3 deals with companion death and chair events.\n\nRoutes 2 and 3 will not skip side content. Every route plays all contracts. Endings are gated behind D1, which requires the full contract chain. This ensures all coverage flags are set without relying on story gates.\n\nEach route needs roughly one hundred thousand ticks. The read-ending goal alone takes about ten thousand ticks, but that is acceptable.\n\nThe B1 depot burn will fire five hundred cells. Spread speed is tuned so the wait lasts fifteen to forty seconds.\n\nThe Ignite goal will auto-complete when the bot fires the correct weapon at a flammable cell. Fire spread will be deterministic so the 3x burn ratio holds under a fixed seed; if the test shows 2.9, the spread constant will be raised. Rain weather will be set to extinguish fire cells.\n\nRain will immediately clear all burning cells once it starts. The games test only needs to verify key uniqueness and Sim::new works, so HUD checks can be skipped. Tests will use expect with context instead of unwrap on potentially failing map lookups. PROGRESS.md and title notes will be written before the main Rust files.\n\nA shell command will inject session.js into index.html after the Rust build succeeds. Adding an optional fp field to CameraRig is safe because the rig lives on the view side and is not part of the simulation snapshot; serde default handles Option as None automatically.\n\nThe `fp` field will be initialized to `None` in `CameraRig::new()` and `Default`, with `data()` checking it first. When the first-person camera is active, `forward()`, `eye()`, `up()`, `ground_axes()`, and `relative_move()` will use its yaw, pitch, and roll so movement matches the view. The `FpCam` struct stores eye position, yaw, pitch, roll, and FOV.\n\nThe first-person camera forward vector is being derived from yaw and pitch. Positive pitch will point upward. The up vector will be computed by tilting world up around the forward axis using roll.\n\nThe first-person camera up vector will fall back to a safe default when the player looks straight up, since cross-product with world up fails in that case. The far plane will be 3000 and the near plane 0.08 when the first-person rig is active. The app will set rig.target to 40 meters ahead for shadow centering, because CameraRig::data uses fp.eye directly rather than target. Next, I will write `pav_app/src/merc_bridge.rs` with the WASM bridge functions.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-f7c71fff-4e70-4aa6-981b-3a05e5630346-91","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"CursorMoved|MouseWheel|fn on_event|DeviceEvent","glob":"**/*.rs","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src","head_limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7329,"output_tokens":3825,"cache_read_input_tokens":164864,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"e751330d-78c9-45bc-bd1d-4ff793ca5514"}
