:\"    target_arch = \\\"wasm32\\\",\"},{\"line_number\":176,\"content\":\"    feature = \\\"wasm-bindgen\\\"\"},{\"line_number\":178,\"content\":\"extern crate wasm_bindgen;\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-adc4c313-793d-4b6f-b670-ca1ff96333da-94","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"e5bbc999-7dbb-4b76-b523-26834c93b488"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_44","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"cpal supports WASM when its feature is enabled.\n\nThe workspace cpal is version 0.18 without explicit features, so WASM support likely comes from defaults. The engine already plays audio in the browser, so events will trigger sound. JavaScript music will be added for reliable continuous playback at gain 0.10 to match the settings.\n\nThe same simple algorithm will be ported to both Rust and JavaScript so the two sources match. The RMS test is Rust-only. I am now writing the files and updating the todos.\n\nCampaign bot goals will be designed to remain completable. The world simulation loop will be sketched in pseudocode before translation. Three full campaigns in one test may be slow, so a tick budget and progress output during development are planned. Clone and Serialize bounds on World are being weighed against Fire BTreeMap size; with history disabled, per-tick cloning should be minimal.\n\nSave tests will use JSON instead of full simulation CBOR. World derives Serialize and contains Rng, which is also serializable. Act is Copy and passed by reference to World::tick.\n\nShell screens are being designed with at most seven items each. The main menu offers Continue, New contract, Multiplayer, Settings, and About. Difficulty selection shows Easy, Normal, and Hard. Mercenary dossiers list four entries. The pause screen includes Resume, Settings, Controls, Dev mode, and Quit.\n\nSettings are organized into four tabs, each with at most seven sliders. Sound covers Master, Music, SFX, and Voices. Controls lists Prompts, Invert, Stick, Mouse, Aim assist, Crouch mode, and Sprint mode. Picture adjusts Fullscreen, FOV, Brightness, and Head bob. Access toggles Subtitles, Reduce motion, Assist markers, and HUD.\n\nDev mode pages include Cheats, Teleport, Spawn, World, and Balance. Cheats and Balance use two pages of seven toggles or sliders each. Teleport, Spawn, and World list seven items per page.\n\nThe About screen is paged text with Next and Back buttons.\n\nMultiplayer offers This computer, Invite code, and Back. The Controls diagram is a single page with Back. The contract card shows Accept and Decline.\n\nA test will verify every screen has at most seven focusable rows. It will drive MenuInput to visit each screen and check row counts. A parallel `max_rows_audit` method will build every screen state for a comprehensive audit.\n\nA shared `rows()` function will provide focusable counts for both the renderer and tests. Focus wraps around: pressing down from the last item returns to the first, and pressing up from the first returns to the last. Hovering sets focus based on pointer position against row y coordinates. The idle attract mode activates after ten seconds of no input in title and shell menus; any input exits it immediately. The test will tick the shell with no input for nine seconds, assert that attract is not running, then tick for another 1.1 seconds and assert it starts. Each attract segment lasts about four seconds in test speed.\n\nAttract segments will last 16 seconds each. Five loops total about eight hundred seconds, or roughly forty-eight thousand ticks. The test will advance through every segment index and repeat the loop five times.\n\nIf no save is written, the test asserts that the save count stays unchanged.\n\nDev mode sliders will be tested by entering from pause, adjusting a value, confirming the simulation reads the new setting, and verifying that reset restores the original value. The same struct the UI modifies is the one the simulation reads.\n\nThe shell will be implemented independently of the world so UI tests do not need the full simulation. A prompt string function will display device-specific button labels such as \"Cross\" or \"A\". The shell can hold a shared Tune struct that the world also uses, or it can live inside the world. UI tests will create a fresh World and drive shell inputs through Act menu fields. Ticks update only the shell when the game is idle. To test dev mode sliders, the test will start a game, open pause, enter dev mode, and adjust values.\n\nThe idle attract threshold is 600 ticks, or ten seconds at a 1/60 tick rate. The test harness will use a `check` helper that collects only failed assertions into a vector for clean output.\n\nI will mark the plan checklist as I finish items. The plan is read and milestone zero is in progress.\n\nI will use edition 2024 with stable features only.\n\nWeapon fire rate converts RPM to a per-shot interval. The jam test will call a `pull_trigger` function that fires one shot when the interval allows. A very high RPM in the weapon definition lets the test fire thousands of shots without waiting through thousands of ticks.\n\nThe jam test will use a `fire_cooldown_scale` tunable to bypass the weapon's RPM limit. This lets the test fire one shot per tick while holding fire, avoiding thousands of ticks of waiting.\n\nThe jam test will use the pistol at 300 RPM. Five thousand shots take about one thousand seconds, or sixty thousand ticks. This is acceptable because weapon condition does not degrade and cooldown is the only limit. A shot accumulator will fire automatic weapons when accumulated shots reach one per tick.\n\nThe bolt rifle is 40 RPM. The test will pull the trigger once per tick and advance cooldown by exactly one interval between shots. This avoids a time-skip helper that would also advance fever timers.\n\nThe jam test will use a fixed 0.2-second tick to fire one shot per call, avoiding thousands of ticks. Semi-auto weapons fire on the rising edge of the fire input once cooldown expires.\n\nThe jam test will call `pull_trigger` directly, but the test must first zero out `player.shot_cooldown` so each shot actually fires. This keeps the cooldown logic in the normal path instead of bypassing it.\n\nA `step_trigger` helper will be added to the test file. It resets the player cooldown and ticks with fire held, bypassing the normal cooldown wait while keeping the jam roll in the real tick path.\n\nThe fire simulation will exit early when no burning cells exist. The playthrough bot will target grass with igniter weapons to test the ignite function.\n\nLightning will strike dry grass near the player during storm weather. A bar mission script lights a cigarette that ignites a small grass patch outside. The tracer rifle can randomly ignite dry grass on every third round.\n\nThe tracer igniter chance will be fixed at 0.25 per round so the bot can reliably light dry grass within forty shots. A fixed random seed keeps the test deterministic. Zebra herds will flee when a nearby shot is fired.\n\nA goal will test zebra fleeing by shooting near them. Enemy panic flags will appear when fire is lit under a squad. A barrel explosion chain will trigger when two barrels are shot in succession. The wait-for-rain goal will schedule a storm via the contract script.\n\nRain will be tested by starting a fire and letting it burn out. The bot will sleep away from the cleared GP1 area to trigger post-remanning, checking distance and view cone. Wind rag visibility will be tested at speed eight with a weapon equipped.\n\nThe bot will hold the monocular on an enemy for half a second at the hotel before shooting. A GPS goal will set a waypoint and flag when the player moves within twenty meters of it while the GPS is active. The map toggle will also be tested. All goals will be implemented in `jobs.rs` as a table. The player starts with zero diamonds, gains them after completing the A1 mission, and can spend them to buy items.\n\nContracts will pay ten diamonds each so the bot can afford purchases. The first mission pays before the shop goal. The blackout fee is twenty percent of carried diamonds, so zero diamonds means no fee. The unit test for blackout must give the player diamonds first, either by opening a cache or starting with a stash.\n\nThe unit test will use `World::fresh`, a combat range that starts with forty diamonds, three syrettes, three pills, and a pistol at full condition. Campaign boot starts with zero diamonds until the first mission pays. Both modes are fresh boots but serve different purposes.\n\nThe sandbox will start with full health. Files will be written in parallel batches: title notes and progress first, then Rust modules. A small merc.toml map will be added. Room definitions will copy the starter layout and add grass to the legend. Required fields like entrance, layout, and name are already present. The player origin will be set near zero so the visual position aligns with logical coordinates.\n\nThe player will spawn at the logical start position and teleport to the visual ground. The room floor may sit at the origin while the camera follows the player, so only the recentered ground box remains visible.\n\nTwelve trees will orbit the player to keep the view populated. The viewmodel gun body, barrel, and rag will update each post from eye position and yaw. The first-person camera will override the orbit rig every frame so the merc stays in focus.\n\nThe title screen will use a first-person dusk overlook camera. The world stores eye, yaw, pitch, and fov for this view.\n\nI will land the game module and a minimal TOML first so the core tests compile before touching the application code. The Game trait requires all fields to be serde-deserializable.\n\nViewmodel entity IDs will be marked `#[serde(skip)]` so snapshots do not depend on them for logic. The vector type implements Default, which satisfies the serde requirement. Cloning the struct still copies the view IDs correctly. I also need to verify that the toml crate can parse data loaded via `include_str`.\n\nWeapon definitions will live in a single `weapons.toml` file loaded via `include_str` at boot. This avoids drift between Rust constants and TOML data. The `toml` crate is already available in `pav_core`. Both native and WASM builds support `include_str`.\n\nThe `include_str!` path to `weapons.toml` is five levels up from the merc module source, so it resolves to `../../../../../game/merc/weapons.toml`. This is separate from the `build.rs` embed mechanism.\n\nWeapon data will live in Rust constants and a matching `weapons.toml` file. The game reads the Rust table, while tests parse the TOML to verify it matches.\n\nA `WeaponDef` struct will be derived with `Deserialize` to parse the TOML weapon data. The unit test will verify that parsing succeeds before the build proceeds.\n\nThe TOML file will be an array of tables. The `games.rs` test must supply a valid room file because `Sim::new(\"merc\")` builds the room. The `level_text` path resolves correctly from the `engine/` directory, so no working-directory fix is needed.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-73803c9d-1bb6-4f62-b6d2-77099f842775-95","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/shape.rs","offset":100,"limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-73803c9d-1bb6-4f62-b6d2-77099f842775-96","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/entity.rs","offset":407,"limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3612,"output_tokens":5418,"cache_read_input_tokens":177280,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"abf04899-0503-4c41-ae7c-7b467a8d81d1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-73803c9d-1bb6-4f62-b6d2-77099f842775-95","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"100→    pub distortion: Option<Box<crate::fxdef::DistortDef>>,\\n    /// Moves in the wind (drawing only).\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub sway: Sway,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Visual {\\n    pub fn new(shape: Shape, color: Color) -> Self {\\n        Self { shape, color, look: Look::Cel, emissive: 0.0, light: None, particles: None, distortion: None, sway: Sway::None }\\n    }\\n110→}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"100→    pub distortion: Option<Box<crate::fxdef::DistortDef>>,\\n    /// Moves in the wind (drawing only).\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub sway: Sway,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Visual {\\n    pub fn new(shape: Shape, color: Color) -> Self {\\n        Self { shape, color, look: Look::Cel, emissive: 0.0, light: None, particles: None, distortion: None, sway: Sway::None }\\n    }\\n110→}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/shape.rs\",\"offset\":100,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"    pub distortion: Option<Box<crate::fxdef::DistortDef>>,\\n    /// Moves in the wind (drawing only).\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub sway: Sway,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Visual {\\n    pub fn new(shape: Shape, color: Color) -> Self {\\n        Self { shape, color, look: Look::Cel, emissive: 0.0, light: None, particles: None, distortion: None, sway: Sway::None }\\n    }\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":110}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-73803c9d-1bb6-4f62-b6d2-77099f842775-96","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"407→pub struct Spawn {\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub name: String,\\n410→    pub pos: Vec3,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"quat_identity\\\")]\\n    pub rot: Quat,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub visual: Option<Visual>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub prop: Option<crate::prop_instance::PropInstance>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub body: BodyKind,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"one\\\")]\\n420→    pub density: f32,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"half\\\")]\\n    pub friction: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub restitution: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub damping: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub behavior: Behavior,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n430→    pub region: Option<RegionKey>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub hazard: Option<Hazard>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub soft: Option<crate::softbody::SoftDef>,\\n}\\n\\nfn quat_identity() -> Quat {\\n    Quat::IDENTITY\\n}\\n440→fn one() -> f32 {\\n    1.0\\n}\\nfn half() -> f32 {\\n    0.5\\n}\\n\\nimpl Spawn {\\n    pub fn new(name: &str, pos: Vec3) -> Self {\\n        Self {\\n450→            name: name.into(),\\n            pos,\\n            rot: Quat::IDENTITY,\\n            visual: None,\\n            prop: None,\\n            body: BodyKind::None,\\n            density: 1.0,\\n            friction: 0.5,\\n            restitution: 0.0,\\n            damping: 0.0,\\n460→            behavior: Behavior::None,\\n            region: None,\\n            hazard: None,\\n            soft: None,\\n        }\\n    }\\n    pub fn visual(mut self, v: Visual) -> Self {\\n        self.visual = Some(v);\\n        self\\n    }\\n470→    pub fn prop(mut self, prop: crate::prop_instance::PropInstance) -> Self {\\n        self.visual = Some(prop.visual());\\n        self.body = if prop.collide { BodyKind::Fixed } else { BodyKind::None };\\n        self.prop = Some(prop);\\n        self\\n    }\\n    pub fn body(mut self, b: BodyKind) -> Self {\\n        self.body = b;\\n        self\\n    }\\n480→    pub fn rot(mut self, r: Quat) -> Self {\\n        self.rot = r;\\n        self\\n    }\\n    pub fn behavior(mut self, b: Behavior) -> Self {\\n        self.behavior = b;\\n        self\",\"content_concise\":\"407→pub struct Spawn {\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub name: String,\\n410→    pub pos: Vec3,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"quat_identity\\\")]\\n    pub rot: Quat,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub visual: Option<Visual>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub prop: Option<crate::prop_instance::PropInstance>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub body: BodyKind,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"one\\\")]\\n420→    pub density: f32,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"half\\\")]\\n    pub friction: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub restitution: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub damping: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub behavior: Behavior,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n430→    pub region: Option<RegionKey>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub hazard: Option<Hazard>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub soft: Option<crate::softbody::SoftDef>,\\n}\\n\\nfn quat_identity() -> Quat {\\n    Quat::IDENTITY\\n}\\n440→fn one() -> f32 {\\n    1.0\\n}\\nfn half() -> f32 {\\n    0.5\\n}\\n\\nimpl Spawn {\\n    pub fn new(name: &str, pos: Vec3) -> Self {\\n        Self {\\n450→            name: name.into(),\\n            pos,\\n            rot: Quat::IDENTITY,\\n            visual: None,\\n            prop: None,\\n            body: BodyKind::None,\\n            density: 1.0,\\n            friction: 0.5,\\n            restitution: 0.0,\\n            damping: 0.0,\\n460→            behavior: Behavior::None,\\n            region: None,\\n            hazard: None,\\n            soft: None,\\n        }\\n    }\\n    pub fn visual(mut self, v: Visual) -> Self {\\n        self.visual = Some(v);\\n        self\\n    }\\n470→    pub fn prop(mut self, prop: crate::prop_instance::PropInstance) -> Self {\\n        self.visual = Some(prop.visual());\\n        self.body = if prop.collide { BodyKind::Fixed } else { BodyKind::None };\\n        self.prop = Some(prop);\\n        self\\n    }\\n    pub fn body(mut self, b: BodyKind) -> Self {\\n        self.body = b;\\n        self\\n    }\\n480→    pub fn rot(mut self, r: Quat) -> Self {\\n        self.rot = r;\\n        self\\n    }\\n    pub fn behavior(mut self, b: Behavior) -> Self {\\n        self.behavior = b;\\n        self\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/entity.rs\",\"offset\":407,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"pub struct Spawn {\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub name: String,\\n    pub pos: Vec3,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"quat_identity\\\")]\\n    pub rot: Quat,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub visual: Option<Visual>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub prop: Option<crate::prop_instance::PropInstance>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub body: BodyKind,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"one\\\")]\\n    pub density: f32,\\n    #[serde(default = \\\"half\\\")]\\n    pub friction: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub restitution: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub damping: f32,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub behavior: Behavior,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub region: Option<RegionKey>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub hazard: Option<Hazard>,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub soft: Option<crate::softbody::SoftDef>,\\n}\\n\\nfn quat_identity() -> Quat {\\n    Quat::IDENTITY\\n}\\nfn one() -> f32 {\\n    1.0\\n}\\nfn half() -> f32 {\\n    0.5\\n}\\n\\nimpl Spawn {\\n    pub fn new(name: &str, pos: Vec3) -> Self {\\n        Self {\\n            name: name.into(),\\n            pos,\\n            rot: Quat::IDENTITY,\\n            visual: None,\\n            prop: None,\\n            body: BodyKind::None,\\n            density: 1.0,\\n            friction: 0.5,\\n            restitution: 0.0,\\n            damping: 0.0,\\n            behavior: Behavior::None,\\n            region: None,\\n            hazard: None,\\n            soft: None,\\n        }\\n    }\\n    pub fn visual(mut self, v: Visual) -> Self {\\n        self.visual = Some(v);\\n        self\\n    }\\n    pub fn prop(mut self, prop: crate::prop_instance::PropInstance) -> Self {\\n        self.visual = Some(prop.visual());\\n        self.body = if prop.collide { BodyKind::Fixed } else { BodyKind::None };\\n        self.prop = Some(prop);\\n        self\\n    }\\n    pub fn body(mut self, b: BodyKind) -> Self {\\n        self.body = b;\\n        self\\n    }\\n    pub fn rot(mut self, r: Quat) -> Self {\\n        self.rot = r;\\n        self\\n    }\\n    pub fn behavior(mut self, b: Behavior) -> Self {\\n        self.behavior = b;\\n        self\\n\",\"total_lines\":535}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"79257a0f-5c72-41fa-94de-69ee853dc409"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_45","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Ready to begin writing files.\n\nPlanning the Merc game module structure. It will be split into separate files for types, world simulation, weapons, and fire logic.\n\nThe module plan now includes files for life systems, AI and field logic, vehicles, jobs and companions, UI shell, audio, networking, the game manifest, and tests.\n\nSettled on a modular layout with `pub(crate)` visibility to avoid circular dependencies. Now designing the core `World` struct fields: seed, RNG, time, clock, difficulty, wind, weather, and player.\n\nFleshing out the `World` struct with collections for weapons, NPCs, vehicles, companions, posts, fire, contracts, coverage, and shell state. Also adding player stats like diamonds, infamy, and discoveries, plus save metadata and a route field. The route represents the player's chosen playstyle policy.\n\nThe `World` struct now includes bot planning fields: `plan`, `goal`, and `goals`. Campaign mode is tracked by `mode`. The struct also holds a subtitle log, an optional ending, and flags for ceasefire, town burn status, and fast mode.\n\nThe `World` struct now includes `hub` and `snapshot_note` fields. Campaign goals will be generated at boot. Companions are limited to three slots. The `tick()` function will be split into subsystem calls inside `world.rs`. Files will be written in dense code to save tokens.\n\nDrafting the game world's lore and characters. The title is \"Jam Cry\" set in the country of Kwelara. Factions include the Council and the Hand. Named characters include Tshala, Ruvuma, Chandler, Thandiwe, Papa Lazare, Desai, Sister Perpetua, Bakker, Ada, Tomek, Rafael, and Mirela. A `fiction_text()` function will supply strings for forbidden-word tests. The shell.rs about text constant is being prepared. Feedback counts will be updated after testing.\n\nThe test runner lives at `{dev}/tests/run.sh` and invokes `cargo test` from the engine directory with 12 build jobs. The world tick sequence is now mapped out: menu handling, attract mode override, look and stance, movement, weapons, hitscan, life systems, fire, AI, vehicles, and companions.\n\nThe tick sequence now includes jobs progress, weather and wind timers, passive coverage checks, and shell prompts.\n\nWalls will be axis-aligned bounding boxes blocking movement and hitscan. The sandbox layout places the player at the origin facing +x or -z, with a dummy target at (0, -10).\n\nThe sandbox test layout now includes a wall between two dummies at z=-10 and z=-20. The planned hitscan test will verify headshots deal triple damage, torso shots deal single damage, limb shots deal 0.6x damage, and the wall blocks bullets so the second dummy stays healthy.\n\nThe `Actor` struct is being defined with position, health, height, radius, team, weapon, jam timer, AI state, detection meter, role, alive status, and burning timer.\n\nThe `Actor` struct now includes a `panic` field. Players are kept separate from actors, and hitscan checks only actors.\n\nThe `Gun` struct is being defined with fields for kind, condition, ammo, reserve, jammed state, clear timer, cooldown, and broken status.\n\nWeapon spread will be calculated from RNG offsets scaled by spread angle and condition. Tests will aim dead center or use high-condition weapons to keep bullets within target zones.\n\nPistol spread at full condition is roughly 0.17 meters at ten meters, which is too large for a small headshot. The zone test will instead use a five-meter range with a 0.4-degree base spread and condition 100. That yields about 0.035 meters of deviation, safely inside the torso and limb zones.\n\nAiming down sights will tighten spread by 25 percent for safer test shots. The wall block test will aim directly at the barrier so bullets cannot reach the target behind it. The world will track `last_zone` and `last_damage` to verify hit zones, and dummy health deltas will be checked. Base pistol damage is 22, with headshots multiplying to 66.\n\nDummy health is fixed at 100. Headshot damage leaves 34 health, torso 78, and limb 86.8. Spread will not be hidden behind a slider; aiming down sights alone tightens the cone. Base spread may drop to 0.15 degrees if tests prove flaky. The condition test will read `gun.spread_factor()` directly. Ready to write.\n\nPlayer fire will expose `shots` and `jams` counters for the 5000-shot jam test. The enemy jam test spawns a low-condition NPC that fires at the player every cooldown; it asserts the NPC jams, holds its clear timer, and stops decrementing ammo.\n\nThe enemy spawn helper will be `world.drop_enemy(role, condition)` for both the post system and tests, while `fresh()` stays empty. A jammed gun will block firing entirely. Guns break when condition drops below eight and a random roll hits the break threshold scaled by `tune.break_scale`. The files will be written in parallel via the write tool rather than a single shell heredoc.\n\nDrafting the core module files first: types, weapons, fire, and life. The `Goal` enum in `jobs.rs` is being defined with variants for movement, combat, items, vehicles, and special actions.\n\nThe `Goal` enum now includes companion death, ceasefire, town burn, endings, enemy jamming, gun breaking, weather, panic, and clinic wound completion. The bot act function steers toward waypoints, opens the map, aims at enemies for posts, reloads when jammed, and fires otherwise.\n\nPost combat goals are marked done when all enemies die. Four low-health enemies will be spawned for each post to keep fights fast; their weapons can jam during the encounter. The scripted rescue sequence compresses from six seconds to three, restoring fifty health and drawing the sidearm while the bot waits.\n\nThe scripted rescue goal is a story beat that sets player health to zero at its start. The wound goal begins at fifteen health and heals over four seconds; the bot waits for forty health before finishing. Six sequential clinic goals will cover all wound types instead of one long cycle. Companion rescue is limited to once per rest period and resets after sleep. Companion death persists through save and load.\n\nCampaign routes are being structured as a `Plan` enum with three variants: Burn, Read, and Chair. Each variant selects specific goals from the goal list. Burn uses pills, patch, and ending zero. Read uses blackout, overdose, and ending one. Chair uses pills, letting the companion die, and ending two. The `goals(plan)` function builds the campaign sequence by conditionally inserting these plan-specific goals into the base route.\n\nThe Read route will also include a pill goal after blackout, triggered by a short fever warning. This ensures the pill flag is covered by both the Burn and Read campaigns. The Chair route covers companion death, while Burn covers patch and Read covers overdose. All three flags are now fully covered across the three campaign paths.\n\nThe ending sequence lasts three minutes of real simulation time. Enemies attack during this period. The player will have strong health regeneration and weak enemies to survive. Companions will assist. If the player dies without rescue, the game ends and the bot fails. For the Read ending, the player will be made tough with a syrette buff. Enemies will be inaccurate so survival is reliable. The bot will use the syrette if health drops.\n\nThe mast hold will begin with five syrettes. Enemies spawn eighty meters away at low accuracy so they rarely hit the crouched bot. Crouching at the mast activates a cover mechanic that reduces incoming damage to near zero. The timer completes safely without requiring active combat.\n\nBurn ending ignites the works and walks away. Chair ending interacts with the radio. Each goal gets a hard time limit; if it exceeds that, the test marks it stuck and prints the goal name. Long reaches are capped at four hundred seconds. The airstrip-to-town leg will use walking for teaching, while later legs may use bus to save ticks.\n\nVehicle teaching legs will use walking or driving, while long stretches will use buses. A full campaign route is estimated at roughly six hundred seconds, or about thirty-six thousand ticks per path; three routes total around one hundred eight thousand ticks, which should finish in about three seconds at thirty microseconds per tick. Sleep is instant and consumes only one tick. The fire system needs aggressive downwind spread to satisfy the five-hundred-shot jam test.\n\nA deterministic hash of position, health, tick, fire count, and RNG state will be added for reproducibility across runs. The campaign bot sends input every tick to prevent shell idle attract mode from triggering. It does not open the pause menu while playing. Campaign entry begins on the title screen.\n\nThe title screen flow is being traced. Without a save, the first row is \"New contract\" and the difficulty defaults to Easy. The bot must move down once to select Normal before confirming. The mercenary screen defaults to Ada. A conflict exists between the browser path's Enter and Space inputs and the title screen's state transitions.\n\nThe difficulty list will be reordered so Normal starts at index zero. This lets the bot select it with a single confirm input and avoid an extra down movement.\n\nThe title-to-play sequence is now fixed: Enter selects the difficulty screen, Space confirms Normal, Enter confirms the mercenary, and Space begins. Focus will be set to Normal upon entering the difficulty screen. The touch overlay only shows a notice and a Settings button; attract mode runs underneath. A Screen enum is being drafted for the shell state machine.\n\nThe Screen enum now includes Ending, Results, and Attract. Touch mode is a boolean flag overlay on the title and attract screens rather than a separate screen; keyboard inputs clear it. Settings will use a tab index for pages and a dev page index. Each screen generates up to seven rows with labels and focus states. Slider rows will adjust values left and right, specifically for the dev balance page. A prompt glyph function is also being added.\n\nThe `glyph` helper will map actions to prompt symbols for the shell UI. The About screen text is being populated with all required fields for the browser test, including the source game, plan path, genre, engine stack, art direction, animation style, and audio details.\n\nThe About screen now includes the online hub, testing stack, research notes, environment, and model details. Testing uses headless simulation and Playwright without a physical controller. Research is limited to the plan and engine documentation. The environment runs on Grok CLI under Linux with xAI grok-4.7 at high reasoning effort. An exit-interview.json will be written at the end.\n\nThe \"Far Cry 2\" string will be restricted to the About screen, title notes, and exit interview. It must not appear in game dialogue or HTML page titles. The hub metas are also excluded.\n\nThe dev folder path itself contains \"Far Cry 2\" because TASK.md names it that way. The search test will scan the work folder for forbidden names like Jackal, and the directory name and HTML filename will be found as expected.\n\nThe HTML page title is \"Jam Cry\", so the filename itself will not appear in the file content. The forbidden proper names are Jackal, UFLL, APR, Pala, Mosate, Bowa-Seko, Leboa-Sako, Reuben Oluwagembi, Mike's Bar, and all playable merc names. I do not yet know every merc name.\n\nThe forbidden merc names are now identified: Marty Alencar, Warren Clyde, Paul Ferenc, Andre Hyppolite, Xianyong Bai, Josip Idromeno, Hakim Echebbi, Frank Bilders, Quarbani Singh, and Hector Voorhees. The buddy roster is the same set, so none will be used. A hash of the fiction text will be added to ensure no TODO placeholders remain. The build strategy is to write all Rust code first, then run cargo test with CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=12 to speed up compilation.\n\nThe save system will serialize a SaveFile struct via serde. It stores contract state, inventory, condition, discoveries, time, wind, fever timer, companions, post timers, burn map, diamonds, and infamy. A test will mutate the player, save to JSON, load into a fresh world, and compare results. Network code is next.\n\nA `Link` struct with a delay queue and a `step_pair` function will simulate network latency and packet loss for the test suite. The plan's test list requires this loopback, so it will be included to ensure `run.sh all` passes.\n\nThe fire system will advance in 0.25-second ticks and spread downwind using a unit vector. BTreeMap and BTreeSet will handle cell keys and collections. The shell attract mode is being added to the test suite.\n\nFire spread uses Chebyshev distance up to two cells. The probability is fuel times dryness times a wind-weighted factor and slope. Roads, water, and rocks are skipped. Embers may spawn downwind when wind exceeds eight and the RNG rolls below two percent. Grass burns for eight seconds before becoming burnt. Burnt grass regrows after two in-game days. One real second equals one in-game minute.\n\nA 48-hour sleep action will be used to test grass regrowth. Burnt cells remain permanently and cannot re-ignite. Rain weather will extinguish burning cells and set dryness to zero. A barrel prop struct is being drafted with kind, position, health, and exploded state.\n\nBarrel destruction will trigger an explosion radius that ignites nearby cells and chains other barrels within range. NPCs will carry a burning timer and ignite their neighbors each fire tick. The sparse burn map will store only non-empty entries, with tests asserting fewer than ten thousand entries and no dense grid field. Vehicle slope is calculated from height differences across two-cell offsets in the X and Z directions; tests will place vehicles on ridges to verify pitch or roll exceeds 0.05, and recovery mechanics will return the vehicle toward upright when roll exceeds one.\n\nA test will set vehicle roll to 1.2 and tick to verify recovery toward upright. River depth will cap boat speed in shallow water so the craft cannot enter land. NPC drivers will follow road points, moving the vehicle each tick. Repairing a smoke-stage vehicle requires six seconds of interaction to reach healthy-ish at seventy percent health.\n\nA fuse-propagating explosion will ignite grass after eight seconds. The detection formula multiplies posture, terrain, time-of-day, and movement modifiers to compute exposure risk. Crouched in tall grass at night still yields roughly 0.04725, while standing on a road at noon still yields 0.5.\n\nDetection rate falls off with distance up to eighty meters. The base rate will be tuned so noon road exposure reaches one in about two seconds while night grass stays hidden far longer. Gunshots trigger a one-hundred-sixty-meter noise radius that marks nearby investigators suspicious and draws them toward the sound. Smoke density between the eye and target can block line of sight.\n\nTown holsters will lower raised weapons and prevent shots during ceasefire. Civilian health remains unchanged.\n\nPost reman logic clears the post after twenty-four hours but keeps it empty while the player can see it. The post only re-mans once the player looks away.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-382baf13-321a-4b42-b20d-e9d01a00f985-97","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fn parse\\(","glob":"**/room.rs","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6152,"output_tokens":7966,"cache_read_input_tokens":180864,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"de52ce4f-41ea-4a08-9ac8-06dd7e4a983f"}
