& self.brains.is_empty() && self.wave > 0 {\"},{\"line_number\":399,\"content\":\"            d.title(150.0, 16.0, \\\"#ffd34d\\\", &format!(\\\"Wave {} cleared!\\\", self.wave));\"},{\"line_number\":405,\"content\":\"            \\\"wave\\\": self.wave, \\\"score\\\": self.score, \\\"kills\\\": self.kills, \\\"over\\\": self.over,\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_audio/src/lib.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":2,\"content\":\"//! and a filter. The mixer runs in the audio callback (cpal) or offline (to .wav). The game\"},{\"line_number\":108,\"content\":\"        let raw = match p.wave {\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_view/src/water.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":132,\"content\":\"            self.wave_step(s, h);\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_tools/src/tools.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":259,\"content\":\"        help: \\\"Run N ticks (optionally driving the player) and render the game's sounds to a .wav file.\\\",\"},{\"line_number\":266,\"content\":\"            arg(\\\"out\\\", \\\"string\\\", \\\"output .wav path\\\"),\"},{\"line_number\":1735,\"content\":\"    let path = PathBuf::from(get_str(a, \\\"out\\\").map(String::from).unwrap_or(format!(\\\"out/audio-{}.wav\\\", s.sim.state.tick)));\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-8a6ddce5-5c34-4ade-8ab8-8d1f9d06b583-101","content":"{\"type\":\"Bash\",\"output\":[102,105,110,100,58,32,226,128,152,101,110,103,105,110,101,226,128,153,58,32,78,111,32,115,117,99,104,32,102,105,108,101,32,111,114,32,100,105,114,101,99,116,111,114,121,10],\"output_for_prompt\":\"exit: 0\\nfind: ‘engine’: No such file or directory\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"find engine -type f \\\\( -name '*.wav' -o -name '*.mp3' -o -name '*.ogg' \\\\) | head -50\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Find any audio files in the engine\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91%2Fwork/01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558/terminal/call-8a6ddce5-5c34-4ade-8ab8-8d1f9d06b583-101.log\",\"total_bytes\":46}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"1723b40e-61e6-4a81-8e8e-b0d685a907a3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_49","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The current working directory may have changed. The function reported the workspace path.\n\nThe shell changed to the dev folder after the clone command. The find engine failed there. The working directory may not be the workspace.\n\nThe working directory should persist between calls. The find command returned exit code 0 despite the missing file error. This is unexpected.\n\nThe `find` command itself exists. The error indicates the target path is missing, not that the tool is absent.\n\nThe working directory is not the dev folder. Absolute paths will be used instead.\n\nNo wav files are expected in the engine.\n\nI am now writing code. I will check the rustfmt edition first.\n\nThe merc module will be structured as a single large file, logic.rs, containing all simulation logic to avoid visibility issues.\n\nI will keep logic.rs compact under 2000 lines. The Merc struct will derive Clone, Serialize, and Deserialize. World must be public for tests. I will also export Act, fresh, and campaign.\n\nI will create a custom test harness in `tests/merc.rs` and disable the default Cargo harness. I must avoid setting `autotests = false` in `Cargo.toml`, since that would require listing every existing test and is impractical.\n\nI will keep the default Cargo harness and use `--quiet` in the test runner. The shell script will filter output to show only failures and a final one-line summary count.\n\nAll checks will live in `tests/merc.rs` as grouped `#[test]` functions. The runner script will transform Cargo's standard output into a compact `PASS N FAIL M` summary line.\n\nI will split the custom tests into several named `#[test]` functions covering world, playthrough, UI audio, and networking. I may optimize the playthrough test after measuring its speed. I will add title notes, PROGRESS tracking, and feedback notes to the project. HTML integration is deferred until simulation tests pass. I am also checking a potential bug in the fire-three-times test.\n\nThe fire-three-times test needs an upwind reach greater than zero. Otherwise the downwind-to-upwind ratio becomes infinite and the assertion passes trivially.\n\nI will cap the fire probability at 0.95 and use a moderate spread rate to prevent deterministic saturation in both directions. I will refine the model after the first run instead of overthinking it. I have drafted the `ignite_cell` API: it checks fuel, skips burnt cells that cannot regrow, inserts a burning cell with burn time and class, and updates coverage.\n\nWorld coordinates map to cells by dividing by two and flooring. The map spans -1024 to 1024, yielding cell indices from -512 to 512.\n\nBiome fuel values are set: grass is 1.0, bushes are 1.5, trees are 3.0, and roads, water, and rocks have zero fuel.\n\nThe fire test will target a grass cell away from roads and the town center.\n\nThe fire test origin at (0, 0) is only about 9.7 meters from the river polyline. That distance falls inside the 20-meter river width, so the cell is water and cannot burn. The ignition point must be moved to a grass cell away from the river.\n\nThe fire test will use (200, -200) for ignition and (-520, -520) for the depot. Both cells are grassy and away from roads and the town center. Roads will be defined as 8-meter-wide segments, the river as 24 meters, and the town radius as 80 meters.\n\nTown grass can burn during town burns, and ceasefires do not stop fire. Rock kopjes will be modeled as zero-fuel circles. I am now writing the code, starting with notes/title.md containing all fifty titles and the RNG output, then proceeding to logic.rs.\n\nI will run clippy and rustfmt to match standard style. The `World` struct must remain public for the integration test crate. The `Rng` field will stay internal. `BTreeMap<(i32,i32), FireCell>` serializes correctly under both JSON and CBOR.\n\nMerc clones World and the fire map for history and snapshots, but Game itself is only cloned during those operations. To prevent player physics from fighting the simulation, pre will return empty input for the player entity, and post will set the player position from the world state.\n\nThe Merc struct will store the pending action in a `#[serde(skip)]` field so it is not serialized. The tick function will use the engine's passed delta time instead of a fixed 1/60 value.\n\nA `play_bot` helper will run the campaign for up to two million ticks, breaking early if the world finishes or becomes stuck. Stuck detection triggers after ninety seconds of unchanged progress, except for wait goals which allow longer idle periods.\n\nThe reach goal will trigger when the bot is within eight meters of the target. The fight goal spawns enemies in a fifteen-meter radius and gives the player one hundred health. Enemies deal four damage per shot, so the player dies in about six seconds if all shots land. The bot will snap aim to face each enemy immediately and fire on the next tick to kill them quickly.\n\nEnemy health is 36 and damage is 26, so four enemies can kill the player in about one second. The rifle cooldown will be 0.1 seconds to match 600 RPM. Enemies will miss often to keep the player safe.\n\nThe jam goal forces the first shot to jam on begin, then requires reloading until the clear timer finishes. The formula still applies to later shots, but a unit test will verify it separately without the forced jam flag.\n\nEnemy cooldown is 0.05 seconds for 20 shots per second. The jam goal will use three seeds to verify the enemy jamming contract. A break gun with 4 condition and a 3% break chance is also planned.\n\nThe break gun goal will fire up to four hundred shots. The chance of surviving all of them is vanishingly small, so the goal almost certainly completes.\n\n`bot_act` will remain pure and not consume the RNG. Only `tick` will advance the random state.\n\nCampaign runs with identical seeds must produce identical outcomes. The verification harness will test this by running the same seeds twice and comparing the final world state.\n\nI will add a `coverage` field as a `BTreeSet<String>` to track which campaign flags were hit. The test will assert that the final coverage set contains every expected flag. I am now writing the code.\n\nI will tag each goal with an optional contract identifier so the campaign can set the corresponding flag when that goal completes. A `campaign_steps` function will return a linear sequence of steps, each pairing a goal with its optional contract tag. The first steps in this sequence cover the map setup, waypoint placement, GPS follow, and reaching the town gate.\n\nThe campaign sequence now includes the fever branch, which resolves to either pill or blackout. After contract A0 completes, the bot proceeds through GP1, a fight post, jam clearing, rescue, gunshot wound, rifle loot, safe house claim, and syrette use. Contract A1 pays 12 diamonds before the bot buys a weapon, then uses the locker and sleeps. The next steps are reaching the depot area or driving the technical.\n\nThe depot sequence now includes ragging, monocular tagging, and multiple ignitions. Contract B1 completes there. Night contracts B2 and B3 follow with sleep to midnight, boat travel, stealth kills, dart kills, vehicle repair, rain fire suppression, and a better-way choice.\n\nThe campaign sequence now continues through contract B4, reaching the hotel, a sniper shot that ends the ceasefire, and contract C1. It then proceeds to fight a convoy with rocket man for contract C2, burn the town for contract C3, reach the cement area for contract D1, and finish with the ending and contract D2. Side goals include early animal encounters near the airstrip, chain barrel ignitions at the depot, panic enemies with fire, an enemy jam during the GP1 fight, a break gun test, cache tracker use, diamond spending, tape playback at the hotel, and bus travel instead of a full drive.\n\nPost reman will occur after contract B1 by sleeping at a distant lodge and skipping twenty-four hours so the post remans. Additional ignitions include a cigarette at the bar, lightning during the rain goal, tracer fire on grass, burning an enemy person, vehicle explosion, and frag. The bot will also visit the clinic to receive all six wound types after rescue.\n\nThe bot starts with three syrettes so it can use one for the syrette goal and still have one for patching. Contract A1 pays twelve diamonds, which covers the eight diamond weapon licence cost. The cache tracker is a briefcase on the road that beeps; the bot walks to it to set the flag and gain diamonds. Post cleared will be set when a fight post completes. NPC spawns will happen in begin_goal tagged by post_id.\n\nThe next step is writing the code file. Unit tests will use a sandbox world at (200, -200) with a pistol, rifle, 40 diamonds, 3 syrettes, and 3 pills, and no story force flags. Default weapon wear scale will be 1 so jam formula tests use real values; tests can set wear to 0. The jam formula checks condition at the moment of the shot and applies wear only after the roll.\n\nThe `discharge` method now implements the full firing logic. It checks that the gun is not jammed, broken, empty, or on cooldown before deducting ammo and setting the reload timer. It then computes the jam chance from the current condition, scales it by the world tune, and rolls the random number. If the roll succeeds or a forced jam flag is set, the gun jams and returns false; otherwise the code proceeds to the break check.\n\nThe break check now uses a three percent chance when condition falls below the threshold. The jam rate test requires five thousand trigger pulls, so each attempt must roll independently. If a shot jams, the test should count that roll and either clear the weapon or unjam it without playing the clear animation.\n\nThe jam rate test will run 5000 ticks with the gun at condition 35, ammo 12, and no cooldown or jam. Each tick rolls the jam chance once. A jammed gun skips the roll on the next tick. The test counts every roll that actually triggers a jam.\n\nThe jam rate test will reset the gun's jammed state between each of the five thousand independent rolls. This avoids counting the same roll twice and keeps the probability measurement valid. A separate test will verify that the clear animation blocks firing. The break test will not reset the gun state.\n\nThe break gun test fires until the weapon breaks. Jams are cleared via reload between shots, so the effective break probability is roughly two percent per shot at low condition. The author is checking whether the specification requires the break roll to be independent even when a jam occurs.\n\nThe break roll will only occur when a round actually fires, not during a jammed shot. The clear test will use natural jamming at low condition instead of a forced jam flag.\n\nI will track `shots_fired` only when a round actually leaves the barrel. The implementation will live in a single `world.rs` file, with `mod.rs`, `Cargo.toml`, and tests added separately. I will set `history_seconds = 0` in the build function and add player teleportation for visual sync. I am now writing the `build` function carefully.\n\nThe `build` function parses the room definition and spawns a standalone Merc scene. It sets `history_seconds` to zero and initializes the world from the simulation seed. The author notes that `World::title` starts at the title screen rather than in the fight, and that `World::fresh` does not require a Sim.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1ad08e1b-5e91-4b16-9d3f-30a92644dffa-102","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_tools/src/tools.rs","offset":1355,"limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1ad08e1b-5e91-4b16-9d3f-30a92644dffa-103","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_tools/src/tools.rs","offset":1675,"limit":55}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3136,"output_tokens":6992,"cache_read_input_tokens":200320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"2e336495-6d2e-4db3-8d6f-c501724e56d3"}
