{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_58","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have a solid picture. Time to write code.\n\nThe merc game will be split into seven modules: mod.rs, types.rs, world.rs, shell.rs, audio.rs, net.rs, and a TOML config. Tests will live in tests/merc.rs. I am designing a compact public API around World and Shell for testing.\n\nThe World API will expose `fresh` and `campaign` constructors, a `tick` loop, bot input, status serialization, and save/load methods. Internal unit helpers like `try_fire`, `jam_chance`, and `roll_jam` will be public so tests can exercise them directly.\n\nA `jam_probability` helper will let tests reuse the same jam formula that `tick` uses. `Gun::attempt_shot` will let tests simulate individual shots and count jams directly.\n\nI will test fire behavior by running `World::fresh(1)` for 60 seconds, setting wind and igniting a grass line, then verifying the fire spreads. The `fresh` world has no campaign goals, so this is safe. I am now sketching the detection logic.\n\nI will expose `exposure` and `detection_rate` for the AI update. Tests can call the real detection function or measure time-to-detect by ticking.\n\nVehicles will be testable too. The player enters a technical, sets throttle, and ticks to verify the vehicle climbs a ridge slope.\n\nThe vehicle climb test will use the real ridge near GP5 instead of a synthetic slope. The terrain will have a hotel-side ramp rising to 40 meters, flattened roads, and a river polyline where boats stop in shallow water. I am now designing the campaign goal machine so all three routes complete and set every flag.\n\nCampaign goals will be a `Goal` enum backed by a vector of `Step` structs. Each step has a kind, a done condition checked after systems update, and an on_enter setup block. The bot reads the current step to produce input. Routes will share a common prefix before branching. Real movement speeds would make the full playthrough too long, so the simulation needs faster scaling.\n\nA 100k tick budget should keep wall time under one second. The three routes total roughly 75,000 ticks at 20 microseconds each, or about 1.5 seconds.\n\nTo prevent infinite loops if the bot stalls, a watchdog will fail the test when a goal stays unchanged for too long of simulated time. The reach radius will be generous at 12 meters, and the bot will snap its yaw quickly so it faces targets in about six ticks.\n\nSprint is FOCUS for 12 seconds, then walk. Buses are allowed for long legs to save ticks. The campaign will start at the title screen and proceed through acts: confirm New contract, select Normal difficulty, choose Ada, begin the prologue, and arrive at the airstrip.\n\nThe campaign will include map and waypoint acts that set flags. The first leg to town is 450 meters on foot, which at sprint speed takes roughly 90 seconds. I am considering a 30 Hz tick rate to keep tests fast.\n\nThe fight post will spawn four enemies close together. The bot will aim at heads and use ADS to score headshots, killing them quickly. Enemies will be modeled as simple Npc structs with position, health, kind, AI state, gun, and alive flag. Hitscan will trace from the player eye along yaw and pitch, applying damage based on zone and spread cone.\n\nI am defining exact hitbox coordinates for the sandbox test. The dummy will sit 10 meters ahead at yaw zero. Head height is 1.55–1.85 meters with a radius of 0.18. Torso height is 0.9–1.55 meters with a radius of 0.28. Limbs are 0.2–0.9 meters high with a radius of 0.2. A ray from the eye along forward direction will intersect the closest hitbox first. Walls will block the ray if they lie between the eye and the target. Pistol damage is set to 22.\n\nADS spread is 0.25. At full condition and close range, base spread is about 0.5 degrees. At ten meters that is nine centimeters. A deterministic zone test will use ADS with full condition and aim at the center. Spread becomes roughly 0.002 radians. The head radius is 0.18 meters. That is 0.018 radians. The shot will hit the zone. A jammed gun will not fire the ray. Guns will be stored in a Vec in the inventory.\n\nShell state will live inside World so one tick updates both the game and the UI. A `menu_step` helper will let tests drive menus directly without a full world tick.\n\nShell screens will be an enum with a focus index. Rows are computed at runtime, capped at seven items.\n\n`MenuInput` will expose directional buttons, confirm and back, pointer coordinates, click, and device type. Hovering a pointer over a row moves focus there; clicking confirms.\n\nGlyphs will be drawn using device-specific glyphs plus an Xbox flag. The attract sequence accumulates idle time on the title and shell screens; after ten seconds it plays five full loops of ten segments each, and any input exits early. Tests can tick the attract loop directly with a large delta, but the segment index must still integrate correctly.\n\nShell balance sliders will be a named `Tune` struct. Each page shows seven values, left and right adjust them, and reset restores defaults. Tests will change `spread_rate` and verify the change reaches `world.tune.fire_spread`, then confirm reset reverts it.\n\nAudio rendering will expose a `render_music` function that returns a float vector for gain, duration, and sample rate. The RMS ratio is fixed at 0.10.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c814b456-454f-454a-862a-b47aad1bcf39-31","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_audio/src/lib.rs","offset":1,"limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c814b456-454f-454a-862a-b47aad1bcf39-32","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_audio/src/lib.rs","offset":500,"limit":50}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c814b456-454f-454a-862a-b47aad1bcf39-33","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"pub use|mod ","glob":"**/pav_audio/src/lib.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c814b456-454f-454a-862a-b47aad1bcf39-34","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1117,"output_tokens":3180,"cache_read_input_tokens":84096,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"71f48815-223a-4000-a8d1-204f763043d8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-c814b456-454f-454a-862a-b47aad1bcf39-31","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→//! Synthesized sound: every sound is built from oscillators, noise, envelopes, pitch sweeps\\n//! and a filter. The mixer runs in the audio callback (cpal) or offline (to .wav). The game\\n//! runs fine without a sound device.\\n\\nuse std::sync::mpsc::{Receiver, Sender, channel};\\n\\nuse glam::Vec3;\\nuse pav_core::SimEvent;\\n\\n10→#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq)]\\npub enum Wave {\\n    Sine,\\n    Square,\\n    Saw,\\n    Triangle,\\n    Noise,\\n}\\n\\n/// One synthesized sound.\\n20→#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\\npub struct Patch {\\n    pub wave: Wave,\\n    /// Start / end frequency (Hz) and the glide time between them (s, exponential).\\n    pub freq: f32,\\n    pub freq_end: f32,\\n    pub glide: f32,\\n    pub attack: f32,\\n    pub decay: f32,\\n    pub sustain: f32,\\n30→    pub hold: f32,\\n    pub release: f32,\\n    pub volume: f32,\\n    /// One-pole low-pass cutoff (Hz) at start and end of the sound.\\n    pub cutoff: f32,\\n    pub cutoff_end: f32,\\n    /// Square-wave duty cycle.\\n    pub duty: f32,\\n}\\n\\n40→impl Patch {\\n    pub const fn new(wave: Wave, freq: f32) -> Self {\\n        Self {\\n            wave,\\n            freq,\\n            freq_end: freq,\\n            glide: 0.1,\\n            attack: 0.002,\\n            decay: 0.1,\\n            sustain: 0.0,\\n50→            hold: 0.0,\\n            release: 0.05,\\n            volume: 0.3,\\n            cutoff: 12000.0,\\n            cutoff_end: 12000.0,\\n            duty: 0.5,\\n        }\\n    }\\n    pub fn length(&self) -> f32 {\\n        self.attack + self.decay + self.hold + self.release\\n60→    }\\n}\\n\\nstruct Voice {\\n    p: Patch,\\n    t: f32,\\n    delay: f32,\\n    phase: f32,\\n    lp: f32,\\n    noise: u32,\\n70→    gain_l: f32,\\n    gain_r: f32,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Voice {\\n    fn env(&self) -> f32 {\\n        let p = &self.p;\\n        let t = self.t;\\n        if t < p.attack {\\n            return t / p.attack.max(1e-5);\\n80→        }\",\"content_concise\":\"1→//! Synthesized sound: every sound is built from oscillators, noise, envelopes, pitch sweeps\\n//! and a filter. The mixer runs in the audio callback (cpal) or offline (to .wav). The game\\n//! runs fine without a sound device.\\n\\nuse std::sync::mpsc::{Receiver, Sender, channel};\\n\\nuse glam::Vec3;\\nuse pav_core::SimEvent;\\n\\n10→#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq)]\\npub enum Wave {\\n    Sine,\\n    Square,\\n    Saw,\\n    Triangle,\\n    Noise,\\n}\\n\\n/// One synthesized sound.\\n20→#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\\npub struct Patch {\\n    pub wave: Wave,\\n    /// Start / end frequency (Hz) and the glide time between them (s, exponential).\\n    pub freq: f32,\\n    pub freq_end: f32,\\n    pub glide: f32,\\n    pub attack: f32,\\n    pub decay: f32,\\n    pub sustain: f32,\\n30→    pub hold: f32,\\n    pub release: f32,\\n    pub volume: f32,\\n    /// One-pole low-pass cutoff (Hz) at start and end of the sound.\\n    pub cutoff: f32,\\n    pub cutoff_end: f32,\\n    /// Square-wave duty cycle.\\n    pub duty: f32,\\n}\\n\\n40→impl Patch {\\n    pub const fn new(wave: Wave, freq: f32) -> Self {\\n        Self {\\n            wave,\\n            freq,\\n            freq_end: freq,\\n            glide: 0.1,\\n            attack: 0.002,\\n            decay: 0.1,\\n            sustain: 0.0,\\n50→            hold: 0.0,\\n            release: 0.05,\\n            volume: 0.3,\\n            cutoff: 12000.0,\\n            cutoff_end: 12000.0,\\n            duty: 0.5,\\n        }\\n    }\\n    pub fn length(&self) -> f32 {\\n        self.attack + self.decay + self.hold + self.release\\n60→    }\\n}\\n\\nstruct Voice {\\n    p: Patch,\\n    t: f32,\\n    delay: f32,\\n    phase: f32,\\n    lp: f32,\\n    noise: u32,\\n70→    gain_l: f32,\\n    gain_r: f32,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Voice {\\n    fn env(&self) -> f32 {\\n        let p = &self.p;\\n        let t = self.t;\\n        if t < p.attack {\\n            return t / p.attack.max(1e-5);\\n80→        }\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_audio/src/lib.rs\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"//! Synthesized sound: every sound is built from oscillators, noise, envelopes, pitch sweeps\\n//! and a filter. The mixer runs in the audio callback (cpal) or offline (to .wav). The game\\n//! runs fine without a sound device.\\n\\nuse std::sync::mpsc::{Receiver, Sender, channel};\\n\\nuse glam::Vec3;\\nuse pav_core::SimEvent;\\n\\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq)]\\npub enum Wave {\\n    Sine,\\n    Square,\\n    Saw,\\n    Triangle,\\n    Noise,\\n}\\n\\n/// One synthesized sound.\\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\\npub struct Patch {\\n    pub wave: Wave,\\n    /// Start / end frequency (Hz) and the glide time between them (s, exponential).\\n    pub freq: f32,\\n    pub freq_end: f32,\\n    pub glide: f32,\\n    pub attack: f32,\\n    pub decay: f32,\\n    pub sustain: f32,\\n    pub hold: f32,\\n    pub release: f32,\\n    pub volume: f32,\\n    /// One-pole low-pass cutoff (Hz) at start and end of the sound.\\n    pub cutoff: f32,\\n    pub cutoff_end: f32,\\n    /// Square-wave duty cycle.\\n    pub duty: f32,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Patch {\\n    pub const fn new(wave: Wave, freq: f32) -> Self {\\n        Self {\\n            wave,\\n            freq,\\n            freq_end: freq,\\n            glide: 0.1,\\n            attack: 0.002,\\n            decay: 0.1,\\n            sustain: 0.0,\\n            hold: 0.0,\\n            release: 0.05,\\n            volume: 0.3,\\n            cutoff: 12000.0,\\n            cutoff_end: 12000.0,\\n            duty: 0.5,\\n        }\\n    }\\n    pub fn length(&self) -> f32 {\\n        self.attack + self.decay + self.hold + self.release\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nstruct Voice {\\n    p: Patch,\\n    t: f32,\\n    delay: f32,\\n    phase: f32,\\n    lp: f32,\\n    noise: u32,\\n    gain_l: f32,\\n    gain_r: f32,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Voice {\\n    fn env(&self) -> f32 {\\n        let p = &self.p;\\n        let t = self.t;\\n        if t < p.attack {\\n            return t / p.attack.max(1e-5);\\n        }\\n\",\"total_lines\":684}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-c814b456-454f-454a-862a-b47aad1bcf39-32","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"500→        | SimEvent::Damage { pos }\\n        | SimEvent::Destroyed { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Swing { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Strike { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Slain { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Slam { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Blast { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Spell { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Coin { pos }\\n        | SimEvent::Loot { pos, .. }\\n510→        | SimEvent::Pickup { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Block { pos } => Some(*pos),\\n        _ => None,\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n/// Plays an event's sounds with distance attenuation and panning.\\npub fn play_event(mixer: &mut Mixer, ev: &SimEvent, l: &Listener, sfx: f32) {\\n    let (pan, gain) = match event_pos(ev) {\\n        Some(p) => {\\n520→            let d = p - l.pos;\\n            let dist = d.length();\\n            ((d.dot(l.right) / 12.0).clamp(-0.85, 0.85), 1.0 / (1.0 + dist / 14.0))\\n        }\\n        None => (0.0, 1.0),\\n    };\\n    for (patch, delay) in sounds_for(ev) {\\n        mixer.play(patch, pan, gain * sfx, delay);\\n    }\\n}\\n530→\\n/// Renders timed events offline: (time in seconds, event). Returns interleaved stereo.\\npub fn render_events(events: &[(f32, SimEvent)], duration: f32, sample_rate: f32, l: &Listener) -> Vec<f32> {\\n    let mut mixer = Mixer::new(sample_rate);\\n    let total = (duration * sample_rate) as usize;\\n    let mut out = vec![0.0f32; total * 2];\\n    let block = 256;\\n    let mut i = 0;\\n    let mut ev = events.iter().peekable();\\n    while i < total {\\n540→        let t = i as f32 / sample_rate;\\n        while let Some((et, e)) = ev.peek() {\\n            if *et > t {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            play_event(&mut mixer, e, l, 1.0);\\n            ev.next();\\n        }\\n        let n = block.min(total - i);\\n        mixer.render(&mut out[i * 2..(i + n) * 2]);\",\"content_concise\":\"500→        | SimEvent::Damage { pos }\\n        | SimEvent::Destroyed { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Swing { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Strike { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Slain { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Slam { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Blast { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Spell { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Coin { pos }\\n        | SimEvent::Loot { pos, .. }\\n510→        | SimEvent::Pickup { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Block { pos } => Some(*pos),\\n        _ => None,\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n/// Plays an event's sounds with distance attenuation and panning.\\npub fn play_event(mixer: &mut Mixer, ev: &SimEvent, l: &Listener, sfx: f32) {\\n    let (pan, gain) = match event_pos(ev) {\\n        Some(p) => {\\n520→            let d = p - l.pos;\\n            let dist = d.length();\\n            ((d.dot(l.right) / 12.0).clamp(-0.85, 0.85), 1.0 / (1.0 + dist / 14.0))\\n        }\\n        None => (0.0, 1.0),\\n    };\\n    for (patch, delay) in sounds_for(ev) {\\n        mixer.play(patch, pan, gain * sfx, delay);\\n    }\\n}\\n530→\\n/// Renders timed events offline: (time in seconds, event). Returns interleaved stereo.\\npub fn render_events(events: &[(f32, SimEvent)], duration: f32, sample_rate: f32, l: &Listener) -> Vec<f32> {\\n    let mut mixer = Mixer::new(sample_rate);\\n    let total = (duration * sample_rate) as usize;\\n    let mut out = vec![0.0f32; total * 2];\\n    let block = 256;\\n    let mut i = 0;\\n    let mut ev = events.iter().peekable();\\n    while i < total {\\n540→        let t = i as f32 / sample_rate;\\n        while let Some((et, e)) = ev.peek() {\\n            if *et > t {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            play_event(&mut mixer, e, l, 1.0);\\n            ev.next();\\n        }\\n        let n = block.min(total - i);\\n        mixer.render(&mut out[i * 2..(i + n) * 2]);\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_audio/src/lib.rs\",\"offset\":500,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"        | SimEvent::Damage { pos }\\n        | SimEvent::Destroyed { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Swing { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Strike { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Slain { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Slam { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Blast { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Spell { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Coin { pos }\\n        | SimEvent::Loot { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Pickup { pos, .. }\\n        | SimEvent::Block { pos } => Some(*pos),\\n        _ => None,\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n/// Plays an event's sounds with distance attenuation and panning.\\npub fn play_event(mixer: &mut Mixer, ev: &SimEvent, l: &Listener, sfx: f32) {\\n    let (pan, gain) = match event_pos(ev) {\\n        Some(p) => {\\n            let d = p - l.pos;\\n            let dist = d.length();\\n            ((d.dot(l.right) / 12.0).clamp(-0.85, 0.85), 1.0 / (1.0 + dist / 14.0))\\n        }\\n        None => (0.0, 1.0),\\n    };\\n    for (patch, delay) in sounds_for(ev) {\\n        mixer.play(patch, pan, gain * sfx, delay);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n/// Renders timed events offline: (time in seconds, event). Returns interleaved stereo.\\npub fn render_events(events: &[(f32, SimEvent)], duration: f32, sample_rate: f32, l: &Listener) -> Vec<f32> {\\n    let mut mixer = Mixer::new(sample_rate);\\n    let total = (duration * sample_rate) as usize;\\n    let mut out = vec![0.0f32; total * 2];\\n    let block = 256;\\n    let mut i = 0;\\n    let mut ev = events.iter().peekable();\\n    while i < total {\\n        let t = i as f32 / sample_rate;\\n        while let Some((et, e)) = ev.peek() {\\n            if *et > t {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            play_event(&mut mixer, e, l, 1.0);\\n            ev.next();\\n        }\\n        let n = block.min(total - i);\\n        mixer.render(&mut out[i * 2..(i + n) * 2]);\\n\",\"total_lines\":684}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-c814b456-454f-454a-862a-b47aad1bcf39-33","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,49,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,47,74,97,109,32,67,114,121,45,70,97,114,32,67,114,121,32,50,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,99,114,97,116,101,115,47,112,97,118,95,97,117,100,105,111,47,115,114,99,47,108,105,98,46,114,115,10,54,55,50,58,109,111,100,32,116,101,115,116,115,32,123,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_audio/src/lib.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":672,\"content\":\"mod tests {\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-c814b456-454f-454a-862a-b47aad1bcf39-34","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→[package]\\nname = \\\"pav_core\\\"\\nversion.workspace = true\\nedition.workspace = true\\npublish.workspace = true\\n\\n[dependencies]\\nglam.workspace = true\\n# One-line physics precision switch: change package to \\\"rapier3d-f64\\\".\\n10→rapier = { package = \\\"rapier3d\\\", version = \\\"0.36\\\", features = [\\\"serde-serialize\\\"] }\\nserde.workspace = true\\nserde_json.workspace = true\\ntoml.workspace = true\\nanyhow.workspace = true\\nciborium.workspace = true\\nlog.workspace = true\\n\\n[lints]\\nworkspace = true\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→[package]\\nname = \\\"pav_core\\\"\\nversion.workspace = true\\nedition.workspace = true\\npublish.workspace = true\\n\\n[dependencies]\\nglam.workspace = true\\n# One-line physics precision switch: change package to \\\"rapier3d-f64\\\".\\n10→rapier = { package = \\\"rapier3d\\\", version = \\\"0.36\\\", features = [\\\"serde-serialize\\\"] }\\nserde.workspace = true\\nserde_json.workspace = true\\ntoml.workspace = true\\nanyhow.workspace = true\\nciborium.workspace = true\\nlog.workspace = true\\n\\n[lints]\\nworkspace = true\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"[package]\\nname = \\\"pav_core\\\"\\nversion.workspace = true\\nedition.workspace = true\\npublish.workspace = true\\n\\n[dependencies]\\nglam.workspace = true\\n# One-line physics precision switch: change package to \\\"rapier3d-f64\\\".\\nrapier = { package = \\\"rapier3d\\\", version = \\\"0.36\\\", features = [\\\"serde-serialize\\\"] }\\nserde.workspace = true\\nserde_json.workspace = true\\ntoml.workspace = true\\nanyhow.workspace = true\\nciborium.workspace = true\\nlog.workspace = true\\n\\n[lints]\\nworkspace = true\\n\",\"total_lines\":19}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"d679eaed-47e3-438e-9a73-d946f97d145e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_59","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Music and voice functions will be added to the pav_audio library.\n\nDesigning the fire model with a 2-meter cell size and chebyshev radius 2. Wind direction will be a unit vector from burning cells to neighbors. The exponential wind factor formula appears to double-count wind speed.\n\nWind direction and speed will be stored separately. The exponential factor alone does not create enough downwind-to-upwind ratio. Ignition probability per tick is spread_rate × fuel × dryness × wind_factor × slope × dt. Grass fuel, dryness, and slope are all 1. At 4Hz the timestep is 0.25. Spread rate must be high enough to saturate probabilities at the maximum.\n\nThe fire front is modeled as a one-dimensional random walk of ignitions. With a 0.5 tick probability, the expected reach after 240 ticks is roughly 240 meters. The target is to achieve at least a threefold downwind-to-upwind ratio while keeping both probabilities strictly positive.\n\nUpwind probability of 0.05 yields about 12 cells and 24 meters of reach, giving a tenfold ratio. Because the neighbor radius includes diagonals, the fire spreads in a cone rather than a strict line. Reach will be measured as the maximum distance along the wind axis. Each burning cell will roll ignition against all eight adjacent neighbors, or up to twenty-four within chebyshev radius two. The simulation must allow large fires since the fifty-cell threshold is a required flag.\n\nSpread rate is set to 0.9 and the wind factor constant to 0.12. Downwind ignition probability becomes about 0.59, upwind about 0.086, giving a roughly 6.8-fold ratio. Both values stay below saturation.\n\nCalm conditions make the wind factor equal to one, so fire spreads roughly round. Roads have zero fuel and block fire unless embers land on them; embers only spawn when wind speed exceeds eight. Burnt cells remain dead for forty-eight in-game hours before regrowth begins. Rain causes burning cells to extinguish.\n\nCell state will be either Burning with remaining fuel or Burnt until a future minute. Ignition sets the burning state. Grass burns for eight seconds, so the front can keep spreading even after the starting cell dies. The reach test may produce a very large fire ellipse.\n\nThe chosen spread rate is too fast. A probability of 0.58 per neighbor per tick would grow the fire to roughly 960 meters in sixty seconds, producing millions of cells. The target is a much smaller fire: about thirty downwind cells and seven upwind cells. The current formula ignites every neighbor independently using its own wind factor, which makes lateral spread far too likely.\n\nA spread rate of 0.15 still produces about nine lateral ignitions per cell over sixty seconds, which is too wide. The core issue is that rolling probability on every neighbor fills the map. Three fixes are under consideration: advance fire only once per second instead of every tick, allow each burning cell to ignite at most one neighbor per tick using a weighted selection, or base the rate on front advancement rather than individual neighbors.\n\nThe formula cannot spread safely at high probability. Eight neighbors multiplied by 256 ticks of burn time means each cell can spawn roughly 256 children. To keep the fire as a thin front, the ignition probability must be about 0.004.\n\nDownwind probability will be 0.02, upwind 0.002, and lateral 0.004. Each burning cell will attempt at most one ignition per tick, checking downwind neighbors first. This keeps the exponential formula while preventing combinatorial fire growth.\n\nLimiting each burning cell to one ignition per tick may violate the plan's instruction to roll against every neighbor. A safer approach is to keep the full neighbor formula but cap total cells or use a narrow fuel corridor so the test stays fast and memory-efficient.\n\nThe spread rate is lowered to 0.08 and the wind factor constant to 0.18. Downwind ignition probability becomes about 0.084 per tick, upwind about 0.0047, and lateral about 0.02. Over eight seconds each burning cell has roughly a ninety-four percent chance of igniting its downwind neighbor, a fourteen percent chance of igniting its upwind neighbor, and a forty-eight percent chance of igniting a lateral neighbor. The fire front advances at about one cell every eight seconds, or roughly 0.25 meters per second. In sixty seconds this yields an expected reach of about seven cells downwind, or fourteen meters.\n\nThe fire will stay manageable at roughly 180 cells across seven generations. Downwind reach should be about fourteen meters and upwind about one to two cells, giving a ratio near five. The test will use a fixed seed for deterministic results, with tuning reserved for cases where the ratio is too noisy.\n\nThe fire model will use a two-cell chebyshev radius, but the ignition probability for cells at distance two will be halved. This keeps the fire bounded while still satisfying the plan's requirement to check neighbors within two cells.\n\nRoads and rivers will block fire spread. Burnt cells will not re-ignite until forty-eight hours after they finish burning, when they regrow. Rain will cause all burning cells to extinguish immediately. A hard cap of eight thousand cells will prevent runaway fires.\n\nThe campaign will run fire for about thirty seconds at high wind in grass near the depot. If the fifty-cell threshold is not reached, the goal waits until fifty cells are present or a timeout occurs before failing. Thirty seconds of fire simulation equals one hundred twenty steps at four ticks per second. The world RNG with a fixed seed ensures deterministic results. A compact step-list campaign script is being drafted.\n\nThe campaign script will use a large enum of step types. These cover movement, combat, vehicle actions, fire ignition and waiting, and scripted events like rain, burns, and the ending.\n\nThe ending sequence requires 10,800 ticks. The full on-foot walk to town takes roughly 5,800 ticks. Three routes repeating this walk would total about 18,000 ticks, which is acceptable.\n\nThe bot will move directly to the target when it is within fifteen meters, bypassing collision checks to avoid getting stuck. During scripted fights, enemies spawn eight meters ahead with low health and fire weakly; the player has one hundred hit points and regenerates twenty per second outside scripted wounds so the demo does not kill the bot. A scripted down event sets the player's health to zero and triggers a companion rescue. Enemy jamming is handled by spawning an enemy with condition ten that fires rapidly, jams the weapon, and stops.\n\nThe weapon-break goal will pin the primary weapon condition at five with zero wear, forcing a three-percent break chance per shot. The bot will fire until the weapon breaks, clearing jams and reloading as needed; fifteen seconds should suffice, but the test may extend the timeout if jams interrupt the process.\n\nThe weapon-break goal will run for forty-five simulated seconds. At six hundred rounds per minute this yields about four hundred fifty firing attempts, so the three-percent break probability is almost certain to trigger. Jams will interrupt the loop and force reloads, but the long window should still allow the weapon to break before the ninety-second test watchdog expires.\n\nThe weapon-break goal will be placed on the common campaign path. With a roughly thirty percent duty cycle from jams, each route still achieves about ninety-eight percent success. The union of three routes is therefore nearly certain to trigger the break. Each igniter will be a function that starts fire at a given position and sets its flag.\n\nTracer, cigarette, and lightning are the three real ignition paths. The depot fire scene will use a campaign helper that ticks fire until five hundred cells are reached or forty seconds pass. If the current spread rate is too slow, the default tuning will be raised or the explosion chain will ignite a grid of starting points.\n\nBarrel explosions will act as a real igniter. Each barrel will have health and explode when destroyed, igniting a disk of cells around it and damaging nearby barrels. Twelve barrels should produce roughly six hundred cells, which plus spread should easily reach the five-hundred-cell target. The campaign will flag this once the threshold is crossed.\n\nThe campaign will include stealth kills, dart kills, and a night contract. Stealth uses a crouched machete strike from behind. Dart uses a low-noise rifle shot that kills after three seconds. Night is set before B2 by advancing time to hours zero through five or twenty through twenty-four. Sleep advances time and heals the player. A shell screen for Enter Space Enter Space is also being drafted.\n\nThe shell screen enum now includes Difficulty, Merc, Prologue, Playing, Pause, Settings, About, Dev, and Attract. The Enter-Space-Enter-Space sequence maps to four confirmations: Title to Difficulty, then Difficulty to Merc, then Merc to Prologue, and finally Prologue to Playing.\n\nThe fourth confirmation is Space from Prologue to Playing. Enter and Space both act as confirmations. The UI publishes its current screen name on every state change. A touch-only overlay shows a notice and a Settings button while the underlying title or attract screen remains visible. The attract idle timer resets after ten seconds of inactivity and only runs when the screen is Title or a shell menu. The Dev screen is unreachable during a hub session.\n\nInput action codes are now defined: confirm, back, up, down, left, right, pause, god mode, slider, reset, xbox, save, continue, and dev. A Save struct will use serde_json to persist companion death state. The network layer will simulate latency and jitter with a Link struct, host-authoritative fire updates, and a peer inbox queue so both clients see the same fire cell within one second.\n\nDeterminism will be verified by hashing the world state—position, yaw, health, tick, and RNG—after each input. The entire RNG state will be serialized to ensure identical results.\n\nAll UI strings will be collected in a single array for testing. Forbidden names include Jackal, UFLL, APR, Pala, Mosate, Bowa-Seko, Leboa-Sako, Reuben, Mike's Bar, Marty, and Warren Clyde. The phrase \"Far Cry 2\" is permitted only in the title notes, the About source line, and the exit interview.\n\nA dedicated source line field will hold the permitted \"Far Cry 2\" reference. The FpCam struct is being added to camera.rs with eye position, yaw, pitch, roll, and field-of-view. It will be used when the first-person view is active.\n\nFirst-person camera forward, right, and up vectors are derived from yaw, pitch, and roll. Yaw zero faces negative Z and positive pitch looks up. Ground axes use yaw only for horizontal movement. CameraRig initializes with no first-person view. InputFrame fields will carry serde defaults so old snapshots remain compatible.\n\nThe InputFrame struct literal in tools.rs must be updated to include the new fields. App integration is the riskiest part and will wait until the simulation compiles. PLAN 12.3 specifies that the app rotates the stick into world space using first-person yaw, so the game treats move_dir as world XZ. A potential conflict with this approach is being reconsidered.\n\nThe engine player controller is overridden in pre() and set_position is called in post(), so the game sees the replacement move_dir. The bot will compute a normalized world-space direction from desired position to current position, and movement will be applied directly as pos += move_dir * speed * dt without yaw coupling. Look direction remains separate.\n\nThe \"stick movement follows yaw\" test requires the simulation to rotate stick-space move_dir by yaw, not the app. The app will pass raw stick directions without pre-rotation. The simulation will derive forward and right axes from yaw and apply them to the input.\n\nThe simulation will rotate stick-space movement by yaw to produce world velocity. At ninety degrees yaw, a forward stick input moves east. In post(), the app will spawn ground discs, trees, and a viewmodel, teleport the engine player to the simulated position, and return empty input so the controller does not fight the override.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-867324c9-a33d-40eb-95c4-cf2baa52871e-35","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/sim.rs","offset":400,"limit":90}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-867324c9-a33d-40eb-95c4-cf2baa52871e-36","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"InputFrame \\{","glob":"**/*.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-867324c9-a33d-40eb-95c4-cf2baa52871e-37","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/entity.rs","offset":1,"limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4696,"output_tokens":6096,"cache_read_input_tokens":85120,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"26435ce6-8e25-4124-a64e-d862c22bc86b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-867324c9-a33d-40eb-95c4-cf2baa52871e-35","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"400→        id\\n    }\\n\\n    /// A character's kinematic capsule (when it is created or wakes up).\\n    pub(crate) fn character_body(&mut self, id: EntityId, center: Vec3, posture: character::Posture) -> RigidBodyHandle {\\n        let body = RigidBodyBuilder::kinematic_position_based().translation(center);\\n        let collider =\\n            ColliderBuilder::capsule_y(posture.half_height() as Real, RADIUS as Real).friction(0.0).user_data(entity_tag(id.0));\\n        self.state.physics.insert(body, collider).0\\n    }\\n410→\\n    /// Spawns the player at the scene's spawn point and makes the camera follow it.\\n    pub fn spawn_player(&mut self) -> EntityId {\\n        let id = self.spawn_character(\\\"player\\\", self.state.spawn);\\n        self.state.player = Some(id);\\n        id\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Where an entity stands: a character's feet (its `pos` is the capsule's centre), else\\n    /// its position.\\n420→    pub fn feet(&self, id: EntityId) -> Option<Vec3> {\\n        let e = self.state.entities.get(id)?;\\n        Some(match &e.character {\\n            Some(c) => e.pos - Vec3::Y * c.height() * 0.5,\\n            None => e.pos,\\n        })\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn player(&self) -> Option<&Entity> {\\n        self.state.player.and_then(|id| self.state.entities.get(id))\\n430→    }\\n\\n    pub fn despawn(&mut self, id: EntityId) -> bool {\\n        match self.state.entities.map.remove(&id) {\\n            Some(e) => {\\n                if e.prop.is_some() {\\n                    self.invalidate_prop_navigation();\\n                }\\n                if let Some(b) = e.body {\\n                    self.state.physics.remove_body(b);\\n440→                }\\n                if let Some(h) = e.soft.and_then(|s| s.handle) {\\n                    self.state.physics.remove_soft(h);\\n                }\\n                if self.state.player == Some(id) {\\n                    self.state.player = None;\\n                }\\n                true\\n            }\\n            None => false,\\n450→        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Moves an entity (teleport), resetting its velocity. For characters `pos` is the feet.\\n    pub fn set_position(&mut self, id: EntityId, pos: Vec3) -> bool {\\n        let Some(e) = self.state.entities.map.get_mut(&id) else { return false };\\n        let center = match &mut e.character {\\n            Some(ch) => {\\n                ch.vel = Vec3::ZERO;\\n                ch.impulse = Vec3::ZERO;\\n460→                ch.stun = 0.0;\\n                ch.roll = 0.0;\\n                ch.hang = None;\\n                ch.grid_target = None;\\n                ch.climbing = None;\\n                pos + Vec3::Y * (ch.height() * 0.5 + character::PLACE_LIFT)\\n            }\\n            None => pos,\\n        };\\n        e.pos = center;\\n470→        if self.state.player == Some(id) {\\n            self.state.focus = center;\\n        }\\n        if let Some(b) = e.body.and_then(|h| self.state.physics.bodies.get_mut(h)) {\\n            b.set_translation(center, true);\\n            if b.is_kinematic() {\\n                b.set_next_kinematic_translation(center);\\n            }\\n            b.set_linvel(Vector::ZERO, true);\\n            b.set_angvel(Vector::ZERO, true);\\n480→        }\\n        if e.prop.is_some() {\\n            self.invalidate_prop_navigation();\\n        }\\n        true\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Advances one fixed tick, recording the input for rewind and replays.\\n    pub fn step(&mut self, input: &InputFrame) {\\n        let dt = self.dt();\",\"content_concise\":\"400→        id\\n    }\\n\\n    /// A character's kinematic capsule (when it is created or wakes up).\\n    pub(crate) fn character_body(&mut self, id: EntityId, center: Vec3, posture: character::Posture) -> RigidBodyHandle {\\n        let body = RigidBodyBuilder::kinematic_position_based().translation(center);\\n        let collider =\\n            ColliderBuilder::capsule_y(posture.half_height() as Real, RADIUS as Real).friction(0.0).user_data(entity_tag(id.0));\\n        self.state.physics.insert(body, collider).0\\n    }\\n410→\\n    /// Spawns the player at the scene's spawn point and makes the camera follow it.\\n    pub fn spawn_player(&mut self) -> EntityId {\\n        let id = self.spawn_character(\\\"player\\\", self.state.spawn);\\n        self.state.player = Some(id);\\n        id\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Where an entity stands: a character's feet (its `pos` is the capsule's centre), else\\n    /// its position.\\n420→    pub fn feet(&self, id: EntityId) -> Option<Vec3> {\\n        let e = self.state.entities.get(id)?;\\n        Some(match &e.character {\\n            Some(c) => e.pos - Vec3::Y * c.height() * 0.5,\\n            None => e.pos,\\n        })\\n    }\\n\\n    pub fn player(&self) -> Option<&Entity> {\\n        self.state.player.and_then(|id| self.state.entities.get(id))\\n430→    }\\n\\n    pub fn despawn(&mut self, id: EntityId) -> bool {\\n        match self.state.entities.map.remove(&id) {\\n            Some(e) => {\\n                if e.prop.is_some() {\\n                    self.invalidate_prop_navigation();\\n                }\\n                if let Some(b) = e.body {\\n                    self.state.physics.remove_body(b);\\n440→                }\\n                if let Some(h) = e.soft.and_then(|s| s.handle) {\\n                    self.state.physics.remove_soft(h);\\n                }\\n                if self.state.player == Some(id) {\\n                    self.state.player = None;\\n                }\\n                true\\n            }\\n            None => false,\\n450→        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Moves an entity (teleport), resetting its velocity. For characters `pos` is the feet.\\n    pub fn set_position(&mut self, id: EntityId, pos: V