{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_62","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Starting to write the code now. It will be split into manageable files.\n\nDesigning a robust campaign playthrough function. It simulates routes with a 25-minute timeout and tracks stuck periods when the goal name doesn't change.\n\nAdding a stuck-goal panic when the timer exceeds the goal budget. The simulation budget is roughly 270,000 ticks across three routes. Fire updates will be limited to every 0.25 seconds. NPC AI will use only a few characters and despawn once their goals are complete. Walking and healing sequences are lightweight enough to run at full speed.\n\nSleep and bus transitions will be fast-forwarded as single ticks that advance game time. The mast hold step must use real ticks because tests may verify time passes while the player stays alive. Those 10,800 ticks are cheap since they skip fire and AI when enemies are distant. A goal machine is being designed with a Step struct and StepKind enum to define each route segment.\n\nSwitched to a simpler `Goal` enum with variants for Title, Map, Waypoint, GPS, Reach, Fight, and Done. A `goal_tick` function will match on the current goal and return input. `begin_goal` will set up the situation when entering each goal. The Burn route sequence is being drafted now, starting with Title.\n\nThe Burn route sequence is now fully detailed. It holds MAP for half a second, sets the waypoint to town on release, walks until 25 meters from start toward that waypoint, then reaches town at (0, 460). After a fever warning, Burn and Chair routes take pills for 2.5 seconds while Read waits 60 seconds before blackout.\n\nCampaign diamonds may be zero while the unit test expects forty. The Read route still sets the blackout flag.\n\nThe remaining Burn and Chair steps are now mapped out. They cover clearing enemies, spawning a jammed enemy, forcing a gun jam on the player, rescuing the companion within three seconds, taking six sequential wounds, looting a rifle, and claiming the safe house.\n\nThe Burn route sequence continues. It now includes buying a weapon after paying a licence, using the locker, syrette, and sleep. Contract flags are set at town arrival and after sleep. The drive to the depot requires a technical vehicle and sets wind rag. It also includes a monocular tag, an igniters sequence, burn 500, mounted gun, panic from fire, animal encounters, and contract b1.\n\nThe Burn route sequence continues. It now includes sleeping at the far safe house to pass twenty-four hours, remanning the GP1 post, driving a boat to the pump, stealth and dart kills, and contract b2.\n\nIt also covers repairing a damaged vehicle, extinguishing rain, and contract b3. The B4 branch splits into three paths: patching the companion for Burn, overdosing for Read, or waiting forty-five seconds for death in Chair. Contract b4 follows.\n\nThe remaining steps are playing the hotel tape, ending the ceasefire with a sniper, and contract c1.\n\nThe Burn route sequence is now complete. It adds convoy rockets for contract c2, a town burn for contract c3, cement work for contract d1, and an ending for contract d2 plus the ending flag.\n\nAdditional steps are being added to the common route: a bus ride between the depot and a far place, a tracker cache near a beep rate with a cache_tracker flag, and a weapon break during a fight or as a dedicated goal. Explosion chains will occur during the igniters sequence.\n\nGPS will only be marked complete after actual movement toward the waypoint. Places will be implemented as a const array. A river polyline is planned for the water test, and a height function is being drafted for vehicle slope.\n\nThe terrain height function now models a ridge road from (200, -400) to (500, -700) with a sine-based elevation. Vehicle tests will start at x=200 and drive forward while checking roll recovery. Boats will throttle on the river but remain stationary in grass. A Python-less test file is also being prepared. Each screen's focusable rows will be limited to seven.\n\nSettings and dev pages will each be one screen with at most seven rows. About is paged.\n\nI will patch app.rs after compilation because line numbers may shift.\n\nworld.rs will be written in a single large block of two to three thousand lines. I will draft it carefully so it compiles on the first attempt.\n\ngames/mod.rs needs `pub mod merc` so external tests can import `pav_core::games::merc`. The current module is `pub mod starter`.\n\nRng must derive Serialize for serde.\n\nWorld will use BTreeSet<String> for flags and skip visual IDs in serde. A companion death save test is being drafted to verify that loading preserves dead states. The buy path with zero diamonds is also being tested.\n\nThe jam rate test will use `Gun::pull_trigger`, which the tick function already calls. A `Trigger` enum is being defined with four outcomes: Dry, Jammed, Fired, and Broke. The exact meaning of \"broke\" is being clarified—whether a shot fires and then breaks the weapon, or if the attempt itself breaks before firing.\n\nThe gun trigger logic is now defined. Jam is checked first; if the weapon is not jammed and has ammo, it fires. Break is rolled after firing, so a shot may still count even if the weapon breaks. A jam blocks all further shots until cleared.\n\nClearing a jam requires reloading. The reload timer starts at 1.2 seconds and scales up to 2.5 seconds based on condition. Tapping reload again within that window reduces the remaining time by forty percent.\n\nThe jam clear timer will be 1.8 seconds at condition 35. A force jam flag will jam the next shot without rolling. Enemy jamming uses condition 8 with high RPM and stops firing when cleared. Spread scales from 0.01 rad at full condition to 0.09 rad at zero, multiplied by 0.25 in ADS. At ten meters this yields a two-centimeter spread inside an eighteen-centimeter head radius.\n\nA ray-AABB intersection test is being drafted to handle wall shots. Dummies will be modeled as vertical segments with head, torso, and leg spheres. Damage will scale by zone multiplier, with the pistol doing 66 damage to the head, 22 to the torso, and 13.2 to limbs.\n\nThe gun struct will track shots and jams for test verification. Condition drops by wear after each real shot, so jam rate tests must reset condition between trials or set wear to zero to keep the value stable.\n\nThe weapon break test will hold fire and reload through jams by driving `World::tick` with a held primary input. Condition is fixed at 5 and wear at 0 so the jam chance stays high. A guard loop limits ticks to 30 seconds.\n\nThe weapon break test will keep the gun selected, hold fire, and press reload whenever the weapon jams. Ammo must stay high so the loop does not run out. The tick function will process look input, menu logic, movement, weapons, healing, AI, fire simulation, fever, and vehicles in that order.\n\nCampaign starts at the title screen. The bot confirms the title, then transitions to the map once all confirmations are received. Shell confirm logic inside tick will handle act confirm, jump, and confirm presses. Difficulty focus resets to one when entering a screen. Screen transitions are handled explicitly in `shell_confirm`. The attract screen activates after ten seconds of idle input on any menu screen.\n\nAny input during attract mode returns to the title screen and resets idle time. The attract script will not write saves, so a flag will skip `save()` calls. The attract sequence runs for eight hundred steps across ten segments, and the test will verify that all segments are visited.\n\nThe attract sequence requires five full loops. It only begins after ten seconds of idle input on the title or shell screens. Menu rows are laid out at fixed vertical positions, and pointer hover selects the focused row. A prompt helper will generate confirmation labels based on the active device type. Music support is also being added to the audio module.\n\nMusic gain is fixed at 0.10. The renderer will use a Karplus-Strass pluck at 0.3 amplitude to avoid clipping. Gun family patches will be compared by their first nonzero samples after 0.2 seconds. Voice synthesis will use formant sines with pitch derived from speaker name hashes. Crackle amplitude scales with cell count, and tests will verify that RMS energy increases.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0861f3ea-d818-4e38-a3b4-993d0e2b3d0e-44","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"\\[workspace.lints|dead_code|unused","glob":"**/engine/Cargo.toml"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":477,"output_tokens":3983,"cache_read_input_tokens":103680,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"03b67264-19d6-4204-9c3b-8b3faa0d528c"}
