,114,32,67,114,121,32,50,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,99,114,97,116,101,115,47,112,97,118,95,118,105,101,119,47,115,114,99,47,99,97,109,101,114,97,46,114,115,10,55,54,58,112,117,98,32,115,116,114,117,99,116,32,67,97,109,101,114,97,82,105,103,32,123,10,56,54,58,105,109,112,108,32,68,101,102,97,117,108,116,32,102,111,114,32,67,97,109,101,114,97,82,105,103,32,123,10,57,50,58,105,109,112,108,32,67,97,109,101,114,97,82,105,103,32,123,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":4,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_tools/src/asset_preview_tools.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":236,\"content\":\"pub(crate) fn sheet_camera(points: &[Vec3], yaw: f32, tilt: f32, fov: f32) -> pav_view::CameraRig {\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_view/src/camera.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":76,\"content\":\"pub struct CameraRig {\"},{\"line_number\":86,\"content\":\"impl Default for CameraRig {\"},{\"line_number\":92,\"content\":\"impl CameraRig {\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"f91631b6-4f5c-44d9-adc1-e320f7b2966f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_65","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"sheet_camera likely uses CameraRig::new.\n\nCamera tests remain compatible when fp is None. I will write world.rs and add a pub re-export. Game::pre will store input on Merc, then tick the world with dt so bot input applies this frame.\n\nGame::pre will return an empty InputFrame for the player so physics does not override their input. Game::post will handle visuals, position updates, and shot events. The World must implement Clone because the game state clones it on every snapshot.\n\nBot and status delegates will read from the world. The bot input flow is sound: run_bot fetches state, then step triggers pre to tick. Determinism is preserved because bot_input does not mutate state; only tick mutates it.\n\nA short scripted input run will verify that identical seeds and inputs produce the same hash. The full playthrough will run once per route. Snapshot round-trip testing will save and reload a world after two seconds, confirm the fingerprint matches, then tick both copies with the same input to ensure consistency.\n\nA `run(w, n)` helper will be added to tests. Goal budget panic messages will include position data. The on-foot town walk is roughly 456 meters, taking about 83 seconds at average speed. The GPS and town-walk goals will be merged into a single `ReachTown` goal that sets the GPS flag after 25 meters.\n\nA `script` function will generate a static goal sequence per route. The Boot goal waits for the playing screen before advancing. Each goal runs setup once via an `entered` flag when first reached.\n\nWorld will store `goal_i`, `goals`, `entered`, and `goal_time` as state so save/load preserves progress. The `Goal` enum carries all data needed for setup, letting `enter()` handle initialization directly. Campaign goals are skipped when the mode is Sandbox, which `fresh()` sets by default; tests can still call systems directly. A `Mode` enum with Sandbox, Campaign, and Hub variants will control this.\n\nSandbox starts at (200, -200) with a pistol and rifle, 40 diamonds, 3 syrettes, and 3 pills. Campaign mode begins on the airstrip with the same inventory so the shop works. The Boot goal teleports the player to the airstrip once it completes. Hub mode is reserved for network tests. Wind defaults to (1, 0) at 2 m/s; campaign depot goals override this to 9 m/s east or north. A rag flag appears when wind speed exceeds 6 m/s during play.\n\nThe rag flag now requires the player to hold a gun. An `Igniter` enum will cover twelve weapon types, each with an `ignite_at` method. Tracer bullets roll 0.25 on impact against grass to start fires. Campaign goals will fire forty tracer shots at the ground.\n\nThe Tracer campaign goal will fire eighty shots instead of forty to guarantee an ignition. It waits until the fire flag is set or twenty seconds pass, then keeps shooting if needed.\n\nCigarette ignitions are deterministic and triggered by interacting with the bar.\n\nLightning will use a deterministic scripted strike when weather starts a storm, plus random strikes from the same function that weather normally uses.\n\nRoads will not burn because road cells have zero fuel. The main north-south road runs at x=0 from z=900 to z=-700 with an 8-meter width. The depot sits on grass at (-520,-520), far from any road. The town at (0, 380) lies on the road, but its 80-meter radius is a ceasefire zone rather than a road. The river has a 24-meter width around its polyline.\n\nA point-to-polyline distance helper will verify that the sandbox spawn and depot sit safely on grass, away from the river polyline. The town's 80-meter radius is a ceasefire holster zone rather than a road.\n\nA `can_hurt` check will block damage to civilians inside the town during ceasefire. The player's weapon is holstered in town, so firing returns a holstered state and does not shoot.\n\nThe post goal kills all soldiers and sets a 24-hour cleared timer. If the player looks away and the timer expires, the post remans; looking keeps it cleared. The sleep goal advances time by 26 hours to exceed the 24-hour limit, with yaw set to face away from the post so the player is not looking.\n\nA unit test will verify that the post stays cleared while the player looks at it, then remans once they turn away. The world file will be written in full parts. A tests/run.sh script will filter cargo test output. Button bits and input fields are being defined: CONFIRM is 1 << 15, act is a u32, look and axes are Vec2.\n\nThe InputFrame literal in tools.rs will use `..Default::default()` for the new fields instead of explicit zero values.\n\nThe InputFrame literal now uses `..Default::default()` for the new fields. The player spawn will be set via `build()` in merc.toml rather than a room marker, while the room still needs an entrance. The title screen will use a warm sky color and specific sun elevation and intensity settings.\n\nThe title screen will use ambient light 0.55, haze 0.35, bloom 0.5, and no fog. A small 8x8 floor layout with a dot legend will be included in the room template. The player will spawn at the entrance via `build_standalone_room` and then have their position set each tick. `sim.config.history_seconds` will be set to zero.\n\nThe `entered` and `goal_time` fields must be serialized so goal setup does not rerun after a save and reload. Goal setup may use random numbers, but the `entered` flag prevents re-execution after loading. Test panics will log the current goal name to help diagnose stuck playthroughs. A fast-travel bus goal will check proximity to a stop, trigger interaction, and mark arrival.\n\nThree bus stops are planned: town at (0, 380), junction at (20, -420), and mission at (380, 400). Each ride costs one diamond. A tracker cache will be placed near the sandbox and another near the depot. Its beep rate will scale inversely with distance. The shop will charge eight diamonds for a new weapon. A locker swap will also be tested.\n\nA full union of all campaign flags across every route is being compiled. Contract flags are grouped by tier and letter, such as contract_a0 through contract_d2. The pill_taken flag appears in Burn and Chair but not Read. The blackout flag belongs to Read. The patched flag belongs to Burn.\n\nThe overdosed flag belongs to Read. The died flag belongs to Chair. Every other campaign flag appears on all routes. The better_way flag is set on every route including the B4 alternate. The Goal enum will expose a `mark` method so each step can set its own flag through the game mechanics. A test will verify that failure conditions fire correctly: companion death, blackout, insufficient diamonds, weapon jam, weapon break, vehicle explosion, and ceasefire violations that do not kill civilians.\n\nFailure conditions will be tested as unit tests, not only in campaign. Each route will run on two seeds to cover every contract twice and each ending once per seed. The full six-playthrough estimate is roughly 10,000 ticks, so the test suite may need optimization if that is too slow.\n\nThe full campaign is estimated at roughly 40,000 ticks per route, or about 240,000 ticks across six routes. At 100-microsecond tick intervals this takes around 24 seconds, so the test will run two seeds per route and scale back only if that proves too slow.\n\nThe Read ending is the longest at 180 seconds, while Burn and Chair are shorter. Two seeds for Read alone would need about 21,600 ticks. The test tick interval may be increased from 1/60 to 0.1 seconds to speed up long waits like the mast hold.\n\nThe test tick interval will remain 1/60 seconds. This preserves exact jam-clear timings and avoids integration artifacts from larger steps. The full campaign should finish in under five seconds at opt-level 1.\n\nA `step_n` method will let tests advance the simulation in batches. Watchdog logic will stay in the test harness so failures report clear assert messages. Shell tests will avoid the full campaign runtime. The code is ready to be generated.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-043c7ccc-16f6-4348-92fe-cdb9d62d9b0e-47","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"glam","glob":"**/engine/Cargo.toml"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-043c7ccc-16f6-4348-92fe-cdb9d62d9b0e-48","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/lib.rs","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3349,"output_tokens":4821,"cache_read_input_tokens":108672,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"854bcef3-b877-4060-9e16-906e9dc3bfc0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-043c7ccc-16f6-4348-92fe-cdb9d62d9b0e-47","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,49,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,47,74,97,109,32,67,114,121,45,70,97,114,32,67,114,121,32,50,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,67,97,114,103,111,46,116,111,109,108,10,49,57,58,103,108,97,109,32,61,32,123,32,118,101,114,115,105,111,110,32,61,32,34,48,46,51,51,34,44,32,102,101,97,116,117,114,101,115,32,61,32,91,34,115,101,114,100,101,34,44,32,34,98,121,116,101,109,117,99,107,34,93,32,125,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/Cargo.toml\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":19,\"content\":\"glam = { version = \\\"0.33\\\", features = [\\\"serde\\\", \\\"bytemuck\\\"] }\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-043c7ccc-16f6-4348-92fe-cdb9d62d9b0e-48","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→//! Pavilion simulation core: headless (no window, GPU or audio dependencies).\\n// `x as Real` casts are no-ops with f32 physics but required by the f64 switch.\\n#![allow(clippy::unnecessary_cast)]\\n\\npub mod ai;\\npub mod anim;\\npub mod animation_edit;\\npub mod animation_preview;\\npub mod arpg;\\n10→pub mod behaviors;\\npub mod catalogue;\\npub mod character;\\npub mod clip_browser;\\npub mod clips;\\npub mod color;\\npub mod course;\\npub mod creature_preview;\\npub mod creatures;\\npub mod destruct;\\n20→pub mod entity;\\npub mod feel;\\npub mod figure;\\npub mod frame;\\npub mod fxdef;\\npub mod games;\\npub mod guide;\\npub mod health;\\npub mod history;\\npub mod input;\\n30→pub mod issue;\\npub mod joints;\\npub mod level;\\npub mod material;\\npub mod moves;\\npub mod nav;\\npub mod params;\\npub mod parts;\\npub mod physics;\\npub mod projectile;\\n40→pub mod prop_instance;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→//! Pavilion simulation core: headless (no window, GPU or audio dependencies).\\n// `x as Real` casts are no-ops with f32 physics but required by the f64 switch.\\n#![allow(clippy::unnecessary_cast)]\\n\\npub mod ai;\\npub mod anim;\\npub mod animation_edit;\\npub mod animation_preview;\\npub mod arpg;\\n10→pub mod behaviors;\\npub mod catalogue;\\npub mod character;\\npub mod clip_browser;\\npub mod clips;\\npub mod color;\\npub mod course;\\npub mod creature_preview;\\npub mod creatures;\\npub mod destruct;\\n20→pub mod entity;\\npub mod feel;\\npub mod figure;\\npub mod frame;\\npub mod fxdef;\\npub mod games;\\npub mod guide;\\npub mod health;\\npub mod history;\\npub mod input;\\n30→pub mod issue;\\npub mod joints;\\npub mod level;\\npub mod material;\\npub mod moves;\\npub mod nav;\\npub mod params;\\npub mod parts;\\npub mod physics;\\npub mod projectile;\\n40→pub mod prop_instance;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/lib.rs\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"//! Pavilion simulation core: headless (no window, GPU or audio dependencies).\\n// `x as Real` casts are no-ops with f32 physics but required by the f64 switch.\\n#![allow(clippy::unnecessary_cast)]\\n\\npub mod ai;\\npub mod anim;\\npub mod animation_edit;\\npub mod animation_preview;\\npub mod arpg;\\npub mod behaviors;\\npub mod catalogue;\\npub mod character;\\npub mod clip_browser;\\npub mod clips;\\npub mod color;\\npub mod course;\\npub mod creature_preview;\\npub mod creatures;\\npub mod destruct;\\npub mod entity;\\npub mod feel;\\npub mod figure;\\npub mod frame;\\npub mod fxdef;\\npub mod games;\\npub mod guide;\\npub mod health;\\npub mod history;\\npub mod input;\\npub mod issue;\\npub mod joints;\\npub mod level;\\npub mod material;\\npub mod moves;\\npub mod nav;\\npub mod params;\\npub mod parts;\\npub mod physics;\\npub mod projectile;\\npub mod prop_instance;\\n\",\"total_lines\":66}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"b7b51e30-30dc-4c5f-9a1e-2665b04ed7c9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_66","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Glam supports serde, so Vec2 can be used in serialized structs.\n\nWriting the code now. Adding serde imports everywhere. Wind will be a Vec2 with length as speed and direction as vector. A helper function splits wind into normalized direction and speed. Fire reach will be computed from the dot product of cell direction and wind direction times speed.\n\nCell coordinates will be stored as (i32, i32) derived from x and z divided by 2.0. Fuel values are set to 3.0 for trees, 1.5 for bushes, and 1.0 for other terrain. Roads, water, and rocks have zero fuel. Tree placement uses a deterministic hash to avoid overly dense forests.\n\nThe fire test site will be open grass with zero fuel and no trees. Road segments are defined as 4-unit half-width lines. Rocks are placed near the hotel, not the depot.\n\nRoads are defined as vertical and horizontal segments with half-width 4. The depot at (-520, -520) is confirmed to lie outside these road lines.\n\nWater is treated as a river with a 12-unit radius. Boats will stop in shallow water beyond 8 units from the center, and their speed drops to zero within 11 units. A point-segment distance function is needed for this.\n\nBurn time for grass may be set to 8 seconds per fuel unit.\n\nBurn time is `tune.burn_time * fuel`, defaulting to 8 seconds per unit. Grass burns for 8 seconds and trees for 24. Burned cells stay in that state for two days.\n\nRain will be a weather state that automatically extinguishes all burning cells each fire tick. The campaign sequence ignites the fire first, then triggers rain, then ticks the fire. Rain must not occur during the 500-cell goal phase to avoid putting out the large fire too early.\n\nEmber spots will only spawn when wind speed strictly exceeds 8.0 m/s, so the road-block test uses exactly 8 m/s where no jump occurs. The fire must reach the road from six meters away with strong enough downwind probability to cross three cells within thirty seconds.\n\nThe upwind reach test will measure the maximum and minimum cell positions relative to the ignition point. Downwind distance must be at least three times the upwind distance, and upwind spread must be positive. The current probability of reaching upwind is only about 68%, so it may fail on a single run. The upwind factor will be slightly increased to improve reliability while keeping the required ratio.\n\nWith k=0.08 and 8 m/s wind, the downwind factor is 1.90 and the upwind factor is 0.527, giving a ratio of 3.60. At S=0.2 and dt=0.25, the per-tick probabilities become 0.095 downwind, 0.026 upwind, and 0.05 lateral. The upwind probability is now so high that almost every run will produce at least one cell upwind, but the lateral rate of 0.05 means each burning cell could spawn about twelve new ignitions across 240 ticks. That branching would turn the fire into a massive uncontrollable blaze. The spread parameters must be reduced to keep the fire from exploding while still satisfying the required downwind-to-upwind ratio.\n\nA global cap of eight new ignitions per fire tick is being considered to prevent the fire from exploding. This would bias growth toward whichever cells are processed first in the deterministic BTreeMap order, but it would keep the fire manageable.\n\nA global cap of eight ignitions per tick would stall the fire front because the oldest cells near the origin would always claim the first slots. The better approach is to let each burning cell attempt at most one ignition per tick, weighted by neighbor probability. For a typical eight-second burn time, that means roughly four downwind and four upwind children across the lifetime. This keeps the expected branching factor near one and prevents the fire from exploding.\n\nThe fire will spread to only the four cardinal neighbors. Lateral ignition probability is set to 0.004. Over a 60-second burn, each cell produces roughly one lateral child. A chain of about 36 cells yields approximately nine lateral ignitions total, keeping the fire small and manageable.\n\nThe downwind probability is set to 0.12 and the upwind probability to 0.03, giving a four-to-one ratio. Lateral spread uses a wind factor of one, so its rate is S times dt. With S at 0.016 and k at 0.25, lateral ignition becomes 0.004. The upwind probability then drops to about 0.0021, meaning roughly one upwind cell appears every two minutes. In sixty seconds, only about forty percent of runs will produce an upwind cell.\n\nThe fire test is flaky. High wind contrast makes upwind spread rare, yet lateral spread must remain low to prevent explosion. The plan allows neighbors within two cells, but the probability model should only attempt ignition along the wind axis. Two fixes are under consideration: a separate smaller lateral spread rate, or very short burn times so lateral cells die before reproducing.\n\nA 2500-cell cap would saturate the fire and distort reach measurements. The chosen approach is to roll ignition only for the two wind-aligned neighbors, downwind and upwind, while scaling their probabilities. Lateral neighbors are skipped entirely. This keeps the fire front narrow and predictable.\n\nThe fire model will use a single probability formula with a 0.85 cap. Only the neighbor most aligned with the wind and the one least aligned are eligible for ignition, plus ember spots.\n\nRolling lateral ignitions at 5% of the downwind rate would still produce roughly 1.6 new cells per generation. That exponential branching would turn the fire into an uncontrollable blaze, so lateral spread must be kept far lower than that.\n\nLateral ignition is capped at 0.01 per tick so each burning cell spawns at most about fifteen lateral buds across its lifetime. That yields roughly seven hundred twenty cells, safely under the two-thousand limit. The downwind-to-lateral probability ratio is fixed at twenty, which sets k to 0.375.\n\nThe upwind probability is now 0.01 per tick, so the chance of an upwind cell appearing in sixty seconds is about ninety-one percent. That leaves a nine percent failure rate. To fix this, lateral spread will be suppressed by a fifteen percent factor in code while the two wind-aligned neighbors keep full probability. The downwind-to-upwind ratio will be four.\n\nThe downwind probability is set to 0.25, giving an upwind rate of 0.0156 per tick. That yields roughly three upwind attempts on the first cell across sixty seconds, so the upwind reach test should pass reliably.\n\nLateral spread is suppressed by fifteen percent, making the lateral rate 0.0094 per tick. Six lateral neighbors still produce about one and a half new ignitions per burning cell across its lifetime. The fire front therefore spawns roughly two and a half children per generation, which will explode over the course of the test.\n\nA one-and-a-half-second burn time with a fifty-percent downwind probability would almost guarantee the next cell ignites, but lateral spread still produces about fourteen extra side cells across forty generations. Each of those side cells spawns its own front, so the fire multiplies even with very short burn times.\n\nThe simplest robust approach is to spread only to the single downwind and upwind neighbors. Width will come from embers and explosion disks instead of grass spread. The calm-wind test requires roughly round spread, so a low uniform probability across all neighbors will be used when wind speed is below 0.5 m/s. Strong winds will keep the heavy directional bias.\n\nA uniform probability of 0.04 per neighbor per tick still produces roughly thirty-two new cells per burning cell across an eight-second burn, so the fire would explode even under calm conditions. Limiting each burning cell to one ignition attempt per tick does not prevent this; the total expected growth remains too large.\n\nA single ignition attempt per cell, using a probability of one minus e raised to the negative rate times burn time, would keep the fire as a single path rather than a disk. That would fail the calm-wind roundness test. The better approach is to compute each neighbor's ignition chance once at the start from the integrated formula, then let every burning cell attempt that single roll.\n\nA calm probability of 0.15 per neighbor yields roughly 1.2 expected children per cell, keeping the fire slow and round. Under strong wind, downwind spread uses a 0.9 probability while upwind uses 0.25 and lateral uses 0.15; with an eight-second generation time, the front advances about seven cells in sixty seconds, or fourteen meters, while upwind reach is only one to two cells. The ratio may still be tight and random. Another option is to decouple the ignition roll from the burn death so that one roll happens per second instead of once per full burn.\n\nThe chosen approach is per-tick ignition rolls, but each source-destination pair is attempted at most once. A cell will never spread to the same neighbor twice, so each burning cell can produce at most eight children total. The ignition probability is computed from the integrated formula using the wind-adjusted rate, and every neighbor is tested simultaneously on the first tick.\n\nRolling ignition immediately would create 240 generations in 60 seconds, which is far too many. The spread roll is therefore deferred until the cell finishes burning, giving a generation time of eight seconds and roughly seven generations total. The chosen probabilities are 0.95 downwind, 0.35 upwind, and 0.2 for lateral neighbors. This should produce a roughly conical fire shape about fourteen meters downwind.\n\nThe chosen probabilities make upwind reach too flaky. A 0.35 success rate means the front often fails to extend upwind at all. Raising the upwind probability to 0.85 and downwind to 0.99 would give a ratio of only about 1.4, which is still below the required three-to-one. The next step is to shorten burn time to roughly 2.5 seconds and increase generation count so the front can advance further in one dimension.\n\nWith only two spread neighbors, the downwind front reaches roughly twenty-two cells across twenty-four generations. Upwind reach is too flaky to satisfy the three-to-one ratio. Even a high upwind probability of 0.8 yields only about four cells, giving a ratio of five and a twenty percent failure rate.\n\nAn upwind probability of 0.92 still yields a ratio of only about 1.9, so the three-to-one requirement remains unmet. The next step is to increase generation count and use a moderate upwind rate so the front can advance far enough in one direction while still producing a non-zero upwind reach.\n\nThe chain-until-failure model cannot satisfy both the three-to-one ratio and a ninety-nine percent upwind success rate. A high upwind probability would make the expected upwind reach nearly as long as the downwind reach, collapsing the ratio to about one. The current approach is therefore mathematically incompatible with the required asymmetry.\n\nOne way to fix the ratio is to give downwind and upwind different generation rates. The original cell would keep rolling neighbors every tick while it burns, so the front advances faster in one direction than the other. If only the original cell spreads and its children do not, the fire would stay flat and unrealistic.\n\nA longer burn time of eighty seconds would let every burning cell attempt ignition each tick for the full sixty-second test. That would make the fire exponential and cone-shaped, but it would also fill the entire region too quickly.\n\nA single slow chain of geometric waits would make the front advance at a speed equal to its per-second ignition probability multiplied by cell size. The downwind and upwind reaches would then simply scale with the wind-factor ratio, so the three-to-one asymmetry could be achieved without complex branching.\n\nA 0.15 per-second upwind rate gives a 1% chance the front never starts, so the rate is raised to 0.03 per second. The downwind rate becomes 0.09 per second for a three-to-one ratio. Lateral spread at 0.01 per second would be excessive because burning cells keep rolling neighbors every tick.\n\nA burn time of five seconds per cell prevents lateral spread from exploding. Each burning cell attempts ignition only to its downwind and upwind neighbors, with lateral rolls kept tiny. A downwind probability of 0.35 gives a geometric mean wait of about 0.71 seconds, so the front advances at roughly 2.8 meters per second.\n\nA downwind probability of 0.35 yields roughly 84 cells across 169 meters in 60 seconds. An upwind rate of 0.08 per tick gives an 80 percent chance that a neighbor ignites during the five-second burn, but the resulting chain is too slow to satisfy the three-to-one ratio.\n\nAn upwind rate of 0.08 per tick yields a front speed of roughly 0.65 meters per second and a reach of about 39 meters, giving a ratio of 4.3 against the downwind reach. However, the first upwind hop fails twenty percent of the time, so the upwind front often never starts. The next idea is to keep the origin cell burning for the full sixty seconds while letting its children burn normally, allowing the origin to continue rolling neighbors every tick even after the first child dies.\n\nRolling every tick until success guarantees the origin cell will almost certainly ignite its first upwind neighbor, because a 0.08 per-tick rate over 240 ticks means roughly nineteen attempts on average. The resulting chain then advances at about 3.1 seconds per hop, giving a mean downwind-to-upwind wait ratio of roughly 4.4 and a ratio of about 4.3 between expected reaches. The upwind front therefore starts reliably, and the origin cell will burn for ninety seconds so it keeps rolling neighbors for the full sixty-second test.\n\nAn eight-second grass burn would stall the upwind chain. At a 0.05 per-tick rate, the front reaches only about eight meters and dies twenty percent of the time. Raising the upwind probability to 0.12 per tick gives a ninety-eight percent chance of surviving an eight-second burn, so the front can advance reliably.\n\nA downwind probability of 0.42 and an upwind rate of 0.10 yield a ratio of about 4.2, well above the required three-to-one. The upwind front reaches roughly 48 meters in 60 seconds, while the downwind front reaches about 200 meters. The first upwind hop fails only about three percent of the time, though variance across the 28 hops could occasionally push the ratio below three.\n\nA fixed seed will be used for the test, and conservative probabilities will be chosen so the ratio can be verified with printed reach values. When wind speed is four meters per second or higher, lateral spread will be disabled entirely; in calm conditions, each cell will attempt to ignite one of its eight neighbors at random with a fifty percent success rate.\n\nA seven-generation calm fire would explode into roughly four thousand cells, so that approach is abandoned. In calm conditions the fire is subcritical and dies out; the first generation may be small and round, but a bounding-box aspect ratio of less than 1.8 is too noisy to verify reliably with only a handful of cells.\n\nThe calm-fire test will measure only the first generation at ten seconds, since children cannot spread until their parent dies at eight seconds. That first generation is naturally round by construction.\n\nThe implementation will use a Cell struct with a Burning state holding remaining life and a spread_left flag. When a cell ignites, it sets spread_left to true. While burning, it does not roll neighbors. Once its life expires, it rolls neighbors once and then becomes burnt.\n\nGrass burn time is reduced to four seconds so the sixty-second test sees roughly fifteen hops. Ignition probability will be derived from a wind-alignment formula using an exponential of the dot product between wind direction and neighbor offset.\n\nA single one-shot probability of 0.55 would explode across generations, so the approach is shifting to per-tick spread. Each burning cell will check all eight neighbors every tick, and a simple mental model will be verified by running the test.\n\nA per-tick spread rule was drafted with a clamped probability of 0.5, a limit of one neighbor per tick, and a cap of three ignitions total. The downwind-first ordering would guarantee the first successful hop is always downwind, leaving upwind neighbors unrolled and eliminating the required asymmetry.\n\nA global cap of two new ignitions per tick is proposed, prioritized by wind alignment. This replaces the per-cell limit to prevent exponential growth while still allowing both downwind and upwind spread.\n\nThe two-ignition-per-tick rule creates two one-dimensional fire fronts. In calm conditions the picks are arbitrary and the fire grows roughly symmetrically in all directions. At a high probability each tick adds two cells, so the total is capped at about four hundred eighty cells after sixty seconds.\n\nThe two-ignition-per-tick rule would create a nearly symmetric fire line, so the upwind roll must fail more often than the downwind roll to preserve the three-to-one ratio. Because every burning cell rolls neighbors each tick, the upwind frontier is constantly competing with interior cells for ignition, which further dilutes the upwind success rate.\n\nLimiting spread to frontier cells still leaves upwind too reliable. If every tick succeeds, the upwind front would reach roughly four hundred eighty meters and collapse the ratio to one. The upwind roll must therefore fail about three-quarters of the time, while downwind rolls succeed nearly every tick.\n\nLimiting spread to the two frontier tips—downwind and upwind—gives a stable ratio of about 3.6. At a 0.25 per-tick upwind rate, the upwind front reaches roughly 120 meters while the downwind front reaches about 432 meters. The probability that no upwind cell ignites is vanishingly small, and the ratio stays tightly clustered around 3.6 with standard deviations near six cells.\n\nThe implementation will select the burning cell with the highest dot product as the downwind tip and the lowest as the upwind tip, then attempt to ignite one neighbor from each. In calm conditions, a random burning cell will also try a perpendicular neighbor to preserve roundness. The probability formula uses an exponential wind factor of 2.23 for downwind and 0.45 for upwind, yielding a downwind success rate of roughly 0.45 and an upwind rate of roughly 0.09.\n\nThe downwind-to-upwind ratio is now roughly 5.0, well above the required three-to-one. In calm conditions the fire will spread to a random cardinal neighbor from each burning cell once per tick, rotating directions to fill a disk; the test accepts any bounding-box aspect ratio between 0.5 and 2.0.\n\nA turn-based cardinal cycle from the frontier produces a diamond-shaped calm fire with aspect ratio near one. Road cells are blocked because try_ignite fails when fuel is zero, so the front halts and leaves the far side empty. Embers allow a four-cell downwind jump only when wind speed exceeds eight meters per second; at exactly eight speed no ember fires. Burnt cells remain blocked until regrowth after forty-eight hours. Rain clears all burning cells and marks them extinguished.\n\nThe per-tick spread algorithm yields roughly one hundred thirty cells total, not five hundred. A single Molotov or barrel blast covers only about twelve to fifty cells, so the five-hundred-cell target requires either a large radius or multiple simultaneous ignitions.\n\nA 16-meter fuel-tank explosion covers roughly 200 cells, so chaining three tanks yields about 600. The test will flag the fire once it reaches 500 cells. A 30-meter blast radius is unrealistic for a six-meter rocket, so the plan now includes a flamethrower stream that paints cells along a path to widen the fire. Five hundred cells span about 2000 square meters, or a 45-meter square.\n\nThe flamethrower sweeps a 14-meter cone each tick, igniting roughly forty cells per pass. If the bot turns and re-ignites new cells as it moves, the fire can reach five hundred cells in about ten seconds.\n\nA hacky workaround using a molotov pool and low-rate lateral spread was rejected. The chosen approach is a global cap of six new ignitions per tick, with one downwind, one upwind, and four lateral. This yields roughly 1440 cells in sixty seconds, satisfying the five-hundred-cell target. Wind-axis reach tests remain valid because the downwind and upwind picks still dominate.\n\nThe ignition cap is refined to one downwind and one upwind frontier cell per tick, with no lateral spread when wind speed is four meters per second or higher. Lateral cells can still become new tips if they project further downwind than the current tip, so they may slightly extend the front. The resulting cell count stays near one hundred thirty.\n\nA 14-meter grid of 20 fuel tanks will be placed in the field. Each 6-meter blast radius covers roughly 28 cells, so the total fire reaches about 560 cells once overlap is accounted for. A rocket strike at one tank triggers a chain reaction that ignites all nearby tanks, satisfying the five-hundred-cell goal without artificial spread.\n\nThe tank farm will use a 7×7 grid of forty-nine fuel tanks spaced ten meters apart. Each five-meter blast radius covers roughly twenty cells, so total unique cells should reach about seven hundred. The spread probability constant is set to 0.9 and the per-tick time step to 0.25.\n\nThe tip will burn out after about forty ticks, so it is expected to pass the cell before it expires. The fire will stall if the tip burns out without igniting a next cell. The calm-fire diamond will use a rotating cardinal neighbor from the centroid each tick, yielding roughly two hundred forty cells.\n\nA road at x=4 blocks the fire tip from reaching x=-4, leaving that side empty. At 8 m/s wind toward the road, the front takes about four seconds to stop. For the ember test, a 9 m/s wind gives each tick a 0.15 chance of igniting an eight-cell jump past the road so the far side burns within ten seconds.\n\nThe implementation will be split into fire.rs and campaign logic inside world.rs. Tests will be written after the code compiles. The goal plan checklist will only update once things pass. A notes/title.md file will also be created. Shell input handling will live in shell.rs and be called from the world tick loop.\n\nThe tick loop branches on screen state: menu and attract screens route through shell input, while playing runs the full combat tick. The attract demo advances its segment timer rather than simulating combat, so unit tests must avoid large time steps during that phase.\n\nThe attract demo will use a lightweight camera movement pose instead of a pure timer fake. The five-loops test runs the campaign world on the title screen with no input and dt=1 for nine seconds, then continues; saves_written stays zero. Dev sliders will be stored as an array of name-value-default-min-max tuples in Tune so reset copies defaults.\n\nThe Tune struct now holds eighteen tweakable values, including fire_spread, burn_time, and wind_strength. Left and right input will adjust the focused slider by step.\n\nThe Tune struct now includes post_reman_hours and player_speed. Weapon jamming uses a multiplier on the base rate, with tests expecting the default of one. Break threshold defaults to eight at three percent damage. The design is complete. Code will be written in chunks. A merc module will be added and inner modules made public. A lightweight hash of player state, yaw, health, gun condition, tick, RNG state, flags, and screen will fingerprint the world for determinism tests.\n\nWorld will derive `Deserialize` and carry `#[serde(default)]` for forward compatibility. A helper will gather every string the game displays to enforce the forbidden-name rule, with dialogue kept in a const array. The forbidden names are Ada Okoro, Tomek Wrona, Rafael Quintero, Mirela Dragan, Émile Tshala, Josephine Ruvuma, The Chandler, Thandiwe Moyo, Papa Lazare, and Mr.\n\nThe forbidden-name list now includes Desai, Sister Perpetua, Bakker, Kilometre 40, Republic of Kwelara, Council, People's Hand, Ombe Flats, Luvande, Ste-Odile, Hotel Splendide, Cement Works, Pump House, Ranger's Lodge, Boat Shed, and Kilo 38. No forbidden names are present.\n\nThe Game struct will derive Clone, Serialize, and Deserialize. A Merc wrapper will hold the World and a shown flag. Pending input does not need to be stored because the tick function receives it directly from the pre-tick input argument. Visual entity IDs will be skipped during serialization and repopulated in post-deserialization.\n\nVisuals will use Color::hex and an emissive field. The mod.rs post() function will set the player position to the world feet, ensure a ground entity exists, place trees in a ring around the player, and add a rifle box in front of the camera. EntityId is not Default, so Option<EntityId> will be used instead. When Merc is cloned for history, the entity IDs remain valid. During snapshot loading, serde skip causes them to be lost and respawned.\n\nThe game status JSON will carry camera state, screen, health, coverage, audio, goal, dev settings, and UI. The app will stringify this object for window.__game.status while keeping UI output separate from world.ui_json().\n\nI will start writing files now. I need to fix the camera and input code first so the workspace compiles cleanly. The tools.rs change must be done before any pav_tools build or full test. Pav_core does not depend on pav_view, so camera changes only affect the app build.\n\nThe campaign bot will use an explicit Goal enum. It covers Boot, RaiseMap, SetWaypoint, Reach with coordinates and optional flag, and Scripted actions such as Fever, Pill, Blackout, FightPost, EnemyJam, ClearJam, BreakWeapon, Rescue, Wound, Loot, Claim, Shop, Locker, Syrette, Sleep, Drive, Monocular, Ignite, TankFarm, Mounted, Panic, and Animals.\n\nThe campaign bot will use a single `campaign_tick` function driven by a `step: u32` counter and a large match. Setup runs when entering each step, and completion is checked with `step_done`. Helper functions `step_count` and `enter_step` will manage the sequence.\n\nThe campaign bot will use a `bot_for_step` function to generate input frames. Step indices will be defined as constants in a comment table so the sequence stays readable. A `goals` function will return a vector of goal structs, each carrying a name, kind, and budget. The bot logic and step entry handlers will match on these kinds. I am ready to write the file now, though I may split it if it grows too large. I also need to decide whether to implement custom serialization for types that contain string references.\n\nThe campaign bot will store only the route, step index, a ready flag, and elapsed step time. The goal list is regenerated from the route and step index at runtime, so no vector needs to be serialized. On load, the ready flag resets and the current step is re-entered via a spec function.\n\nA `script` function will build the step list from the route on every call. `Spec` is a `Copy` enum and does not need to be stored. The bot logic, entry setup, and completion checks will each use a large match over the spec variants.\n\nWorld will expose a `goto` method for the bot to walk to coordinates, with teleportation reserved only for bus, blackout, and rescue drag mechanics. Arrival is considered done when the player is within eight meters of the target. The merc.rs test file will be lengthy.\n\nIntegration tests will live in `engine/crates/pav_core/tests/merc.rs` and must use only public API. I will export `World`, `MenuInput`, `Device`, `loopback`, `jam_probability`, and the `Gun` methods, plus make the inner modules public so the test file can import them.\n\nWorld will expose `gun()` and `gun_mut()` accessors. The companion struct tracks name, alive, down, down_t, rescue_spent, and trust. Three companions are planned; the player merc defaults to Ada. Files will be written in parallel via the tool.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The engine hook is clear. I'll add the merc simulation and the small input and camera fields it needs, then compile."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b973509f-d35c-496a-8897-c32bdb6e8e8f-49","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/notes/title.md","new_string":"# Title roll\n\nProcess: 50 playful riffs on the source title, then one index from a cryptographic RNG (`secrets.randbelow`, seeded by `os.urandom`). The game on screen uses only the chosen title.\n\n## RNG\n\n```\nINDEX 46\nTITLE Jam Cry\n```\n\n`secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 46.\n\n## The fifty, in order\n\n00 Near Cry\n01 Far Try\n02 Far Fry\n03 Bar Cry\n04 Far Sly\n05 Far Dry\n06 Par Cry\n07 Far Pry\n08 Jar Cry\n09 Far Shy\n10 Mar Cry\n11 Far Fly\n12 Star Cry\n13 Scar Cry\n14 Far Pie\n15 Far Lie\n16 Char Cry\n17 Far High\n18 Far Buy\n19 Afar Cry\n20 Fair Cry\n21 Fare Cry\n22 Far-Fetched Cry\n23 So Far a Cry\n24 Cry Farther\n25 The Farther Sigh\n26 Two Cries Out\n27 A Cry Too Near\n28 Far from a Cry\n29 Not That Far a Cry\n30 Far, Then Cry\n31 Cry at Range\n32 Distant Cry\n33 Yonder Cry\n34 Far Grumble\n35 Remote Howl\n36 The Second Howl\n37 Far Weep Two\n38 Dry Far\n39 Rust Far\n40 Far Rust\n41 Fever Far\n42 Far Fever\n43 Grass Cry\n44 Laterite Cry\n45 Wind Cry\n46 Jam Cry\n47 Diamond Cry\n48 Ledger Cry\n49 The Hired Howl\n\nSource of the riff: Far Cry 2. That phrase is the inspiration for the list, not a name used in the fiction.\n","old_string":""}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b973509f-d35c-496a-8897-c32bdb6e8e8f-50","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/input.rs","old_string":"    pub const DODGE: u32 = 1 << 13;\n    pub const POTION: u32 = 1 << 14;\n\n    pub const NAMES: &[(&str, u32)] = &[\n        (\"jump\", JUMP),\n        (\"crouch\", CROUCH),\n        (\"interact\", INTERACT),\n        (\"use\", USE),\n        (\"focus\", FOCUS),\n        (\"sprint\", SPRINT),\n        (\"primary\", PRIMARY),\n        (\"secondary\", SECONDARY),\n        (\"crawl\", CRAWL),\n        (\"skill3\", SKILL3),\n        (\"skill4\", SKILL4),\n        (\"skill5\", SKILL5),\n        (\"skill6\", SKILL6),\n        (\"dodge\", DODGE),\n        (\"potion\", POTION),\n    ];","new_string":"    pub const DODGE: u32 = 1 << 13;\n    pub const POTION: u32 = 1 << 14;\n    /// Jam Cry: reload / clear a jam, next weapon, throw, melee, map, monocular, heal, pills, tracker, confirm.\n    pub const RELOAD: u32 = 1 << 15;\n    pub const NEXT: u32 = 1 << 16;\n    pub const THROW: u32 = 1 << 17;\n    pub const MELEE: u32 = 1 << 18;\n    pub const MAP: u32 = 1 << 19;\n    pub const MONOCULAR: u32 = 1 << 20;\n    pub const HEAL: u32 = 1 << 21;\n    pub const PILLS: u32 = 1 << 22;\n    pub const TRACKER: u32 = 1 << 23;\n    pub const CONFIRM: u32 = 1 << 24;\n\n    pub const NAMES: &[(&str, u32)] = &[\n        (\"jump\", JUMP),\n        (\"crouch\", CROUCH),\n        (\"interact\", INTERACT),\n        (\"use\", USE),\n        (\"focus\", FOCUS),\n        (\"sprint\", SPRINT),\n        (\"primary\", PRIMARY),\n        (\"secondary\", SECONDARY),\n        (\"crawl\", CRAWL),\n        (\"skill3\", SKILL3),\n        (\"skill4\", SKILL4),\n        (\"skill5\", SKILL5),\n        (\"skill6\", SKILL6),\n        (\"dodge\", DODGE),\n        (\"potion\", POTION),\n        (\"reload\", RELOAD),\n        (\"next\", NEXT),\n        (\"throw\", THROW),\n        (\"melee\", MELEE),\n        (\"map\", MAP),\n        (\"monocular\", MONOCULAR),\n        (\"heal\", HEAL),\n        (\"pills\", PILLS),\n        (\"tracker\", TRACKER),\n        (\"confirm\", CONFIRM),\n    ];"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b973509f-d35c-496a-8897-c32bdb6e8e8f-51","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/input.rs","old_string":"    /// A menu action this tick (Shardfall: equip, sell, travel...), queued one per tick.\n    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]\n    pub cmd: Option<crate::arpg::GameCmd>,\n}","new_string":"    /// A menu action this tick (Shardfall: equip, sell, travel...), queued one per tick.\n    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]\n    pub cmd: Option<crate::arpg::GameCmd>,\n    /// Radians of look since the previous tick (x = yaw, y = pitch). Summed and cleared like `pressed`.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub look: Vec2,\n    /// Analog vehicle axes: x steer, y throttle (positive) / brake (negative).\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub axes: Vec2,\n    /// Encoded menu or world action for replays and the bot (Jam Cry). 0 means none.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub act: u32,\n}"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b973509f-d35c-496a-8897-c32bdb6e8e8f-52","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_tools/src/tools.rs","old_string":"        let f = pav_core::InputFrame {\n            move_dir: dir.clamp_length_max(1.0),\n            vertical: 0.0,\n            aim,\n            held: held | if i == 0 { press } else { 0 },\n            pressed: if i == 0 { press } else { 0 },\n            cmd: None,\n        };","new_string":"        let f = pav_core::InputFrame {\n            move_dir: dir.clamp_length_max(1.0),\n            aim,\n            held: held | if i == 0 { press } else { 0 },\n            pressed: if i == 0 { press } else { 0 },\n 