{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_71","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"View keys are separate from parameters.\n\nParsing accepts arbitrary view keys as ParamValue. Writing all source now. The campaign script will be compact. A potential bug was noted: the boot step bot sends confirm every frame, while Title apply_menu only confirms once per tick.\n\nConfirmed the tick order: shell runs before play, so boot and gameplay never overlap. The 4th confirm advances the step once the screen reaches Playing. Pausing during play stops time.\n\nThe boot bot never pauses. During title playthroughs, the attract test ticks idle input without bot commands, so idle time accumulates. The boot budget is only eight seconds, which could be exceeded if the attract test runs for eight hundred seconds. The watchdog lives in the playthrough test rather than in tick.\n\nadvance_goal will not auto-skip on budget. step_t simply grows. Entering attract does not reset anything that writes a save.\n\nThe shell function will advance the attract timer and ignore menu confirmations while the screen is Attract. Idle logic remains inside shell. Touch-only mode keeps the notice visible above attract.\n\nThe shell function now transitions to Attract after ten seconds of idle time unless the hub is active. The playthrough bot confirms before that idle threshold is reached.\n\nIn touch-only mode, the title screen keeps its rows and a notice string in the UI JSON. Virtual sticks are omitted because touch controls are disabled. The settings button remains tappable while attract runs behind the title.\n\nA `set_touch_only` method will be added to World. The code will be split into `world.rs` for state and logic, and `campaign.rs` for the script.\n\nRust module privacy prevents a sibling module from accessing a parent struct's private fields. The campaign script cannot read World's internals without changing visibility. All World fields will be public so the campaign code can access them directly.\n\nFireMap methods will remain on the FireMap struct to avoid a circular dependency with World. The fire system uses a tip algorithm and a FireCell struct tracking burning state and life.\n\nFireCell now tracks `burnt_until` as game minutes for cells that are no longer burning. The fire system is sparse: only burning and burnt cells live in the map, while unburnt cells are absent. Regrow removes burnt cells once their burn time expires. Rain extinguishes burning cells or resets them to unburnt. The `ignite_cell` method checks that a cell is not already burning and that it has not been recently burnt before igniting.\n\nFireMap will spread every 0.25 seconds. Fuel is fetched from geom::fuel inside fire.rs. Burned cells still count toward the 500-cell threshold, tracked by a total ignited counter.\n\nA note about terrain mesh simplification will be added to deviations later. The bot movement code is being drafted: it computes a desired yaw from the player's position and clamps the look input to keep the bot facing its target.\n\nThe bot movement code now includes forward thrust and focus activation when stamina is high. Yaw calculations were verified against compass directions. A `bot_drive` helper is being drafted for vehicle steering.\n\nThe bot's `bot_drive` helper steers toward a target using yaw error clamped to the steering axis. Vehicle physics in tick now updates yaw from input axes.x and speed from axes.y, with maximum speed reduced by smoke stage and zeroed for boats on shallow water.\n\nVehicle physics now updates position from yaw and speed, computes pitch from terrain height differences, and recovers roll by damping it each tick. The player position follows the vehicle.\n\nVehicle damage stages will be triggered by health loss and update the stage threshold. An OnFire fuse lasts eight seconds before explosion and ignition. Repair requires the player to hold interact within four units of the vehicle for six seconds to restore seventy health and reach steam or ok stage. NPC drivers will follow a fixed road route, with tests verifying movement along the north-south axis.\n\nUSE cycles the player through driver, gunner, and exit seats. The gunner seat fires primary weapons when mounted. A `moved_vehicle` distance accumulator will trigger the `technical_driven` flag after twenty units of travel. The shell module will expose a `fiction_lines()` scanner for campaign text. One Far Cry 2 reference line is permitted in the source.\n\nThe about text will use honest preliminary feedback rather than placeholder issue counts. The hand-in must be accurate after the test run. Forbidden words include TODO, TBD, placeholder, and lorem.\n\nThe campaign script will be kept short while remaining correct. A `net.rs` module is being drafted with a `Packet` struct and a `Link` struct that simulates network latency, jitter, loss, and per-second bandwidth tracking.\n\nThe `Link::send` method drops unreliable packets based on loss probability, adds jitter to the delivery time, and queues the packet. A `poll` method will return received messages. A loopback fire test will simulate two worlds over 150 ms latency to verify that a host-ignited fire cell reaches the client after one second. This test function is also used by the hub round.\n\nFire sync will use a small JSON message with cell coordinates. The host sends five updates per second, which fits the twenty-per-second limit. Fire events are reliable so they arrive after latency. Kill events, objective changes, and finish flags will also be duplicated across the network. A simple audio synthesizer is being drafted.\n\nThe audio synthesizer now generates a two-tone pluck with a repeating envelope. It samples sine waves at 220 Hz and 330 Hz, applies a slow envelope, and clamps the output to ±1.0. The RMS amplitude is checked against the gain parameter.\n\nA `render_gun` function will generate weapon sounds from burst frequencies. A `render_voice` function will synthesize speech by mapping speaker bytes to pitch and syllable characters to rhythm. A `render_crackle` function will produce noise scaled with the square root of cell count. These audio helpers will be wired into the game status so the shipped build can call them directly.\n\nThe game will call the audio synthesizer during World::tick for live events. Rendering full buffers on every shot is too expensive, so the test invokes the library function directly to avoid test theater.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cd707631-1c36-43d8-800f-8197408233f2-60","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"pub fn play|pub struct Mixer|pub fn new","glob":"**/pav_audio/src/lib.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":984,"output_tokens":4376,"cache_read_input_tokens":146816,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"b1dd6f21-d3e5-4872-87ab-6a5493b66f58"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-cd707631-1c36-43d8-800f-8197408233f2-60","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,53,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,47,74,97,109,32,67,114,121,45,70,97,114,32,67,114,121,32,50,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,99,114,97,116,101,115,47,112,97,118,95,97,117,100,105,111,47,115,114,99,47,108,105,98,46,114,115,10,49,51,54,58,112,117,98,32,115,116,114,117,99,116,32,77,105,120,101,114,32,123,10,49,52,54,58,32,32,32,32,112,117,98,32,102,110,32,110,101,119,40,115,97,109,112,108,101,95,114,97,116,101,58,32,102,51,50,41,32,45,62,32,83,101,108,102,32,123,10,49,53,49,58,32,32,32,32,112,117,98,32,102,110,32,112,108,97,121,40,38,109,117,116,32,115,101,108,102,44,32,112,58,32,80,97,116,99,104,44,32,112,97,110,58,32,102,51,50,44,32,103,97,105,110,58,32,102,51,50,44,32,100,101,108,97,121,58,32,102,51,50,41,32,123,10,53,49,55,58,112,117,98,32,102,110,32,112,108,97,121,95,101,118,101,110,116,40,109,105,120,101,114,58,32,38,109,117,116,32,77,105,120,101,114,44,32,101,118,58,32,38,83,105,109,69,118,101,110,116,44,32,108,58,32,38,76,105,115,116,101,110,101,114,44,32,115,102,120,58,32,102,51,50,41,32,123,10,54,49,53,58,32,32,32,32,112,117,98,32,102,110,32,112,108,97,121,95,101,118,101,110,116,40,38,115,101,108,102,44,32,101,118,58,32,38,83,105,109,69,118,101,110,116,44,32,108,58,32,38,76,105,115,116,101,110,101,114,44,32,115,102,120,58,32,102,51,50,41,32,123,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":5,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_audio/src/lib.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":136,\"content\":\"pub struct Mixer {\"},{\"line_number\":146,\"content\":\"    pub fn new(sample_rate: f32) -> Self {\"},{\"line_number\":151,\"content\":\"    pub fn play(&mut self, p: Patch, pan: f32, gain: f32, delay: f32) {\"},{\"line_number\":517,\"content\":\"pub fn play_event(mixer: &mut Mixer, ev: &SimEvent, l: &Listener, sfx: f32) {\"},{\"line_number\":615,\"content\":\"    pub fn play_event(&self, ev: &SimEvent, l: &Listener, sfx: f32) {\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"fc375763-4e8d-4542-88b1-ee11bae86b6b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_72","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Mixer::play and render are public.\n\nI will write geom.rs, gun.rs, fire.rs, shell.rs, net.rs, world.rs, mod.rs, toml, audio append, games/mod.rs, and tests. World.rs will be kept under 1500 lines. I am also planning an efficient playthrough test with instant setup and a lightweight bot.\n\nThe bot will aim at enemy heads. It calculates yaw and pitch errors from distance and clamps look input. It only fires when both angles are within 0.15 radians.\n\nThe bot fires on the first frame it locks onto an enemy. Enemy jamming is modeled with 600 RPM and condition 5, giving a roughly 19% jam chance per shot. Enemies spawn in combat state with full ammo and aim at the player.\n\nEnemy jamming should occur in about half a second, well within the four-second budget. The AI will call pull_trigger during its update loop.\n\nFor the weapon break test, the player bot will hold fire and reload when jammed. The test gun will use an SMG with 800 RPM and condition 5, with wear set to zero so condition stays fixed. enter() will initialize the gun with full ammo and reserves.\n\nThe weapon break test is flaky. At condition 5, a jam clears in about 2.4 seconds and occurs roughly every 2.8 seconds. Over 40 seconds, that yields about 70 shots with only a 12% chance of breaking. Three routes gives a 99.8% chance the union succeeds, but any single route can stall on the break step and fail its watchdog before later flags.\n\nThe break test budget is raised to 90 seconds. The bot will second-tap reload during the jam step to shorten the clear time by roughly 40 percent, cutting the jam cycle to about 1.8 seconds. This yields roughly 250 shots over the full budget and a failure probability of only 0.05 percent per route.\n\nThe break step will use an SMG at 800 RPM. The jam step will call `force_jam` to bypass randomness and guarantee a jam on the first pull. The clear step will wait deterministically until the gun is no longer jammed. If any test route stalls, it will print the goal name so I can fix it. I am also considering adding a `fast` mode.\n\nReal delta-time will be kept. The companion death step uses a 50-second deterministic timer. The ending step crouches the player for cover, gives enemies very low accuracy and damage 2, and adds regen plus syrettes so the player survives; enemies will not kill. This requires roughly 10,800 iterations over 180 seconds. A Python generator is being considered.\n\nI will write the tests in Rust rather than Python. A single `cargo test` run will validate everything. I will add a `run.sh` script later. File writes will proceed in parallel, and world.rs will be kept concise to avoid length limits. The `script` function uses `Route::Human` for player-driven campaigns that include runtime choices; bot routes such as Burn, Read, and Chair skip menus and follow fixed branches. Human boot still includes the title flow, and dev and browser tests rely on Human.\n\nThe Human route will use the Burn script as its default story to keep the campaign completable. At the B4 choice screen, the game will either wait for player input or auto-select a preset route.\n\nThe world will store a difficulty setting defaulting to Normal. Easy and hard modes apply 0.7 and 1.3 multipliers to enemy damage and wear. Bots select Normal because the focus starts at one. The title screen hides Continue when no save exists and defaults to New contract.\n\nI will add a `has_save` helper and expose test coverage flags through a `coverage` method. A deterministic `fingerprint` hash will be computed from the JSON-serialized state using FNV-1a, which works because serde handles the RNG. I am now ready to begin writing the files.\n\nThe `idle` step is kept because it affects the attract mode. No fields will be excluded from serialization. NaN is avoided for f32 values. BTreeMap tuple keys serialize safely as JSON arrays. The World struct will use `#[serde(default)]` and implement Default for nested types so new fields do not break existing save files.\n\nI will not use `#[serde(default)]` on the World struct. Instead, I will write full JSON and let loading replace the entire world state. This avoids recursion in `Default` and keeps saves complete. I am adding `save_json` and `load_json` methods for the companion death test. The struct will also store posts timer, wind, fever, diamonds, infamy, and inventory so they persist.\n\nThe World struct will store `fever_state`, `fever_attack`, and `pills`. Fever triggers after 15 minutes of play. The unit test will set the hidden timer near the warning threshold to tick through the 40-second warning phase before the attack begins, using roughly 2400 ticks.\n\nThe blackout test will run the full warning and attack sequence, requiring roughly 6000 ticks. The fever state machine advances through three phases: a hidden timer triggers the warning after 15 minutes of play, a 40-second warning period follows, and a 60-second attack phase begins. During the attack, holding the pill action for 2.5 seconds ends the fever early; otherwise the attack expires into a blackout.\n\nBlackout teleports the player to the nearest safe house, restores 50 HP, deducts 20% diamonds, resets fever state and timer, flags the event, and adds six minutes. A god mode flag will skip damage for dev builds; the bot will not enable it. The test file will be written after compiling a stub.\n\nTests will be written alongside the code so a single compile validates everything. I will name tests clearly and use cargo test filters. The playthrough test may take a while to compile, but that is acceptable. I also need to update games/mod.rs and write a small merc.toml for the layout legend.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-6fb5e0ca-611b-4326-9e3a-a6d5cf411870-61","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"enum ParamValue","glob":"**/params.rs","-A":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4721,"output_tokens":3362,"cache_read_input_tokens":147712,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"a92a3277-3a74-476b-bdcf-7e81d5ae99a4"}
