97,109,86,97,108,117,101,58,58,66,111,111,108,40,98,41,32,61,62,32,83,111,109,101,40,42,98,41,44,10,51,48,45,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,80,97,114,97,109,86,97,108,117,101,58,58,73,110,116,40,105,41,32,61,62,32,83,111,109,101,40,42,105,32,33,61,32,48,41,44,10,51,49,45,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,80,97,114,97,109,86,97,108,117,101,58,58,70,108,111,97,116,40,102,41,32,61,62,32,83,111,109,101,40,42,102,32,33,61,32,48,46,48,41,44,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":2,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/pavilion-lite/src/params.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":10,\"content\":\"pub enum ParamValue {\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/params.rs\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":11,\"content\":\"pub enum ParamValue {\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"a90f6e1c-7643-4c5a-adfa-0dbeebb47dc9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_73","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The View type is a BTreeMap<String, ParamValue>.\n\nTOML parsing of RoomDef correctly maps integers and floats. The struct uses known fields only. I will now write the source files, including world.rs and a flag helper macro. Campaign flags are set immediately on entry, so done returns true in the same tick.\n\nThe `begin_step` function marks flags and sets the step ready flag. `step_done` returns true immediately for flags. The `advance` loop will skip instant steps to avoid wasting ticks.\n\nThe `advance` loop caps at eight steps per tick. It begins the current step if not ready, then advances immediately if done, otherwise breaks. `advance` runs once at construction to start boot and again at the end of each tick. During reach steps, the player's origin is stored on entry. If the player moves more than 25 units from that origin while a waypoint is set and the GPS flag is unset, the GPS flag is marked as followed.\n\nThe MAP step requires holding the action for 0.4 seconds. The bot will press CONFIRM to set the waypoint at the town gate.\n\nThe waypoint step will set the target to (0, 460) and mark \"waypoint_set\" when the bot presses CONFIRM or act 1. The bot will use a `bot_input` helper to send the correct input per spec. During the fight step, the bot will aim at the nearest living soldier.\n\nSoldiers spawn as kind Soldier. The fight step ends when all soldiers die, marking post_cleared and scheduling post_reman_at for 24 hours later. Scripted NPCs are despawned at the start of each step that spawns them to prevent accumulation. Sandbox unit tests use Dummy bodies that remain in the world since they never run steps.\n\nWorld::fresh initializes Sandbox mode with no script, so the world never advances. World::campaign sets the route and screen to Title at step zero.\n\nThe Human route will use the Burn script with a choice at step B4. A new `Spec::EndingChoice` step will let human players pick an ending at the end. Preset routes skip this menu.\n\nThe delivered game uses Sim::new for the Human route. A dev story jump tool will let tests skip directly to a contract. Unit tests can use this for failure conditions, but the full playthrough must not. I will add tests covering six failure paths: buying with zero diamonds, companion timer expiry, blackout, vehicle explosion, leaving a civilian in town untouched, and jam.\n\nWeapon break is added to the failure conditions. Post-remnant checks will skip while the player looks. The test list is complete. I am writing world.rs in two parts. Sandbox mode returns an empty script, which advance safely handles. Campaign and fresh worlds both start with 40 diamonds, 3 syrettes, and 3 pills. Fresh mode begins with a pistol and an AR; campaign starts with only a pistol, and loot or shop purchases add more weapons. Campaign ammo is sufficient.\n\nGuns will use a catalog function to return stats like RPM, damage, wear, noise, magazine size, and range. Each shot subtracts wear from the weapon condition. The machete is implemented as a melee button; stealth kills require the player to be behind an enemy. Soldiers spawn facing away so they can be snuck up on. The dart weapon applies a three-second poison timer before killing the target, and it makes noise eight.\n\nEnemies will be placed twenty meters away so the bot can fire its eight-meter-range dart without alerting them. The poison flag is set when the target dies from poison. I am now writing the source files and registering the Merc game in games/mod.rs with key \"merc\". The level TOML path is games/merc.toml.\n\nThe module will be a directory module at `games/merc/mod.rs` with `geom.rs` inside. The TOML file sits beside it as `games/merc.toml`. The module loads the level data with `include_str!(\"../merc.toml\")`.\n\nAudio functions will be added. A `rows_now` helper will compute the current screen's row count, and a test will verify that no screen exceeds seven rows. Settings tabs are limited to four tabs, each with at most seven items.\n\nThe settings menu splits into four tabs: Sound, Controls, Picture, and Access. The dev menu has five pages: Cheats, Teleport, Spawn, World, and Balance. Cheats will be paginated in groups of seven toggles. Teleport options will also be grouped by sevens.\n\nA test-only `set_ui_page` helper will let tests walk every menu page and assert that `rows_now` never exceeds seven rows. The same check will also run during normal input navigation.\n\nMenu rows will be sliced into fixed windows of seven to guarantee the row limit. Focus wraps around vertically, and hover sets focus while click confirms the selection. A glyph test is planned. The Device enum is being defined.\n\nThe `Device` enum distinguishes keyboard and gamepad. A `glyph` helper maps each action to a display string, using Enter for confirm, LMB for fire, RMB for aim, R for reload, M for map, and controller buttons like Cross or A.\n\nThe glyph helper now covers all remaining gamepad mappings, including reload, fire, aim, and map. The world stores an xbox toggle that tests will verify by checking glyphs before and after switching it. I am ready to write the source files and may add a camera unit test in pav_view.\n\nThe first-person camera test will live in `pav_view/src/camera.rs` as a `#[cfg(test)]` module. `pav_core` does not depend on `pav_view`, so the integration test cannot use it.\n\nCampaign logic will live in `world.rs` rather than a separate file, since all fields are public and it keeps the code in one compile unit. The camera test will be placed at the end of `camera.rs`. World tests will verify look clamps and stick movement. I am batching all source writes now.\n\nThe source files will be written in this order: geom, gun, fire, net, shell, world, mod, and tests. Tests will need public access to world methods such as fresh, campaign, tick, bot_input, position and rotation fields, gun and fire state, spawn helpers, menu input, detection exposure, jam probability, vehicle fields, and save or load logic. The exposure function will be placed in geom.rs.\n\nThe `exposure` helper multiplies posture, grass, light, and motion factors. A test asserts the open visibility is over eight times higher than the hidden value. Distance falloff is handled separately. NPCs accumulate a see meter using exposure multiplied by falloff and delta time, with an alert threshold of 1.\n\nThe NPC update logic will live in `world::sense()`. A `cell_at` helper maps world coordinates to grid cells using `div_euclid` so negative positions round correctly, with cell centers at odd offsets. I am now writing the source files.\n\nShell and world.rs will be written together in one turn so the shell module compiles against the World struct. The World struct draft includes seed, rng, route, screen, focus, tab, page, difficulty, merc, and x.\n\nThe World struct draft now includes position, rotation, eye height, stance, stamina, health, inventory counts, active weapon slot, syrette and pill timers, wound severity, fever state and warnings, plus collections for bodies, walls, and fire.\n\nThe World struct draft now includes weather, time, ceasefire, companions, vehicle, flags, step counters, waypoints, map and mono timers, post-clearance state, save metadata, and idle or attract timers.\n\nThe World struct draft now includes attract timers, device and xbox state, music volume, finished flag, outcome ending, shot counters, tracer index, and god mode. The Dev struct holds cheat toggles for god mode, infinite ammo, no jams, no fever, noclip, invisibility, and AI visibility.\n\nThe Dev struct now includes toggles for fire, nav, and stats visibility, plus an open flag. The dev menu cannot open while the hub is active. A const slider-name list will keep shell and reset in sync with the 21 Tune fields. The Tune struct will expose indexed getters, setters, names, and ranges. Reset copies tune0, which was captured at construction, so mutating it afterward would corrupt the default values.\n\nThe `jam_chance` and `player_speed` sliders will be wired into the jam probability and movement formulas. The `fire_spread` slider replaces the hardcoded `FIRE_SPREAD` constant, defaulting to 0.9. A `wind_strength` slider will multiply wind speed for spread, defaulting to 1.\n\nA `FireParams` struct will carry fire settings into `FireMap::tick`. A unit test for jam probability will live in `tests/merc.rs` and call `Gun::pull` directly with an external RNG, avoiding a full world setup.\n\nWorld tick passes `tune.jam_chance`, `tune.weapon_wear`, and `tune.break_threshold` into `gun.pull`. A 5000-iteration jam test asserts the rate stays between 1/30 and 1/12, matching the expected ~5.6% chance. The test calls `Gun::pull` directly to verify the same logic used in the world loop.\n\nA test will verify that a jammed gun does not consume ammo during the world tick. Another test will confirm that guns with condition above seventy never jam. The camera unit test and games/mod.rs registration will be appended after the core source files.\n\nShell methods will be implemented directly inside world.rs to avoid a circular module dependency. world.rs tick can call self.shell_tick() without any cross-module import.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The camera and input hooks are in. Next I’ll add the merc simulation itself: weapons, fire, the country, and the campaign."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0e3dacc4-b8f7-4a74-b6c0-a7005ad3b6a6-62","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/merc.toml","new_string":"# Jam Cry — a small visual room. The country itself is the merc simulation, not this mesh.\nname = \"Jam Cry\"\nabout = \"Last dry season. The grass burns anyway.\"\nentrance = { at = [4, 4], facing = \"north\" }\n\n[camera]\ntilt = 12.0\ndistance = 6.0\nfov = 75.0\n\n[view]\nsky = \"#f0a060\"\n\"light.sun_elevation\" = 14.0\n\"light.sun_intensity\" = 1.1\n\"light.ambient\" = 0.55\n\"light.shadow_radius\" = 120.0\nhaze = 0.35\nhaze_height = 12.0\nshafts = 1.2\nbloom = 0.5\nbloom_threshold = 1.0\nfog = false\nsaturation = 0.9\n\n[layout]\norigin = [-4.0, 0.0, -4.0]\n\n[[layout.layer]]\ny = 0\nmap = '''\n........\n........\n........\n...P....\n........\n........\n........\n........\n'''\n\n[layout.legend.'.']\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.4, y1 = 0.0, color = \"#c9a24b\" }]\n\n[layout.legend.'P']\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.4, y1 = 0.0, color = \"#c9a24b\" }]\nmarker = \"player\"\n","old_string":""}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0e3dacc4-b8f7-4a74-b6c0-a7005ad3b6a6-63","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs","old_string":"pub mod starter;\n\n/// Every game the engine knows. `load scene=<key>` (or `<key>/<rest>`) builds it.\npub static GAMES: &[Entry] =\n    &[entry::<starter::Starter>(\"Starter: collect every shard while slimes chase you (the template to copy).\", starter::build)];","new_string":"pub mod merc;\npub mod starter;\n\n/// Every game the engine knows. `load scene=<key>` (or `<key>/<rest>`) builds it.\npub static GAMES: &[Entry] = &[\n    entry::<starter::Starter>(\"Starter: collect every shard while slimes chase you (the template to copy).\", starter::build),\n    entry::<merc::Merc>(\"Jam Cry: a contract in the last dry season.\", merc::build),\n];"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-0e3dacc4-b8f7-4a74-b6c0-a7005ad3b6a6-64","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_audio/src/lib.rs","old_string":"#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {","new_string":"/// Default music bus gain. Measured by `render_music`: RMS(gain) / RMS(1) equals the gain.\npub const MUSIC_GAIN: f32 = 0.10;\n\n/// Sparse two-voice figure. Amplitude stays at 0.3 at full gain so the bus does not clip.\npub fn render_music(gain: f32, seconds: f32, sample_rate: f32) -> Vec<f32> {\n    render_tone(220.0, 330.0, 0.3 * gain.max(0.0), seconds, sample_rate, 2.0)\n}\n\n/// One gunshot family. The families differ in pitch and decay so a test can tell them apart.\npub fn gun_patch(family: &str) -> Patch {\n    let (wave, freq, decay, volume) = match family {\n        \"pistol\" => (Wave::Square, 180.0, 0.08, 0.4),\n        \"rifle\" => (Wave::Saw, 140.0, 0.12, 0.45),\n        \"shotgun\" => (Wave::Noise, 90.0, 0.18, 0.5),\n        \"sniper\" => (Wave::Triangle, 70.0, 0.22, 0.45),\n        \"launcher\" => (Wave::Sine, 48.0, 0.35, 0.5),\n        \"flame\" => (Wave::Noise, 240.0, 0.4, 0.28),\n        _ => (Wave::Square, 160.0, 0.1, 0.4),\n    };\n    let mut p = Patch::new(wave, freq);\n    p.freq_end = freq * 0.45;\n    p.glide = decay;\n    p.decay = decay;\n    p.volume = volume;\n    p.cutoff = 1800.0;\n    p.cutoff_end = 400.0;\n    p\n}\n\npub fn render_patch(patch: Patch, seconds: f32, sample_rate: f32) -> Vec<f32> {\n    let mut mixer = Mixer::new(sample_rate);\n    mixer.play(patch, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0);\n    let n = (seconds * sample_rate).max(1.0) as usize;\n    let mut out = vec![0.0; n * 2];\n    mixer.render(&mut out);\n    out\n}\n\n/// Formant gibberish. Pitch follows the speaker; syllables follow the line.\npub fn render_voice(speaker: &str, line: &str, sample_rate: f32) -> Vec<f32> {\n    let mut pitch = 140.0;\n    for b in speaker.bytes() {\n        pitch += (b as f32) * 0.73;\n    }\n    pitch = pitch % 180.0 + 90.0;\n    let seconds = (line.chars().count().max(1) as f32) * 0.045;\n    render_tone(pitch, pitch * 1.5, 0.22, seconds.max(0.12), sample_rate, 8.0)\n}\n\n/// Crackle whose energy rises with the number of burning cells.\npub fn render_crackle(cells: u32, sample_rate: f32) -> Vec<f32> {\n    let amp = (0.02 + (cells as f32).sqrt() * 0.01).min(0.4);\n    let mut p = Patch::new(Wave::Noise, 800.0);\n    p.decay = 0.4;\n    p.sustain = 0.8;\n    p.hold = 0.3;\n    p.volume = amp;\n    p.cutoff = 2000.0;\n    render_patch(p, 0.5, sample_rate)\n}\n\nfn render_tone(f0: f32, f1: f32, amp: f32, seconds: f32, sample_rate: f32, rate: f32) -> Vec<f32> {\n    let n = (seconds * sample_rate).max(1.0) as usize;\n    let mut out = vec![0.0; n * 2];\n    let tau = std::f32::consts::TAU;\n    for i in 0..n {\n        let t = i as f32 / sample_rate;\n        let env = (1.0 - (t * rate).fract()).clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n        let s = (t * f0 * tau).sin() * 0.62 + (t * f1 * tau).sin() * 0.38;\n        let v = (s * env * amp).clamp(-0.95, 0.95);\n        out[i * 2] = v;\n        out[i * 2 + 1] = v;\n    }\n    out\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4189,"output_tokens":7825,"cache_read_input_tokens":152320,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"ba3bf716-0fdf-44d8-be79-19f4b041ec08"}
