{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_116","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"FireMap is re-exported from merc, but its tick and ignite methods are public.\n\nThe geom module is private, so tests cannot access its helper functions. I will make it public and expose the needed helpers directly.\n\nAll submodules will be made public. World fields are already public, so tests can set up scenarios directly. Tests must drive real mechanics like position and tick, never fake completion flags. Playthrough tests only need bot_input and tick.\n\nI am implementing the `water_ahead` helper to find the nearest point on the river polyline to a given position. I will also fix the boat's `ceasefire` logic before writing tests.\n\nThe `water_ahead` helper now walks along the river polyline from the nearest point to find a position at a given distance ahead.\n\nThe `water_ahead` helper now handles cases where the forward path runs short by walking backward along the river. The boat bot will target a point 40 meters ahead of its current position using this helper.\n\nSteering toward the 40-meter-ahead point should keep the boat near the centerline and allow it to reach the 15-meter target within a few seconds. The ceasefire fix will clear the flag and mark \"ceasefire_ended\" when a body dies during the Ceasefire step.\n\nDie places the companion 40 meters away and sets down_t to zero. The companion dies after 45 seconds regardless of player proximity. Read ending enemies fire rarely and deal minimal damage over 180 seconds. Regeneration at eight points per second should offset that damage.\n\nThe ending enemy damage will not be reduced unless the test fails. The jam campaign forces a jam on the first pull, giving the bot about 1.2 seconds to clear it before reloading. The fight at z-12 uses four soldiers placed in a spread pattern; the bot aims at the first alive target and kills them one by one. At 12 meters with full condition, the pistol spread is only 0.06 meters, so hits should connect and the 25-second budget should suffice.\n\nSoldiers in the jammy campaign only need to be jammed, not killed. They pull every tick with a cooldown, and the jam probability is roughly nineteen percent per shot, so the twenty-five-second budget is more than enough. The soldier update correctly stops firing when it is jammed. The `current()` method is available inside `shots_tick`. Audio tests will live in the separate `pav_audio` crate.\n\nAudio tests will be added to the existing `pav_audio` tests module. A test in `merc` will verify that no WAV files exist in the game tree, using `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` to locate the crate directory.\n\nThe audio test will walk the engine root for WAV, MP3, and OGG files. A forbidden-names check against the summary list is planned. A unit test for `fp_basis` will be added to `pav_view/camera.rs`. The playthrough test will print its goal on failure.\n\nThe playthrough test boots a campaign world and runs it for up to eight hundred seconds. It feeds bot inputs into the world each tick, tracks the current goal name and elapsed time, and breaks early if the world finishes.\n\nThe playthrough test will panic if the bot stays on the same goal for more than one second past its budget. I noticed that reading `goal_budget` after `tick` could miss a goal change mid-tick, so I will snapshot the goal name before each tick and reset the stuck timer if it changes.\n\nThe boot sequence is confirmed to finish within four ticks. The bot sends a confirm every tick, so it never idles on the title screen and the attract mode stays out of reach.\n\nThe test file will be a single long module. MenuInput is public, so hover tests can use it directly. A glyph test will verify that the character map contains every expected symbol. The slider test targets fire_spread in the Dev menu: page one with focus two yields index eight, so applying a right input should increase the value.\n\nThe last Dev menu page holds three sliders and a Reset button. Focusing on Reset and confirming restores the original fire_spread value. The attract-mode test will idle for nine seconds to verify the bot stays on the title screen.\n\nThe attract-mode test ticks the world for nine seconds to confirm the screen remains Title, then ticks for one more second to reach Attract. After 800 seconds it asserts five attract loops and a fully set attract_seen bitset, verifying all ten segments were visited. It also checks that no saves are written during attract mode.\n\nThe attract-mode early return correctly skips adding time for that frame. A four-key input sequence will drive the world from Title through Difficulty, Merc, and Prologue into Playing. The menu up-arrow wraps focus from row zero back to row two.\n\nA hover test will place the pointer at (200, 300) to focus the second row, then click to confirm and enter Difficulty. The pointer position maps to rows with a height of 64 pixels. A loop will also verify that no screen index exceeds seven.\n\nA nested loop will verify that every menu screen, page, and tab has at most seven rows. Glyph tests will assert specific symbols for confirm, fire, and gamepad keys. A network test will create a host and client world, ignite fire on the host, and publish the state through a link.\n\nThe network test advances the link for one second and applies the polled bodies to the client. It asserts that the host and client share a burning cell, the body contains a fire tag, and the link stays under 1200 bytes. The host_publish function currently sends up to four bodies per call without rate limiting, so a 5 Hz cap may need to be added.\n\nA rate gate will be added to `host_publish` to enforce the five-per-second limit. It will block new sends when the window reaches five and the world is not finished, allowing only the finish message through.\n\nThe rate gate will allow the finish message through even when the five-message window is full. A loss test will verify that unreliable sends drop at 100% loss. Fire messages are reliable, so network loss does not affect them. The JSON body shape will include both a coordinate array and separate x and z fields to satisfy either parser.\n\nThe fire message format will be `{\"t\":\"fire\",\"c\":[i,j]}` to match the plan. The client parser already handles digit groups, so negative coordinates are fine. I will silence a dead_code warning by using `stats.name` in the HUD. Unit tests will run first, then the playthrough test.\n\nThe `hash_f32` signature used by geometry is valid. A companion rescue pattern mutates a companion found via `iter_mut().find()` and then assigns to `self.rescue_t`, which compiled successfully. This suggests Rust 2024 two-phase borrow checking allows the borrow to end before the assignment.\n\nThe companion rescue pattern should fail borrow-checking because the mutable borrow from `iter_mut().find()` overlaps with assigning `self.rescue_t`. The fact that it compiles is puzzling, and the exact code needs re-examination.\n\nThe companion rescue pattern compiles, so I will trust the compiler and move on. I will check that games.rs builds with tests enabled, then write the tests and run cargo test. The playthrough budget is set to 140 seconds; 1200 meters at 16 m/s needs about 75 seconds plus turning, so it should pass. The bot drive logic clamps steering error to ±1 and limits yaw rate to 1.6 rad/s at 60 fps, which risks circling the target at high speed.\n\nThe 16 m/s vehicle with a 1.6 rad/s yaw rate has a 10 m turning radius, so it should converge on point targets without wild overshoot. If it still orbits outside the 12 m done radius, the yaw rate will be increased. The technical_driven flag is set correctly for both drives. The bus drive covers roughly 510 m from depot to junction, and an orbiting vehicle might miss the 14 m arrival radius.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1c269de8-e8fd-4ca0-be46-8132600da645-104","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"flank|shooter|morale|Suspicious|smoke","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/PLAN.md","head_limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3721,"output_tokens":5117,"cache_read_input_tokens":140928,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"5377e322-ce88-4e0b-9a8c-6fb1f58ad1b4"}
