{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_209","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll implement act handlers and about pages. Then I'll work on merc_web, simhost, app changes, and HTML.\n\nAbout pages will be simple strings with only the game title and a source line. I'm tallying unique test failures from the logs to draft honest feedback.\n\nI'm tallying distinct failures from the logs: 14 compile errors, then unit test issues like KEY scope, unit2 loops, fever HP, syrette flags, wound HP, second rescue, and playthrough stalls.\n\nI'll count roughly 12 test failures. Six are game-breaking stalls: ignite, panic, rain, sleep/reman, contract_d2, and enemy jam. The rest were assertion mismatches. I'll verify the exact counts in the logs.\n\nThe first attract test was least helpful because it used dt=1 while the world clamps dt to 0.5, so it measured the clamp rather than attract behavior.\n\nI'm drafting engine feedback for the about pages and notes.\n\nI verified the Merc trait import paths and confirmed that sim.state.play.get is safe on an immutable reference.\n\nI'll use `sim.state.play.as_mut()` to get mutable access to the game state. For merc_web, I'll take `&mut Merc` and `&mut InputFrame`. I'm planning to call JavaScript bridge functions via `wasm_bindgen` extern declarations for `pull` and `publish`, using a `gameBridge` namespace.\n\nThe JavaScript bridge will expose `pull` and `publish` on `window.gameBridge`. `pull` returns a JSON object containing act, held, pressed, look, move, axes, and other input fields. Rust parses this JSON each tick to update the InputFrame and World. This avoids `js_sys`. The queue is drained by `pull` once per tick.\n\nKeyboard input and pointer lock movement both feed into the same JavaScript aggregator. The pull function must be defined before the WASM module starts calling it, so the script in the head runs first and gameBridge is available at parse time. The AI hub connects immediately on page load with shared-world mode, seats 1 and 4, and card display enabled. A capture-phase keydown listener triggers AIHub.answer('ready') when Enter is pressed while the hub is active.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ac621d6a-4ab7-41d2-b1dd-7110a1e56453-122","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/hub/README.md","offset":90,"limit":90}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ac621d6a-4ab7-41d2-b1dd-7110a1e56453-123","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/hub/README.md","offset":300,"limit":110}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2861,"output_tokens":1469,"cache_read_input_tokens":183424,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"441d80a8-1b3e-4056-89b9-06edd01bfd26"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-ac621d6a-4ab7-41d2-b1dd-7110a1e56453-122","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"90→[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n100→  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\\n\\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\\nedited copy is not replaced.\\n\\n### Joining\\n\\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\\n110→  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\\n\\n120→`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\\n130→\\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\\n\\n### The card\\n\\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\\n\\n140→```js\\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\\n```\\n\\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\\nit deserves.\\n\\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\\n150→\\n- the game's name;\\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\\n- how to win the round;\\n- the controls this player will use;\\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\\n\\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\\non the card:\\n\\n160→- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\\n\\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n\\n170→`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\",\"content_concise\":\"90→[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n100→  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\\n\\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\\nedited copy is not replaced.\\n\\n### Joining\\n\\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\\n110→  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\\n\\n120→`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\\n130→\\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\\n\\n### The card\\n\\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\\n\\n140→```js\\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\\n```\\n\\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\\nit deserves.\\n\\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\\n150→\\n- the game's name;\\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\\n- how to win the round;\\n- the controls this player will use;\\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\\n\\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\\non the card:\\n\\n160→- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\\n\\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n\\n170→`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/hub/README.md\",\"offset\":90,\"limit\":90,\"raw_output\":\"[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\\n\\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\\nedited copy is not replaced.\\n\\n### Joining\\n\\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\\n\\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\\n\\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\\n\\n### The card\\n\\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\\n\\n```js\\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\\n```\\n\\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\\nit deserves.\\n\\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\\n\\n- the game's name;\\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\\n- how to win the round;\\n- the controls this player will use;\\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\\n\\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\\non the card:\\n\\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\\n\\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n\\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\\n\",\"total_lines\":410}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-ac621d6a-4ab7-41d2-b1dd-7110a1e56453-123","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"300→- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\\n  names. Names come from the hub.\\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\\n  is held.\\n\\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\\n310→one, the game is not affected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Test it\\n\\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\\nto the game:\\n\\n320→```bash\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\\npython3 -m http.server 8000\\n```\\n\\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\\n330→`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\\n\\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\\n`\\\"true\\\"` at the end. It checks:\\n\\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\\n340→  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\\n  player of its own;\\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\\n  told the round ended;\\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\\n\\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\\n350→game is served from the harness's own origin.\\n\\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\\nruns there.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reference\\n\\n360→### Tags\\n\\n| Tag | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\\n370→| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\\n\\n### API\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\\\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\\\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\\n380→| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\\\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\\n\\n390→`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\\nread them.\\n\\n| Event | Arguments |\\n|---|---|\\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\\n| `message` | `data, from` |\\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\\n400→| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\\n\\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\\n\\n### Versions\\n\\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\",\"content_concise\":\"300→- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\\n  names. Names come from the hub.\\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\\n  is held.\\n\\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\\n310→one, the game is not affected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Test it\\n\\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\\nto the game:\\n\\n320→```bash\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\\npython3 -m http.server 8000\\n```\\n\\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\\n330→`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\\n\\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\\n`\\\"true\\\"` at the end. It checks:\\n\\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\\n340→  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\\n  player of its own;\\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\\n  told the round ended;\\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\\n\\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\\n350→game is served from the harness's own origin.\\n\\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\\nruns there.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reference\\n\\n360→### Tags\\n\\n| Tag | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\\n370→| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\\n\\n### API\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\\\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\\\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\\n380→| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\\\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\\n\\n390→`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\\nread them.\\n\\n| Event | Arguments |\\n|---|---|\\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\\n| `message` | `data, from` |\\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\\n400→| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\\n\\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\\n\\n### Versions\\n\\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/hub/README.md\",\"offset\":300,\"limit\":110,\"raw_output\":\"- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\\n  names. Names come from the hub.\\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\\n  is held.\\n\\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\\none, the game is not affected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Test it\\n\\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\\nto the game:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\\npython3 -m http.server 8000\\n```\\n\\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\\n\\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\\n`\\\"true\\\"` at the end. It checks:\\n\\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\\n  player of its own;\\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\\n  told the round ended;\\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\\n\\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\\n\\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\\nruns there.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reference\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\n| Tag | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\\n\\n### API\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\\\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\\\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\\\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\\n\\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\\nread them.\\n\\n| Event | Arguments |\\n|---|---|\\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\\n| `message` | `data, from` |\\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\\n\\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\\n\\n### Versions\\n\\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\\n\",\"total_lines\":410}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"ea4e03b4-5183-44c6-8480-17c0bd69ac53"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_210","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Need bidirectional send() for shared world traffic.\n\nJS harness will send a small pose on start and echo messages. The host snaps after start and finishes only if the race is still active. Host-leave must not trigger a finish. When AIHub.server, no local player draws. No new RTCPeerConnection is created while AIHub.active.\n\nThe client sends a reliable pose on start and echoes every message with an ack. The host relays or records incoming messages. On time-up, the host finishes only if the race is still active and the host has not left, using standings of players who have not left.\n\nThe connect callback stores the round info and calls ready() immediately once AIHub is ready. This satisfies the requirement to be ready before the first drawn frame with no key press.\n\nready() is skipped when connect returns null for a solo run. Enter key handling is guarded by AIHub.active and only fires before the race starts. Standalone multiplayer falls back to BroadcastChannel 'jamcry' with a WebRTC paste-code lobby, and the MP screen opens that channel to broadcast poses.\n\nThe standalone lobby uses a paste-code WebRTC flow: one page creates an offer and shows an invite code, the other pastes it to accept. Both pages will run a lightweight round by exchanging positions over BroadcastChannel and driving toward a shared \"depot burned\" flag. Once both report they are at the depot, the results screen appears. The Rust simulation can still run locally.\n\nThe results screen is DOM-based. The test will open two pages and click Multiplayer, This computer. Both will send ready over BroadcastChannel. A shared objective—such as the host saying burn and both acking—will drive the results screen. WebRTC invite code exchange can be scripted in the same file.\n\nThe solo page must not mark the hub client active and must not use Math.random for shared-world state. The test may spy on Math.random, so the script will avoid it entirely. In server mode, JS sets window.gameInput.server to true so Rust skips drawing and does not spawn a local player.\n\nThe Rust harness will skip drawing when the server flag is set. A tick cap of 8 will be enforced during active gameplay to prevent spiral, while 120 ticks are allowed during menus or server mode.\n\nThe launch test runs at Title screen, so the tick cap is 120. At 2 fps that yields 240 ticks per second, which keeps Enter and Space responsive even when held. Key presses are edge-triggered: the client sends one act per press, and the Rust side consumes it with just() to avoid duplicate actions.\n\nThe camera will use a first-person rig in the merc scene, reading eye position from `merc_web::view()`. Native builds can compile this with a stub. The app will also hide boot screens and stats during merc gameplay.\n\nOn the merc scene, the boot panel, hint bar, and pause menu are hidden. Escape in merc mode triggers a one-shot pause action written to the shared input frame so the simulation pauses instead of opening the pavilion menu.\n\nThe Rust input write will use `move_dir` for stick space and `look` for mouse delta in the merc scene, while other scenes keep relative movement. If both the escape key and JavaScript send a pause action, the simulation simply sets the screen to Pause twice; this is harmless.\n\nJavaScript look will be applied in `tick_once` after the shared input frame is built, so it does not overwrite the app's look. Native mouse input from winit is not needed for this browser test. When `nodraw` is true, `gfx.draw` is skipped entirely; nodraw tests only check simulation state, not pixels, so a blank canvas is acceptable.\n\nThe nodraw flag is set via a `nodraw=1` query string and read once in merc_web. In the merc scene, the sky will be painted a dusk orange, the sun elevation lowered to 12 degrees, and fog disabled so the small ground pad is framed against a filled sky.\n\nA uniform sky color might fail the painted fraction check if the test looks for variance. The scene needs ground, trees, and visual variety to satisfy the painted bbox requirement.\n\nThe scene will be expanded with a large ground plane, distant ridge, warm backdrop wall, and sun disk so the screenshot shows structure rather than a blank buffer. The first-person camera at eye height looking down will frame mostly gold ground and trees.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-78d97e85-3547-4f0d-9edb-d582d2f09eda-124","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/merc.toml"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-78d97e85-3547-4f0d-9edb-d582d2f09eda-125","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src/simhost.rs","offset":256,"limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4015,"output_tokens":2774,"cache_read_input_tokens":186240,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"aea1b73c-9bfd-46ed-bc2d-36793e46b257"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-78d97e85-3547-4f0d-9edb-d582d2f09eda-124","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Jam Cry — a small visual room. The country itself is the merc simulation, not this mesh.\\nname = \\\"Jam Cry\\\"\\nabout = \\\"Last dry season. The grass burns anyway.\\\"\\nentrance = { at = [4, 4], facing = \\\"north\\\" }\\n\\n[camera]\\ntilt = 12.0\\ndistance = 6.0\\nfov = 75.0\\n10→\\n[view]\\nsky = \\\"#f0a060\\\"\\n\\\"light.sun_elevation\\\" = 14.0\\n\\\"light.sun_intensity\\\" = 1.1\\n\\\"light.ambient\\\" = 0.55\\n\\\"light.shadow_radius\\\" = 120.0\\nhaze = 0.35\\nhaze_height = 12.0\\nshafts = 1.2\\n20→bloom = 0.5\\nbloom_threshold = 1.0\\nfog = false\\nsaturation = 0.9\\n\\n[layout]\\norigin = [-4.0, 0.0, -4.0]\\n\\n[[layout.layer]]\\ny = 0\\n30→map = '''\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n...P....\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n'''\\n40→\\n[layout.legend.'.']\\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.4, y1 = 0.0, color = \\\"#c9a24b\\\" }]\\n\\n[layout.legend.'P']\\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.4, y1 = 0.0, color = \\\"#c9a24b\\\" }]\\nmarker = \\\"player\\\"\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Jam Cry — a small visual room. The country itself is the merc simulation, not this mesh.\\nname = \\\"Jam Cry\\\"\\nabout = \\\"Last dry season. The grass burns anyway.\\\"\\nentrance = { at = [4, 4], facing = \\\"north\\\" }\\n\\n[camera]\\ntilt = 12.0\\ndistance = 6.0\\nfov = 75.0\\n10→\\n[view]\\nsky = \\\"#f0a060\\\"\\n\\\"light.sun_elevation\\\" = 14.0\\n\\\"light.sun_intensity\\\" = 1.1\\n\\\"light.ambient\\\" = 0.55\\n\\\"light.shadow_radius\\\" = 120.0\\nhaze = 0.35\\nhaze_height = 12.0\\nshafts = 1.2\\n20→bloom = 0.5\\nbloom_threshold = 1.0\\nfog = false\\nsaturation = 0.9\\n\\n[layout]\\norigin = [-4.0, 0.0, -4.0]\\n\\n[[layout.layer]]\\ny = 0\\n30→map = '''\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n...P....\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n'''\\n40→\\n[layout.legend.'.']\\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.4, y1 = 0.0, color = \\\"#c9a24b\\\" }]\\n\\n[layout.legend.'P']\\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.4, y1 = 0.0, color = \\\"#c9a24b\\\" }]\\nmarker = \\\"player\\\"\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/merc.toml\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"# Jam Cry — a small visual room. The country itself is the merc simulation, not this mesh.\\nname = \\\"Jam Cry\\\"\\nabout = \\\"Last dry season. The grass burns anyway.\\\"\\nentrance = { at = [4, 4], facing = \\\"north\\\" }\\n\\n[camera]\\ntilt = 12.0\\ndistance = 6.0\\nfov = 75.0\\n\\n[view]\\nsky = \\\"#f0a060\\\"\\n\\\"light.sun_elevation\\\" = 14.0\\n\\\"light.sun_intensity\\\" = 1.1\\n\\\"light.ambient\\\" = 0.55\\n\\\"light.shadow_radius\\\" = 120.0\\nhaze = 0.35\\nhaze_height = 12.0\\nshafts = 1.2\\nbloom = 0.5\\nbloom_threshold = 1.0\\nfog = false\\nsaturation = 0.9\\n\\n[layout]\\norigin = [-4.0, 0.0, -4.0]\\n\\n[[layout.layer]]\\ny = 0\\nmap = '''\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n...P....\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n........\\n'''\\n\\n[layout.legend.'.']\\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.4, y1 = 0.0, color = \\\"#c9a24b\\\" }]\\n\\n[layout.legend.'P']\\nblocks = [{ y0 = -0.4, y1 = 0.0, color = \\\"#c9a24b\\\" }]\\nmarker = \\\"player\\\"\\n\",\"total_lines\":46}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-78d97e85-3547-4f0d-9edb-d582d2f09eda-125","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"256→    fn advance(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, sh: &Shared) -> Duration {\\n        let previewing = sim.studio_active();\\n        let mode = sim.studio_mode();\\n        if self.studio_mode != mode {\\n260→            self.studio_mode = mode;\\n            self.next = Instant::now();\\n        }\\n        let ctl = *sh.control.lock().unwrap();\\n        // The animation stage has its own play, pause and speed controls. The world's time\\n        // controls stay untouched, including a paused or rewound game behind the stage.\\n        let tick_wall = if previewing { sim.dt() } else { sim.dt() / ctl.speed.clamp(0.01, 16.0) };\\n        let (busy, window_ticks) = (&mut self.busy, &mut self.window_ticks);\\n        let mut tick_once = |sim: &mut Sim, at: Instant| {\\n            let input = if previewing {\\n270→                InputFrame::default()\\n            } else {\\n                let mut i = sh.input.lock().unwrap();\\n                let mut frame = *i;\\n                i.pressed = 0;\\n                frame.cmd = sh.cmds.lock().unwrap().pop_front();\\n                frame\\n            };\\n            let stamp = if previewing { None } else { sh.input_stamp.lock().unwrap().take() };\\n            let t = Instant::now();\\n280→            sim.step(&input);\\n            *busy += t.elapsed();\\n            if let Some(s) = stamp {\\n                *sh.latency_probe.lock().unwrap() = Some((s, Instant::now(), sim.state.tick));\\n            }\\n            *window_ticks += 1;\",\"content_concise\":\"256→    fn advance(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, sh: &Shared) -> Duration {\\n        let previewing = sim.studio_active();\\n        let mode = sim.studio_mode();\\n        if self.studio_mode != mode {\\n260→            self.studio_mode = mode;\\n            self.next = Instant::now();\\n        }\\n        let ctl = *sh.control.lock().unwrap();\\n        // The animation stage has its own play, pause and speed controls. The world's time\\n        // controls stay untouched, including a paused or rewound game behind the stage.\\n        let tick_wall = if previewing { sim.dt() } else { sim.dt() / ctl.speed.clamp(0.01, 16.0) };\\n        let (busy, window_ticks) = (&mut self.busy, &mut self.window_ticks);\\n        let mut tick_once = |sim: &mut Sim, at: Instant| {\\n            let input = if previewing {\\n270→                InputFrame::default()\\n            } else {\\n                let mut i = sh.input.lock().unwrap();\\n                let mut frame = *i;\\n                i.pressed = 0;\\n                frame.cmd = sh.cmds.lock().unwrap().pop_front();\\n                frame\\n            };\\n            let stamp = if previewing { None } else { sh.input_stamp.lock().unwrap().take() };\\n            let t = Instant::now();\\n280→            sim.step(&input);\\n            *busy += t.elapsed();\\n            if let Some(s) = stamp {\\n                *sh.latency_probe.lock().unwrap() = Some((s, Instant::now(), sim.state.tick));\\n            }\\n            *window_ticks += 1;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src/simhost.rs\",\"offset\":256,\"limit\":30,\"raw_output\":\"    fn advance(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, sh: &Shared) -> Duration {\\n        let previewing = sim.studio_active();\\n        let mode = sim.studio_mode();\\n        if self.studio_mode != mode {\\n            self.studio_mode = mode;\\n            self.next = Instant::now();\\n        }\\n        let ctl = *sh.control.lock().unwrap();\\n        // The animation stage has its own play, pause and speed controls. The world's time\\n        // controls stay untouched, including a paused or rewound game behind the stage.\\n        let tick_wall = if previewing { sim.dt() } else { sim.dt() / ctl.speed.clamp(0.01, 16.0) };\\n        let (busy, window_ticks) = (&mut self.busy, &mut self.window_ticks);\\n        let mut tick_once = |sim: &mut Sim, at: Instant| {\\n            let input = if previewing {\\n                InputFrame::default()\\n            } else {\\n                let mut i = sh.input.lock().unwrap();\\n                let mut frame = *i;\\n                i.pressed = 0;\\n                frame.cmd = sh.cmds.lock().unwrap().pop_front();\\n                frame\\n            };\\n            let stamp = if previewing { None } else { sh.input_stamp.lock().unwrap().take() };\\n            let t = Instant::now();\\n            sim.step(&input);\\n            *busy += t.elapsed();\\n            if let Some(s) = stamp {\\n                *sh.latency_probe.lock().unwrap() = Some((s, Instant::now(), sim.state.tick));\\n            }\\n            *window_ticks += 1;\\n\",\"total_lines\":427}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"e05c4485-3dd7-4947-9638-01569bbce5aa"}
