{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_236","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The Game trait must be in scope for get_mut.\n\npav_app will import Game and Merc from pav_core::games. A tick cap helper checks the active player key, Merc screen state, and a fast-ticks flag to determine the cap.\n\nPreview mode keeps the 8-tick cap. The higher cap applies only to the non-preview loop.\n\nMerc nodraw is read from merc_web once per frame. It is false on native.\n\nStatus and UI publish every 15 ticks after sim.step. This ensures the published state matches the tick.\n\nmerc_web::publish writes Merc status and UI into window.__game.\n\nlocalStorage is handled in apply before each step. On the first tick it checks for a jamcry-save entry and sets world.has_save and save_blob without replacing the world. After stepping, it writes the blob to localStorage whenever saves_written exceeds last_saved. The first tick uses this to seed the save state before Continue loads it.\n\nInput from device, touch, Xbox, and pointer sources is read as JSON from JavaScript each tick and applied to the world.\n\nThe gameBridge extern uses wasm_bindgen with a catch block. It defines pull() and publish(status, ui). If the JavaScript object is missing, the error is caught and processing is skipped. The page script must be loaded before the module runs.\n\nThe packer appends its loader and glue after the template scripts, so gameBridge is already defined when the WASM module runs.\n\nsession.js must be the first script after the charset tag. The current index.html places it after the style element and body scripts, so that order needs to change.\n\nThe packer's title and metadata replacements still work. The session.js file was checked for a closing script tag and is clean.\n\nThe build script will temporarily swap the session.js marker in index.html before packing and restore it afterward. The packer reads the file directly, so a wrapper script is needed to perform this substitution.\n\nInlining the 16KB session.js directly into index.html is possible but fragile. The build script will instead read the raw bytes from ../hub/session.js and insert them into web/index.html at pack time.\n\nThe build script will use a trap to restore the original index.html after packing. The packer hardcodes the page path, so the substitution must happen in place.\n\nA DOM shell div #shell will sit on the left in large type. It updates from window.__game.ui each frame and shows the title before WASM loads. Rows appear as buttons with notice text and about pages in large type.\n\nTouch devices are detected via matchMedia for coarse pointers without hover. The bridge payload sets touch:true. The shell displays a notice and places the settings button in a row. Touch controls remain disabled. Attract mode still runs because idle increments when the hub is not active.\n\nMusic playback requires a first pointer or key gesture to create the AudioContext. An oscillator and gain node with value 0.10 connect to the destination. The test wraps AudioNode.connect to verify the gain value. Rust sets status.audio.music to 0.10. A gamepad stub is being prepared.\n\nThe gamepad stub will match PLAN §2.4 exactly. It uses a specific ID, 17 buttons, four axes, and a connected state. navigator.getGamepads returns this single pad.\n\nPointer lock activates on canvas click during play. Mouse movementX and movementY feed into look input. Releasing pointer lock sends action 10 while playing.\n\nTests dispatch MouseEvent with movementX. That property may not be settable in all browsers.\n\nPlaywright Chromium accepts movementX in MouseEvent constructors.\n\nEscape keydown sends action 10 and exits pointer lock.\n\nThe WebGPU requirement message stays as-is.\n\nA hub object will live in the page script. It tracks phase, info, ended, finished, present, started, and card.\n\nAIHub connects in shared-world mode with seats 1 and 4. It copies the returned info into hub.info and builds a present set from player IDs. The server is excluded from that set. AIHub.ready() signals readiness.\n\nCard events update hub.card and trigger renderCard. Start events set hub.started to true and hub.phase to play.\n\nNon-host clients send reliable acknowledgments for every message they receive.\n\nLeave events remove player IDs from the present set. Time-up triggers tryFinish, which aborts if the hub is already ended or finished. End events set hub.ended and stop audio. tryFinish only runs on the host; it skips finishing when the host itself leaves.\n\nThe host page is removed when the host leaves, so it cannot finish. Other clients are not the host, so they do not finish either. The remaining clients should void the round because the host left, not because they finished. tryFinish only calls AIHub.finish when time-up arrives while the host is still present and the hub has not ended.\n\nAfter start, non-server copies send a reliable pose every 200 ms. The server sends a world update instead. Both directions keep traffic flowing.\n\nThe host will never finish the round on its own leave event. Only time-up triggers a finish. This prevents the host page from finishing before the round is voided.\n\nWhen time-up triggers, the host finishes the round using only still-present players. The present set drops any player removed by the n>=3 rule before the timeout. Standings list every remaining player exactly once.\n\nThe server is excluded from the present set and standings because its player list omits itself. During a card, Enter in the capture phase sends a ready answer to AIHub without changing the round state. Confirm input still reaches the simulation when not inside a card.\n\nForwarding Enter to the simulation during a card is safe as long as it does not send a finish signal. The card will use key \"m\" for \"More time\" and display that label. When AIHub is inactive, two standalone pages need a results screen using BroadcastChannel or WebRTC.\n\nThe multiplayer menu will offer a BroadcastChannel connection for \"This computer\" and an invite code path for WebRTC. A host creates an RTCPeerConnection offer only when AIHub is inactive. Both pages then play a short depot round and display results on a shared screen. The implementation must reach that real results screen rather than using a fake flag. The test will drive this end-to-end flow.\n\nThe BroadcastChannel path uses a hello exchange. Both pages show a depot countdown and then a shared results screen reading \"Extracted together\". The WebRTC path generates a base64 SDP offer for the host and an answer for the peer. Both sides paste their codes to complete the connection. Tests will drive both flows through window.gameNet methods: hostBC, joinBC, offerCode, acceptOffer, acceptAnswer, and results.\n\nThe solo page must not call Math.random for shared-world state. The test will spy that function and assert it is never invoked during a solo run. session.js only seeds Math.random inside a hub session, so the solo path should remain unaffected. Our code must avoid Math.random entirely.\n\nThe solo page must not activate the hub client and must not call Math.random for shared-world state. A seeded mulberry generator may replace random for deterministic behavior. The WASM engine might still invoke Math.random, but the test only needs to verify the gameNet and shared code paths stay free of it.\n\nThe canvas drawing size must match the viewport width and height. Playwright will set the viewport explicitly, and the test will assert that canvas.width equals the requested viewport width.\n\nThe engine may scale the canvas by devicePixelRatio in headless mode. I will check how gfx sets the canvas size and set deviceScaleFactor to 1 if needed. I will not hack the canvas directly to avoid breaking the renderer.\n\nA static \"Jam Cry\" heading appears before WASM loads. The Title screen then transitions through Difficulty, Merc, Prologue, and Playing as Enter is pressed four times. The test checks these state changes. Attract mode may consume the first key if the wait is too long. With 120 ticks per frame, each real second covers about two simulation seconds. At ten simulation seconds of idle time, that equals roughly 2.5 real seconds. If WASM loading delays the first frame, the engine can catch up within the 120-tick cap.\n\nThe clock resets its next tick to the current time when it falls behind, so one frame never runs more than 120 ticks. That means ten simulation seconds of idle time require five frames of waiting. The launch test will poll until the Title screen is visible, then immediately dispatch four keys with brief pauses between them.\n\nIf attract mode starts first, the initial key returns to Title without consuming the confirm, so the state still changes. The test will dispatch four keys quickly to reach Playing.\n\nThe dev mode browser test navigates from playing to pause, then through the Dev menu to Cheats and Balance tabs. It toggles God mode and increases fire_spread.\n\nThe dev menu has five rows: Resume, Settings, Controls, Dev mode, and Quit. Focus starts at zero. Three downs and a confirm open the Dev tab.\n\nThat tab lists Cheats, Teleport, Spawn, World, and Balance. Confirming Cheats selects the first page.\n\nOn that first page, God mode is the first row. Confirm toggles it.\n\nBalance is the fifth row on that page. Confirming it opens the Balance tab. Fire_spread is the third item on page one of that tab. The test can send act 12 to increment fire_spread from anywhere. It can send act 11 to toggle God mode.\n\nThe dev-mode test will open the Dev tab first, then use act 11 and 12 to toggle God mode and increase fire_spread while the screen is Dev. The Settings tab also has an Xbox glyphs option that toggles via act 14; the confirm glyph switches from Cross to A.\n\nThe launch test uses keyboard input, which will flip the active device to Keyboard and cause confirm glyphs to display as Cross. The browser test for PS glyphs must instead set the device lock via a gamepad poll or by passing {device:'gamepad'} to keep the device locked as Gamepad. With the Gamepad device, glyph(\"confirm\", Gamepad, false) returns \"Cross\"; setting Xbox true returns \"A\".\n\nPlaywright's `emulateMedia` does not set the pointer media query, so it cannot reliably trigger the phone profile. The test should use `hasTouch: true` or an iPhone device profile to force the page into touch-only mode.\n\nPlaywright's `devices['iPhone 13']` context sets both `isMobile` and `hasTouch`, which Chromium usually interprets as a coarse pointer. A `?touch=1` JavaScript flag will be added as a fallback if media queries fail to detect touch. The test will verify the phone profile by checking `navigator.maxTouchPoints` and `matchMedia`. On mobile, attract mode requires ten simulation seconds of idle time.\n\nThe test passes ?nodraw=1&test=1 to skip rendering and cap ticks at 120 per frame. The clock advances by dt/speed each frame, so a 16 ms frame yields roughly one tick. The 120-tick limit only activates when a frame is late, such as during a software GPU stall.\n\nWith nodraw enabled, the engine advances roughly one simulation second per real second. The attract idle timer therefore requires about twelve seconds of wall-clock time to reach ten simulation seconds.\n\nAlternatively, the test can set a high simulation speed via control.speed when ?test=1 is active. This makes each frame run many ticks at once until the clock catches up.\n\nSetting simulation speed to 8x during test mode makes menus fly by. At that pace, ten simulation seconds of idle time take roughly one second of wall-clock time. The test will set this speed from the app or a callback, and the clock will cap ticks at 120 per frame while advancing much faster.\n\nThe launch test should not use the ?test=1 flag, so the normal frame rate is preserved and attract mode does not start instantly. The 120-tick cap only applies when the screen is not Playing. If the first frames are slow due to WASM loading or GPU stalls, the idle timer could still reach ten simulation seconds before the test dispatches keys. One mitigation is to delay counting idle time until the page receives pointer or keyboard focus.\n\nThe idle timer will start only after the first UI publish, so GPU stalls cannot trigger attract mode before the test sends keys. If attract starts first, a single key press exits it to Title and still satisfies the state-change criterion. The test will dispatch Enter and Space as soon as the game screen element appears.\n\nThe test will record the screen before and after the four key presses and assert they differ. The first frame will be filled by setting the fp camera in App::new so the overlook is visible immediately. The dress() call will enlarge the ground to cover the full 8x8 floor, and the sky color #f0a060 will fill the background. The player spawn position still needs verification.\n\nThe camera eye will be fixed at (0, 1.7, 2.2) looking toward the trees at negative Z, so the first frame is not a blank sky. Large boxes such as acacia and termite mounds will be added to fill the view with painted geometry rather than flat colors.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-4ee7d4ac-702a-4b97-968e-e230d9e5dc72-56","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src/app.rs","offset":1280,"limit":100}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-4ee7d4ac-702a-4b97-968e-e230d9e5dc72-57","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"look:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/merc/world.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-4ee7d4ac-702a-4b97-968e-e230d9e5dc72-58","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/input.rs","offset":1,"limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1833,"output_tokens":6344,"cache_read_input_tokens":115712,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"1104f877-ab5f-4022-8de1-61ca041265ca"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-4ee7d4ac-702a-4b97-968e-e230d9e5dc72-56","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1280→                let a = if d.length() > self.sim_config.bombs.throw_range {\\n                    let d = d.normalize() * self.sim_config.bombs.throw_range;\\n                    Vec3::new(feet.x + d.x, a.y, feet.z + d.y)\\n                } else {\\n                    a\\n                };\\n                scene.meshes.push(MeshInstance {\\n                    mesh: MeshKey::Cylinder,\\n                    transform: Mat4::from_scale_rotation_translation(\\n                        Vec3::new(0.5, 0.02, 0.5),\\n1290→                        Quat::IDENTITY,\\n                        a + Vec3::Y * 0.03,\\n                    ),\\n                    color: Vec3::new(1.0, 0.95, 0.7),\\n                    emissive: 0.0,\\n                    style: Style::Unlit,\\n                    flags: rflags::NO_SHADOW | rflags::NO_CUT,\\n                    group: 0,\\n                    material: 0,\\n                    ext: Vec3::ZERO,\\n1300→                });\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        // UI.\\n        let stats = *host.shared.stats.lock().unwrap();\\n        let mut ctl = host.control();\\n        let ctl_before = ctl;\\n        let crash = host.shared.crashed.lock().unwrap().clone();\\n        self.panel.record_timing(dt * 1000.0, stats.tick_ms);\\n1310→        let gfx = self.gfx.as_mut().unwrap();\\n        let (render_scale, lights) =\\n            self.scaler.update(self.prefs.quality, self.touch_screen, dt * 1000.0, gfx.size(), gfx.window.scale_factor() as f32);\\n        gfx.renderer.render_scale = render_scale;\\n        gfx.renderer.max_shadow_lights = lights;\\n        let info = ui::OverlayInfo {\\n            fps: self.fps.fps,\\n            frame_ms: self.fps.frame_ms,\\n            tps: stats.tps,\\n            tick_ms: stats.tick_ms,\\n1320→            tick: stats.tick,\\n            entities: stats.entities,\\n            gpu_errors: gfx.gpu_errors.load(std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed),\\n            adapter: &gfx.adapter_name,\\n            crash: crash.clone(),\\n            paused: ctl.paused,\\n            speed: ctl.speed,\\n            rewinding,\\n            render_scale,\\n        };\\n1330→        if let Some(t) = self.boot_done_at {\\n            if t.elapsed().as_secs_f32() > 10.0 && self.show_boot {\\n                self.show_boot = false;\\n                self.boot_done_at = None;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        let toast = self.toast.as_ref().filter(|(_, t)| t.elapsed().as_secs_f32() < 3.0).map(|(m, _)| m.clone());\\n        let card_visible = !studio_active && self.hud.card_visible();\\n        let guide_visible =\\n            !studio_active && self.app_settings.show_guide && self.started.elapsed().as_secs_f32() < 25.0 && !card_visible;\\n1340→        let device = self.input.last_device;\\n        let touch_ui = device == Device::Touch;\\n        if touch_ui != self.touch_styled {\\n            self.touch_styled = touch_ui;\\n            pav_ui::touch_style(&self.egui_ctx, touch_ui);\\n        }\\n        self.game_ui.touch = touch_ui;\\n        self.game_ui.tree.touch = touch_ui;\\n        self.touch.swipes.begin_frame();\\n        let touch = &mut self.touch;\\n1350→        let auto_attack = self.prefs.auto_attack;\\n        let tips = touch_ui && !self.prefs.tips_seen && self.started.elapsed().as_secs_f32() < 45.0;\\n        let mut prefs = self.prefs.clone();\\n        let portal = self.game_frame.as_ref().is_some_and(|g| g.place != pav_core::arpg::Place::Town);\\n        let state = self.egui_state.as_mut().unwrap();\\n        let mut raw = state.take(&gfx.window);\\n        raw.events.append(&mut self.pad_events);\\n        let pad_cursor = self.pad_cursor;\\n        let mut show_boot = self.show_boot;\\n        let menu_open = self.menu_open;\\n1360→        let scene_name = self.scene_name.clone();\\n        let mut menu_action = None;\\n        let mut panel_actions = Vec::new();\\n        let mut teleport = None;\\n        let mut save_room = false;\\n        let room_name = self.hud.current.as_ref().map(|r| r.key.clone());\\n        let editor = &mut self.editor;\\n        let hud = &mut self.hud;\\n        let config_before = self.sim_config.clone();\\n        let app_before = self.app_settings.clone();\\n1370→        let panel = &mut self.panel;\\n        let master_before = self.audio_settings.master;\\n        let view_proj = self.rig.view_proj(w as f32 / h.max(1) as f32);\\n        let mut root = Root {\\n            sim: &mut self.sim_config,\\n            camera: &mut self.rig.params,\\n            view: &mut self.view,\\n            app: &mut self.app_settings,\\n            audio: &mut self.audio_settings,\\n        };\",\"content_concise\":\"1280→                let a = if d.length() > self.sim_config.bombs.throw_range {\\n                    let d = d.normalize() * self.sim_config.bombs.throw_range;\\n                    Vec3::new(feet.x + d.x, a.y, feet.z + d.y)\\n                } else {\\n                    a\\n                };\\n                scene.meshes.push(MeshInstance {\\n                    mesh: MeshKey::Cylinder,\\n                    transform: Mat4::from_scale_rotation_translation(\\n                        Vec3::new(0.5, 0.02, 0.5),\\n1290→                        Quat::IDENTITY,\\n                        a + Vec3::Y * 0.03,\\n                    ),\\n                    color: Vec3::new(1.0, 0.95, 0.7),\\n                    emissive: 0.0,\\n                    style: Style::Unlit,\\n                    flags: rflags::NO_SHADOW | rflags::NO_CUT,\\n                    group: 0,\\n                    material: 0,\\n                    ext: Vec3::ZERO,\\n1300→                });\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        // UI.\\n        let stats = *host.shared.stats.lock().unwrap();\\n        let