{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_292","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A fiction test might call fiction_lines, which includes about_pages.\n\nThe about screen will be sliced into pages of four lines each. Page advancement will be capped to prevent an endless loop.\n\nThe page index will be clamped inside the slice function itself. Back at page zero returns to the title screen. This is a small world.rs edit and native tests are unaffected.\n\nThe dev_rows list will include cheats and sliders. The browser test uses act 11/12 to change status, and pause also opens Dev mode.\n\nA 15-tick delay at 60fps is 250ms, which is acceptable for the three-second test timeout.\n\nThe wasm module imports Merc, Screen, and InputFrame.\n\nI will write the files and apply the about slice before starting the rebuild.\n\nThe unused import warning for Game came from the parent module, which declares Merc and Screen but never uses them. The wasm child imports those same types through a glob, so they are effectively used there. Native code only needs InputFrame and Sim.\n\nThe parent module's Merc and Screen imports will be wrapped in `#[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")]`. This keeps native builds free of unused-import warnings while still allowing the wasm child module to see them through `super::*`.\n\nInputFrame and Sim will remain unconditional imports because native code also uses them. Only Merc and Screen will be wrapped in `#[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")]`, since the wasm child still accesses them through `super::*`.\n\nThe fire_spread default will be 0.9 with a step of 0.05, and the test will assert an increase. Yaw will start at zero and rise by roughly 0.2 radians after mouse movement. Pointer lock must be active because the mouse listener checks document.pointerLockElement.\n\nA fallback exists for look commands without pointer lock, but the test specifically requires a real pointer-lock mousemove. Chrome headless supports requestPointerLock. Chromium will launch with exact required flags plus --disable-background-timer-throttling so animation frames stay alive.\n\nAdditional Chromium flags will prevent renderer backgrounding and occluded-window throttling. xvfb is unnecessary for headless runs, but a headed fallback is kept if pointer lock fails. The Python test file needs careful writing. A two-page network test can run on file:// without waiting for WASM or GPU, since startBroadcast and WebRTC are page JavaScript.\n\nSolo Math.random identity can be tested on file:// without WASM. The page only activates AIHub when the URL contains ?aihub= and is framed, so a plain load leaves it inactive and preserves the native random source.\n\nThe network test will still attempt to initialize WebGPU even with ?nodraw=1, because the hub skips GPU work only when AIHub.active is true. To avoid consuming GPU memory during network tests, the hub must remain inactive.\n\nA `?nogpu=1` query flag will be added to skip WebGPU initialization. The classic script will set `window.__jamSkipGpu` when this flag is present, letting network and solo tests avoid GPU memory without relying on AIHub.\n\nThe `__jamSkipGpu` flag will be set at the top of the classic script before boot, combining the existing AIHub check with the new `?nogpu=1` query. The loader runs before the message is removed, so it never initializes GPU. Four hub iframes will each skip GPU startup because AIHub remains active.\n\nThe about screen will be sliced into four-line pages inside `ui_value`. A dev folder will hold tests. `notes/feedback.md` and a pack script are also planned. The browser test will use clear FAIL messages. `run.sh` will print only failures and a summary.\n\nThe Python test runner will emit only `FAIL:` lines on failure and nothing on success, exiting with code 1 if any test fails. The shell script will filter cargo output to show only failures.\n\nThe shell script will grep the cargo log for failures and only print those lines, followed by a summary. It must avoid set -e so the summary line is always shown. Eight test threads are sufficient. The playthrough test may take a few seconds because compiling is slower than running individual filters.\n\nThe merc suite will be compiled once with `--no-run` before running individual filters. Four sequential cargo invocations are acceptable since the binary already exists and only checks freshness. Pav_audio and pav_view tests will run next in the ui group. Pav_view tests live in camera.rs and can be targeted by name. Pav_audio tests live in lib.rs.\n\nThe shell script will check cargo logs for \"0 passed\" and \"0 failed\" to catch empty test filters. Multiple cargo filters run tests matching any pattern. The engine directory must be the working directory because tests read merc.toml from there.\n\nThe pack script will back up index.html before editing it. It uses a Python heredoc to patch the page and session files, then restores the backup on exit.\n\nThe pack script validates the session.js content for unsafe HTML tags before replacing the marker in index.html. It then runs wasm-bindgen to generate JavaScript bindings from the existing WASM artifact.\n\nThe pack script will run a Python tool to embed the generated WASM bindings into a single HTML file. It will set the page title, description, theme color, and default scene to merc. The temporary bindgen directory will be removed after packing.\n\nThe pack script will verify the embedded session script matches the original session.js byte-for-byte after packing. The about screen will be sliced into five-line pages, requiring roughly six pages for thirty lines of text. The browser test will click Next up to twelve times to collect all text.\n\nThe about screen field in `ui_value` will include a page index so the test can track pagination. A potential race condition was spotted: the loader uses `replaceState` to add `scene=merc` to the URL, but Rust reads `location.search` during its first `ensure()` call. If the search is read before the state update completes, the query string may be missing.\n\nThe loader runs during page parsing and sets the scene URL before the WASM module starts, so Rust reads both query flags correctly. The engine settings will read ?scene= from the URL at startup. Next I will update ui_value and index nogpu, write the scripts, and start cargo. I will also mark the burst helper as used to avoid a dead_code warning.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d476ca71-7a5d-4bc7-a907-a245d5238ace-70","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src/merc_web.rs","old_string":"#[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\npub fn burst() -> bool {\n    false\n}\n\n#[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\npub fn skip_draw() -> bool {","new_string":"#[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\npub fn skip_draw() -> bool {"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d476ca71-7a5d-4bc7-a907-a245d5238ace-71","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src/merc_web.rs","old_string":"    pub fn burst() -> bool {\n        ensure().test\n    }\n\n    pub fn skip_draw() -> bool {","new_string":"    pub fn skip_draw() -> bool {"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d476ca71-7a5d-4bc7-a907-a245d5238ace-72","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src/merc_web.rs","old_string":"pub use wasm::{apply, burst, publish, skip_draw, tick_cap};","new_string":"pub use wasm::{apply, publish, skip_draw, tick_cap};"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d476ca71-7a5d-4bc7-a907-a245d5238ace-73","name":"search_replace