).get(self.focus).cloned().unwrap_or_default(),\\n            \\\"notice\\\": self.notice(),\\n            \\\"touch_controls\\\": false,\\n            \\\"glyph\\\": shell::glyph(\\\"confirm\\\", self.device, self.xbox),\\n            \\\"xbox\\\": self.xbox,\\n            \\\"device\\\": format!(\\\"{:?}\\\", self.device),\\n            \\\"about\\\": self.about_slice(),\\n        })\\n    }\\n}\",\"new_line\":2270,\"context_before\":\"        })\\n    }\\n\\n\",\"context_after\":\"\\nfn wound_flag(n: u8) -> &'static str {\\n    match n {\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"df078b57-0bcf-4ed3-9ea5-d5fc1b494aeb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_294","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Checking whether the `page` field exists on World.\n\nConfirmed `page` exists on World. `div_ceil` is stable in Rust 1.98. Now writing test scripts and notes, starting the WASM build, and checking that `merc_web` compiles and `burst` is fully removed from simhost. Preparing browser.py with Playwright sync API and GPU flags.\n\nAdding SwiftShader and background throttling flags to the browser launch. Planning a pixel-distribution test using PIL to catch canvas rendering failures. Will verify the page title via DOM rather than pixels. Starting to outline the browser launch flow.\n\nDrafting the `launch` function in browser.py. It launches a headless Chromium browser with GPU flags, navigates to the HTML page, and asserts the title is \"Jam Cry\" before WASM loads.\n\nThe `launch` function now waits for the canvas element to appear and reach near-window dimensions. It pauses briefly to let a frame paint, then captures both a full-page screenshot and a direct canvas bitmap.\n\nThe `launch` function now captures the initial UI state via `page.evaluate`, then presses Enter and Space keys to trigger a frame change. It asserts that the UI state differs after input and that no errors occurred.\n\nThe `launch` function now asserts the canvas drawing dimensions match the viewport exactly. It closes the browser after verification. Setting the device scale factor to 1 ensures the WebGPU canvas buffer aligns with the requested viewport size.\n\nThe canvas drawing size equals the viewport because the device scale factor is 1. The left panel overlay does not affect this. Using `full_page=False` captures the viewport including the title panel.\n\nAdding a `canvas_varied` helper that polls the canvas screenshot until it shows more than three distinct colors, with a 60-second timeout. This catches slow software-rendered first frames. Also noting that WebGPU warnings during load may surface as console errors.\n\nThe browser script will record all console and page errors but only fail on actual exceptions or unexpected console errors. Benign SwiftShader warnings will be filtered out later if needed. The \"needs WebGPU\" message is treated as plain text, not an error. Hooks will load the game with `?test=1&nodraw=1` to bypass drawing.\n\nAdding helper functions to browser.py. `push` queues commands via `window.gameTest.queue.push`. `ui` reads the game state from `window.__game.ui` and `window.__game.status`. `wait_until` wraps `page.wait_for_function` with a timeout. The glyph sequence begins with `{\"act\": 3}`.\n\nThe test script now waits for the glyph state to become 'Enter'. It then stubs navigator.getGamepads with a fake DualShock 4 controller and initializes an empty pad state.\n\nThe glyph sequence now proceeds from Enter to Cross and then to A. The default gamepad glyph is already Cross, so the act 3 flip to Keyboard/Enter and the stub reset to Cross are expected. Slow publish cycles are handled by polling wait_for_function. New helpers confirm() and wait_screen() are being drafted to push the confirm action and wait for a specific UI screen.\n\nThe `wait_screen` helper now polls the UI state for a specific screen name. The test will clear localStorage before navigation so no prior save interferes. On file://, an init script can safely call `localStorage.clear()` because it runs before page scripts. The launch sequence will confirm through Title, Difficulty, and Merc screens.\n\nThe test sequence now confirms through Prologue and reaches the Playing screen. Pointer lock is tested by clicking the canvas, dispatching a mousemove event to rotate yaw, and exiting pointer lock. The game pauses afterward.\n\nThe test now waits for the Dev mode row to appear in the UI. It then pushes actions 11 and 12 to toggle god mode and verify fire spread increases. The save flow is tested by pushing action 15, confirming the save exists in localStorage, reloading the page, selecting Continue, and returning to the Playing screen.\n\nAn init script will wrap the audio context connect call before the page starts music. Pressing Enter afterward lets the test verify that audio gains are active.\n\nThe About screen is reached by clicking a hover target near the bottom of the menu. The test scrolls through the twelve paragraphs by repeatedly pressing action 1 and waiting for the required text to appear.\n\nThe About screen test will wait for the paragraph text to change after each Next press. A required strings list is being prepared for validation.\n\nFor the phone test, the iPhone 13 device profile is copied and its device scale factor is overridden to 1. This prevents an oversized canvas that could cause an out-of-memory crash.\n\nThe phone test will wait for the Attract screen to appear, which may take up to twenty seconds.\n\nA WebKit browser launch is planned without WebGPU flags. The test will verify that the page displays the \"needs WebGPU\" message while still loading the title correctly.\n\nFor the network test, two pages will open with `?nogpu=1`. Each page will start a broadcast, and both will assert that the results text contains \"BroadcastChannel\".\n\nThe WebRTC test will exchange offer and answer codes between two pages, then assert the results contain \"WebRTC\". For the solo random test, an init script will replace `Math.random` with a wrapper that counts calls before `session.js` loads, ensuring the game captures the wrapper rather than the native function.\n\nThe solo random test will assert that `__randCalls` stays at zero and `AIHub.active` is false, confirming the game never invokes the seeded generator. The test page must not call `Math.random` directly; only the wrapper may do so. For the hub test, an HTTP server will serve the title, and four Playwright harness pages will fetch it.\n\nThe hub test will start an HTTP server via `subprocess` and parse the port from its stderr output. It will fetch the title page and assert the `<title>Jam Cry</title>` tag. Playwright Chromium will then run the hub tests without WebGPU flags, since the hub skips GPU initialization.\n\nThe hub harness page will be loaded at `http://127.0.0.1:{port}/{devname}/hub/harness.html?game={game_rel}&{query}`. It waits for `window.__hubCheck.done` with a ninety-second timeout, then evaluates the result and fails if checks fail. The game path resolves from the hub folder up to the work root via `../../`. The game filename must be URL-encoded because it contains spaces.\n\nThe hub harness resolves the game URL via `new URL(game, location)`, so the relative path correctly points to the work-root HTML file. Four sequential hub cases may take about three minutes, so a ninety-second timeout per case is appropriate. Server mode will run without a local player; JavaScript handles it, and failure will be debugged if needed. The HTTP server will be stopped by sending SIGTERM to its process ID.\n\nThe server process will be terminated with `terminate()` and `wait(timeout=5)` to avoid killing unrelated processes. The full test suite will run in this order: sim combat, sim world, playthrough, UI audio checks, browser tests, and hub tests. Each browser launch with software WebGPU may take two to four minutes.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ca88369c-483f-4b15-9e6f-658a6adc01c7-76","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"page:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/merc/world.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ca88369c-483f-4b15-9e6f-658a6adc01c7-77","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src/merc_web.rs","offset":210,"limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":623,"output_tokens":4120,"cache_read_input_tokens":134400,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"353dee4e-37af-4231-baac-9eb7a617d6b4"}
