ext-align: center; padding: 24px; z-index: 5;\\n    background: #2a2420; }\\n  #shell { position: fixed; left: 0; top: 0; bottom: 0; width: min(440px, 42vw); z-index: 4;\\n    padding: 28px 28px 32px; box-sizing: border-box; overflow: auto;\\n    background: rgba(28, 22, 18, 0.55); color: #f4e6cf; font: 28px/1.25 system-ui, sans-serif;\\n    pointer-events: auto; }\\n  h1 { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 64px; line-height: 0.95; letter-spacing: -1px; }\\n  #tag, #notice, #about p, #card p, #results { font-size: 28px; line-height: 1.3; }\\n  #notice { color: #ffd090; }\\n  button { display: block; width: 100%; text-align: left; margin: 8px 0; padding: 12px 16px;\\n    font: 32px/1.2 system-ui, sans-serif; color: #f4e6cf; background: transparent;\\n    border: 0; border-left: 6px solid transparent; min-height: 64px; }\\n  button.on, button:focus { border-left-color: #e0703a; background: rgba(224, 112, 58, 0.18); }\\n  #settings-btn { border: 2px solid #e0703a; }\\n  #about p { margin: 0 0 18px; }\\n  #card, #results, #invite { margin-top: 12px; }\\n  #paste { width: 100%; min-height: 72px; font: 16px monospace; }\\n  #turn { display: none; }\\n</style>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n<div id=\\\"msg\\\" role=\\\"status\\\" aria-live=\\\"polite\\\">Loading Jam Cry…</div>\\n<div id=\\\"shell\\\" data-screen=\\\"Title\\\">\\n  <h1>Jam Cry</h1>\\n  <p id=\\\"tag\\\">Everything rusts. The grass burns anyway.</p>\\n  <p id=\\\"notice\\\" hidden></p>\\n  <button id=\\\"settings-btn\\\" type=\\\"button\\\" hidden>Settings</button>\\n  <div id=\\\"rows\\\"></div>\\n  <div id=\\\"about\\\" hidden></div>\\n  <div id=\\\"card\\\" hidden></div>\\n  <div id=\\\"results\\\" hidden></div>\\n  <div id=\\\"invite\\\" hidden>\\n    <button id=\\\"make-offer\\\" type=\\\"button\\\">Make code</button>\\n    <p id=\\\"offer\\\"></p>\\n    <textarea id=\\\"paste\\\" aria-label=\\\"Invite code\\\"></textarea>\\n    <button id=\\\"join-code\\\" type=\\\"button\\\">Join code</button>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n<script>\\n(function () {\\n  var pending = { look: [0, 0], move: null, axes: null, pressed: 0, act: 0, hover: null, click: false, device: null, touch: null, xbox: null, lock: false, hub: false };\\n\",\"total_lines\":421}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"b3b396a8-db49-4aec-a7ed-b6bcd0422a7b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_299","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Clicking the canvas reveals a shell covering the left 42%.\n\nClicking the canvas center avoids the shell. On a 1280px viewport the center is 640px, right of the 440px shell. On a 390px phone the shell is only 164px.\n\nPointer lock checks that the click target is the canvas itself. The full-screen message overlay disappears once the canvas loads. During play, the shell still intercepts pointer events on the left while the right side remains clickable. I will write the scripts and may disable shell pointer events during gameplay.\n\nThe shell will stay visible during play. The title screen shows the title in the shell while the 3D world renders behind it.\n\nThe canvas bitmap is the overlook camera. The scene renders even on the title screen because dress() adds the scenery.\n\nI will write a compact browser.py. The click listener will skip pointer lock on the title screen. Keyboard Enter during launch will reach the page directly.\n\nSpace key presses map correctly to the game's actFor handler. The run.sh orchestrator will parse cargo test output, counting compile errors as failures and printing only FAIL lines and a final summary.\n\nThe run.sh script will filter out \"running 5 tests\" lines. A helper function parses each cargo log to count passed and failed tests. Compile errors are treated as failures and printed.\n\nThe run.sh helper will treat zero passed tests as a failure. Audio files found will be logged as FAIL lines. Python test scripts may exit with code 1 even when multiple individual tests fail, so counting one failure per script would undercount the actual failures.\n\nPython scripts will print `FAIL` lines for each failure and nothing on success. The orchestrator counts these printed failures to keep the summary accurate.\n\nEach Python script will write pass counts to a `test-logs/counts` file. The orchestrator will sum those counts and print the final summary. This avoids mixing pass totals into stdout.\n\nThe browser.py script will print a `CHECKS` line at the end of its output. The run.sh orchestrator will grep that line from the log to count passes and filter stdout so only FAIL lines and the final summary remain visible.\n\nI will also add a note about a potential playwright import failure. To avoid earlyoom during the launch tests, I will use a 960x540 viewport for the first launch and a 1280x720 viewport for the second, documenting this in the log.\n\nI will check the WASM `block_until` behavior later. The exit-interview and PROGRESS notes will be written after the tests so they can reference real results. I will include a handin step in run.sh that checks files, but I must write the JSON first so the `all` target does not fail before it is ready.\n\nI will write the exit-interview, PROGRESS, and feedback notes now because the answers are already known. The browser tests will run next, followed by copying logs to scratch and updating the plan checkboxes. If the browser finds a real bug, I will rebuild WASM and repack before updating any about-page counts.\n\nA potential bug surfaced in the page's `msg.replaceChildren` call when WebGPU is unavailable. The loader and message element timing during WASM initialization looks acceptable.\n\nMore critically, `history.replaceState` on a `file://` URL might throw in Chrome. If it fails, the scene query parameter is lost and the game defaults to the town scene instead of Merc.\n\nThe packer bakes the scene into the loader. If `history.replaceState` fails on `file://`, the query parameter is lost and the game defaults to town. I need to verify how the engine reads the scene, and consider a fallback like a global `window.__scene` variable.\n\nI will check settings.rs to see how the engine selects the scene and whether it can read a JavaScript hook. Chrome may allow replaceState on file:// URLs, but if it fails I can patch the loader or update the engine to read a global window variable.\n\nThe loader only warns if `history.replaceState` fails, so the scene parameter is lost. I will patch the engine to read a global `window.__jamScene` variable as a fallback. Editing the packer is acceptable because it is an engine change.\n\nThe packer inserts the loader and WASM before the closing body tag. The game module script is deferred, so it runs after the loader finishes. This means a global fallback variable set by the loader will be visible to the engine when it initializes.\n\nThe deferred game module runs after the loader, so it can intercept a failed `replaceState` and fix the URL or set a global variable before `loadGame` starts the WASM. This provides a JavaScript-side fallback without editing the packer.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e849749a-598a-449b-b9e0-9557d68b5b3f-84","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"scene|search_params|location\\(\\)","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_app/src/settings.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1192,"output_tokens":2739,"cache_read_input_tokens":145664,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"3b1a71ee-8267-4936-8d15-c45c931267e3"}
