{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_364","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"hub.py's only child is http.\n\nNo Playwright node process is visible under the hub.py parent. The Python process may be stuck reading the HTTP server's stdout and never reached the \"Serving HTTP\" line.\n\nThe HTTP server prints to stderr by default, but stderr is redirected to stdout. The \"Serving HTTP\" message should therefore be available in the pipe. The readline loop might still be blocking.\n\nThe HTTP server's stderr is block-buffered when not attached to a TTY. The \"Serving HTTP\" message therefore never reaches the pipe, so readline() blocks forever and the parent process never reaches its 10-second deadline check.\n\nThe fix is to run the HTTP server with unbuffered output, for example by adding `-u` to `python3 -m http.server` or setting `PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1`. The verification plan already specifies using that exact command.\n\nThe fix will set `PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1` in the Popen environment rather than changing the command arguments. A non-blocking read is also being considered to ensure the parent fails within 10 seconds if the banner never appears.\n\nOnly the two processes started by this run will be killed: hub.py (PID 648002) and the HTTP server (PID 648003). Other http.server instances will remain untouched. The shell script launching hub.py is a background task, so killing the parent should let it exit cleanly.\n\nSIGKILLing hub.py would prevent its finally block from terminating the HTTP server, so both PIDs must be killed. The fix will be applied to hub.py and the script rerun. A short select timeout will also be added to readline to fail quickly if buffering issues occur.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The hub server never flushed its port line, so the test has been blocked on that read. I’ll stop only that server and fix the wait."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7803efcb-3f7e-436e-bdf1-f0d15c