{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_395","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7311e933-616d-49f9-8283-1952f11c0335-2","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/STAGE.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7311e933-616d-49f9-8283-1952f11c0335-3","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/TASK.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7311e933-616d-49f9-8283-1952f11c0335-4","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91%2Fwork/01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558/goal/plan.md"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3698,"output_tokens":255,"cache_read_input_tokens":22528,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"b31a8d67-be3a-4b89-87a4-b071966bf332"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7311e933-616d-49f9-8283-1952f11c0335-2","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/STAGE.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7311e933-616d-49f9-8283-1952f11c0335-3","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Far Cry 2\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:35:03\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Far Cry 2\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:35:03\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"SOURCE: Far Cry 2\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:35:03\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7311e933-616d-49f9-8283-1952f11c0335-4","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person survival shooter inspired by Far Cry 2, and leave TASK.md's hand-ins in this folder.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in and original identity.** The work folder (the one that holds `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`) contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML, and exactly one self-contained game page named `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html`. `{GameTitle}` is the title picked by brainstorming 50 playful riffs and then a real RNG (the 50 names and the RNG output are saved). A dev subfolder with that same stem holds the project. The page opens from `file://` (no silent blank from module CORS). The title screen shows that title. In-fiction names, places, factions, and dialogue do not copy the source: the strings Jackal, UFLL, APR, Pala, Mosate, Bowa-Seko, Leboa-Sako, Reuben Oluwagembi, and Mike's Bar, and the source's playable mercs or buddies, do not appear, and the words \\\"Far Cry 2\\\" appear only in the title-riff notes, the About/Credits source line, and the exit-interview source fields. Shipped text and behavior have no TODOs, TBDs, placeholders, lorem, or stubbed features. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in TASK.md's exact shape, with `usage` equal to `added on import` and every other value filled (`unknown` only where it cannot be confirmed). The in-game About/Credits screen (paged, large type) includes every Source Information field from TASK.md plus the Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback answers in question order.\\n\\n2. **First person, guns that fall apart, fever, and actions in the hands.** From a fresh simulation (not a mid-fight snapshot), the player looks and moves in first person on foot: stance changes eye and capsule height, stick move follows yaw, and mouse-look changes yaw and pitch. Hitscan shots from the camera apply head ×3, torso ×1, and limb ×0.6, and solid walls block them. The loadout is one sidearm, one primary, one special, plus throwables and a machete, with the kinds of weapons PLAN.md §5.3 names (including igniting flare, rocket, flamethrower, mortar, molotov, and a silent dart). Each weapon has condition: wear, wider spread as condition falls, jam chance 0 above 70 and otherwise `0.22 × ((70 − c) / 70)²` (about 1 in 18 at c = 35 over a large seeded sample), a clear-jam action that blocks firing until it finishes, and a break-apart below the low threshold. Enemy guns use the same jam rule and that enemy stops firing to clear it. Health, a syrette, and six distinct wound treatments restore the amounts PLAN.md §5.5 states, and the treatment matches the cause. A hidden fever warns, then impairs aim and movement until a pill clears it and resets the timer; with no pill the player blacks out, wakes at a safe house minus a diamond fee, and it is not a game over. The map, GPS, and monocular are physical first-person props (no HUD minimap); a waypoint is set on the map and read from the GPS.\\n10→\\n3. **Wind-driven fire, the country, vehicles, posts, and stealth.** The playable map is a 2048 m × 2048 m open country (dry grass, laterite roads, a river, settlements from PLAN.md §6.3) with a day-night cycle. Fire is deterministic and wind-driven: at 8 m/s, downwind reach after 60 s is at least 3× upwind reach; calm spread is roughly round; roads, water, rock, and cut cells stop spread except ember jumps; burnt cells do not re-burn until they regrow; rain extinguishes; barrels, fuel tanks, and burning people can ignite and chain; a large burn's saved state stays sparse. Vehicles (technical with a mounted gun, jeep, supply truck) pitch and roll on slopes, take damage stages, can be repaired, and explode into grass fires; a boat floats and stops in the shallows; an NPC can drive a road. Guard posts shoot on sight outside the ceasefire, stay clear for their re-man timer, and never re-man while the player can see them. Inside the ceasefire town, weapons cannot be raised, so civilians cannot be shot. Detection is lower when crouched in tall grass at night than when standing on a road at noon; noise draws investigators; dense smoke blocks sight.\\n\\n4. **Vertical slice, companions, three endings, saves, coverage.** Every contract row in PLAN.md §11 is playable, including both paymasters' jobs, a companion's alternate approach, the ceasefire breaking, the town burning, and the finale. The player chooses among three endings: burn the ledger, hold the mast while the names are read, or take the chair. A seeded bot, from a new game on normal difficulty, using only normal inputs and actions (bus travel allowed; no cheats and no teleport), reaches each ending. Across those runs, every coverage flag named in PLAN.md §15 is set by real play (union across runs is enough where one run cannot both save and lose the same companion or see every ending). A living companion rescues the downed player once per rest and the player stands at about half health; a downed companion can be patched or given a mercy overdose; a death stays dead across save and load. A save written from `file://` restores contract state, inventory and condition, discoveries, time, wind, fever timer, companions, post timers, the sparse burn map, diamonds, and infamy. Instance counts may follow PLAN.md §1.5's cut order (fewer caches, posts, or side jobs; tapes at least 4). Missing a system, a contract row, or an ending is a failure.\\n\\n5. **10-foot shell, code-only audio, attract, dev mode, and multiplayer.** Keyboard and mouse use the same screens as a standard-mapping gamepad, with prompts for the last device (PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox A B X Y in Settings). Mouse hover moves focus and click confirms. No screen has more than 7 focusable rows or a dense table. Touch-only shows the exact notice \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button, shows no touch controls, and still runs attract mode. After 10 seconds without input on the title or shell menus, a looping attract demo starts in the real world, reaches every scripted segment, writes no save, and any input exits it. Dev mode, opened from the pause menu and absent in a hub session, includes cheats, teleport, spawn, world controls, and live sliders for the balance variables in PLAN.md §8.7, each with reset to defaults. All music, sound effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no shipped wav, mp3, or ogg). Voices are syllable gibberish, not recorded speech. Music defaults to 10% volume (offline music-bus RMS at default ÷ RMS at full is 0.10 ± 0.01). Hub play follows the ai-hub session client: that script is pasted unmodified as the first script after the charset meta; the `hub:session*` metas from PLAN.md §7.1 are present; a session jumps straight into the shared 1–4 player depot contract (burn the tanks, carry the ledger, extract together) with host-authoritative fire, AI, and damage; the four harness cases in PLAN.md §14.4 pass, including server mode with no local player; when no hub is present the solo title game runs and shared-world code does not consume `Math.random`. The same round is playable on two local pages via BroadcastChannel and via pasted WebRTC codes, and no `RTCPeerConnection` is created while a hub session is active. One test command prints only failures and a final one-line count, and a full run ends with zero failures.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Confirm the HTML path, dev subfolder, `PROGRESS.md`, and title notes. Parse `exit-interview.json` and assert TASK.md's keys, array lengths (testing 4, engine 6, prompt 4), and `usage == \\\"added on import\\\"`. Search shipped game strings for the forbidden proper names and for TODO, TBD, placeholder, and lorem. Capture the command output to `{SCRATCH}/handin.log`. Pass only if the files exist, JSON parses, and the searches are clean outside the allowed source lines.\\n2. **gating:** Run the shipped native simulation tests (the game's own test binary, starting each case from a fresh boot) for look and movement, hitscan zones and wall blocking, jam rate, jam clear, weapon break, enemy jam, syrette and wound types, fever warning, pill clear, and no-pill blackout with a diamond fee. Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/sim-combat.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass and the log shows zero failures for this group.\\n20→3. **gating:** Run the shipped native tests for fire (8 m/s downwind ≥ 3× upwind at 60 s, breaks, regrowth, rain, chain ignition, sparse snapshot), vehicles (slope, boat shallows, repair, explode-and-ignite, NPC driver), detection (grass, night, posture, smoke, noise), and guard-post re-man rules including \\\"not while visible\\\" and town holster. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/sim-world.log`. Pass only if each of those outcomes is asserted by the run, not by a re-implementation.\\n4. **gating:** Run the shipped bot from a new game through each of the three endings on normal difficulty, then assert the PLAN.md §15 coverage-flag union, companion rescue, patch, overdose, permadeath across save and load, and a file-backed save round trip of the §5.21 fields. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/playthrough.log`. Pass only if the log shows all three endings reached and zero failures. This run must be deterministic enough that a second run of the same seeds also passes.\\n5. **gating:** Run the shipped UI and offline-audio tests: focus wrap and a max of 7 rows, glyphs follow device and the Xbox setting, attract starts at 10 s of idle and not before, any input exits, dev sliders change tunables and reset restores defaults, music RMS ratio 0.10 ± 0.01, voice output non-silent and different per speaker, no NaNs. Also search the dev tree for wav, mp3, and ogg files belonging to the game. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ui-audio.log`. Pass only if those checks pass and no game audio files are found.\\n6. **gating:** Launch the real HTML from `file://` with Playwright Chromium more than once (two separate runs), using the engine WebGPU flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader` (do not use `--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`). Each run: zero page errors; the canvas drawing size equals the viewport requested for that run; the frame is substantially filled (painted fraction high or a painted bbox covering most of the surface, not a clear buffer); the visible title or the published UI state equals `{GameTitle}`; keyboard input (the Enter, Space, Enter, Space solo path) changes that state. Save screenshots and console logs to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must succeed. A readback that returns a blank image is a failure to fix. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-fail.log` and treat criteria 2–5's native tests plus a static check that the page contains a browser entry (not a Node-only bundle) as the launch bar.\\n7. **gating:** On that same page, drive the shipped browser hooks: gamepad stub flips prompts to PlayStation glyphs, the Xbox setting changes them, pointer-lock mousemove changes yaw, losing the lock opens pause, a phone profile shows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and Settings with attract running and no touch controls, WebKit (no WebGPU) shows a clear message instead of a blank page, dev mode toggles a cheat and a slider visible in published status, save then Continue works from `file://`, published music gain is 0.10, and About text contains the Source Information fields and all three feedback sections. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\\n8. **gating:** Serve the real page with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, confirm the fetched `<title>` is this game, run PLAN.md §14.4's four harness URLs, and stop only that server PID. Pass only if `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true for each case. Then play the standalone round on two pages through BroadcastChannel and through a pasted WebRTC code to the results screen, and assert the solo page does not mark the hub client active and does not use `Math.random` for shared-world state. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n9. **gating:** Run the one full test command (expected path `{dev}/tests/run.sh all`) after the code it covers is finished. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/run-all.log`. Pass only if it prints failures (none) and one summary line whose failure count is 0.\\n10. **evidence:** 1920×1080 screenshots of golden hour on the plain, the depot fire, a weapon viewmodel, the title, and the dev balance page, written under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to judge couch-readable type and that the scene reads as the sun-bleached style. Absence of a shot does not fail the goal when steps 1–9 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n30→- Copying Far Cry 2's map, mission graph, 50 km² size, malaria journal, real factions, or proper names. The inspired systems are the ones PLAN.md specifies, not a scene-for-scene port.\\n- Hang gliders, player-versus-player modes, an in-game map editor, and character customisation beyond the four-merc pick.\\n- FPS targets, draw-call optimisation, and other performance work TASK.md defers.\\n- A physical DualSense, a real iPhone or iPad, a TV, a second human, or audible speakers. Stub the pad, use a phone profile and WebKit, judge 1920×1080 shots, use two local pages, and measure audio offline or with an analyser.\\n- Extra modes or systems not in PLAN.md §5–§11 (for example a cure for the fever, or a minimap).\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: the folder that contains `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`. All new files stay there. Do not read or write sibling agent folders or shared `/tmp` project files.\\n- Dev subfolder `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/`, containing a shardfall engine clone (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall) and the new game, plus downloaded ai-hub files from https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub (`session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`).\\n- Final page and `exit-interview.json` in the work root. `PROGRESS.md` in the work root. Title notes and feedback notes inside the dev folder.\\n40→- Toolchain already on this machine: Rust and wasm32 target, `wasm-bindgen` 0.2.129, Python Playwright 1.63, Chromium, Firefox, WebKit. `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS` at most 12. `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` stays inside the dev folder. No `playwright install`. No `pkill`, `killall`, or `fuser -k`.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md as the design (milestones M0–M11, data in TOML, pure seeded simulation separate from rendering, UI, and the page). Verifiers judge the criteria above, not a matching directory tree or identical type names. Keep rules `Clone`-able and free of hashmap iteration order and wall-clock randomness so two runs of the same seed agree. Build the campaign bot beside each contract so the playthrough test stays cheap. Put numbers in data files. Expose game status and a test-command queue on the page for browser checks, and a no-draw switch so browser tests are not limited by software-rendered frame rate. Pack one HTML that runs from `file://` and still loads the unmodified session client first. Track answers for the feedback questions while working so About and `exit-interview.json` are filled from real notes, not blanks.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Clone the engine into the dev subfolder, pick the title with 50 names plus a real RNG, save the notes, register the game scene, place unmodified `session.js` first, and make the single test command print a one-line count.\\n- [x] Add first-person look and movement, the controller and keyboard layouts, a held weapon with the wind rag, hitscan, and a sky, and pass the combat sim tests.\\n- [x] Add the 2048 m country, wind-driven fire, weapon condition and jams, healing and fever, stealth and guard posts, and drivable vehicles and the boat; pass the world sim tests.\\n- [x] Add companions, every PLAN.md §11 contract, three endings, saves, and a seeded bot playthrough that sets every §15 coverage flag.\\n50→- [x] Add code-only music, effects, and gibberish voices with music at 10%, plus the 10-foot shell, dev-mode sliders, attract mode, touch-only notice, and About/Credits.\\n- [x] Add the hub depot round and standalone two-page play; pass the four harness cases and the solo check.\\n- [x] Pack `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html` into the work root, write `exit-interview.json` and `PROGRESS.md`, run the full test command and the file:// launch twice, and store the captured logs.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Fire advances from the downwind tips and the calm extremes, not a full neighbor flood; the country is a seeded height and fuel field shown through a dressed overlook, not a streamed terrain mesh; the 10-foot menus are the page shell, not an egui panel.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- Software WebGPU on this host is slow, and the wrong Chromium flags (`--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`) leave a blank canvas that still reports frames. Use the flags in verification step 6. A blank screenshot from a successful readback is an app failure. If the browser process cannot start, step 6's written fallback applies. earlyoom kills the largest browser under memory pressure; keep a single browser and stop it by PID.\\n- PLAN.md §1.5 allows cutting instance counts, never systems. Criterion 4 follows that. It is not a license to drop fire, jams, fever, companions, a contract row, or an ending.\\n60→- TASK.md also asks for the Source Information and the three feedback sections in the implementer's chat reply. The checkable copies are About/Credits and `exit-interview.json`; the reply must repeat them, and verifiers do not fail the goal only because they cannot see the chat.\\n- The hub harness needs a local server. Solo play must still work from `file://`. Do not treat a server-only page as meeting criterion 1.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person survival shooter inspired by Far Cry 2, and leave TASK.md's hand-ins in this folder.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in and original identity.** The work folder (the one that holds `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`) contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML, and exactly one self-contained game page named `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html`. `{GameTitle}` is the title picked by brainstorming 50 playful riffs and then a real RNG (the 50 names and the RNG output are saved). A dev subfolder with that same stem holds the project. The page opens from `file://` (no silent blank from module CORS). The title screen shows that title. In-fiction names, places, factions, and dialogue do not copy the source: the strings Jackal, UFLL, APR, Pala, Mosate, Bowa-Seko, Leboa-Sako, Reuben Oluwagembi, and Mike's Bar, and the source's playable mercs or buddies, do not appear, and the words \\\"Far Cry 2\\\" appear only in the title-riff notes, the About/Credits source line, and the exit-interview source fields. Shipped text and behavior have no TODOs, TBDs, placeholders, lorem, or stubbed features. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in TASK.md's exact shape, with `usage` equal to `added on import` and every other value filled (`unknown` only where it cannot be confirmed). The in-game About/Credits screen (paged, large type) includes every Source Information field from TASK.md plus the Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback answers in question order.\\n\\n2. **First person, guns that fall apart, fever, and actions in the hands.** From a fresh simulation (not a mid-fight snapshot), the player looks and moves in first person on foot: stance changes eye and capsule height, stick move follows yaw, and mouse-look changes yaw and pitch. Hitscan shots from the camera apply head ×3, torso ×1, and limb ×0.6, and solid walls block them. The loadout is one sidearm, one primary, one special, plus throwables and a machete, with the kinds of weapons PLAN.md §5.3 names (including igniting flare, rocket, flamethrower, mortar, molotov, and a silent dart). Each weapon has condition: wear, wider spread as condition falls, jam chance 0 above 70 and otherwise `0.22 × ((70 − c) / 70)²` (about 1 in 18 at c = 35 over a large seeded sample), a clear-jam action that blocks firing until it finishes, and a break-apart below the low threshold. Enemy guns use the same jam rule and that enemy stops firing to clear it. Health, a syrette, and six distinct wound treatments restore the amounts PLAN.md §5.5 states, and the treatment matches the cause. A hidden fever warns, then impairs aim and movement until a pill clears it and resets the timer; with no pill the player blacks out, wakes at a safe house minus a diamond fee, and it is not a game over. The map, GPS, and monocular are physical first-person props (no HUD minimap); a waypoint is set on the map and read from the GPS.\\n10→\\n3. **Wind-driven fire, the country, vehicles, posts, and stealth.** The playable map is a 2048 m × 2048 m open country (dry grass, laterite roads, a river, settlements from PLAN.md §6.3) with a day-night cycle. Fire is deterministic and wind-driven: at 8 m/s, downwind reach after 60 s is at least 3× upwind reach; calm spread is roughly round; roads, water, rock, and cut cells stop spread except ember jumps; burnt cells do not re-burn until they regrow; rain extinguishes; barrels, fuel tanks, and burning people can ignite and chain; a large burn's saved state stays sparse. Vehicles (technical with a mounted gun, jeep, supply truck) pitch and roll on slopes, take damage stages, can be repaired, and explode into grass fires; a boat floats and stops in the shallows; an NPC can drive a road. Guard posts shoot on sight outside the ceasefire, stay clear for their re-man timer, and never re-man while the player can see them. Inside the ceasefire town, weapons cannot be raised, so civilians cannot be shot. Detection is lower when crouched in tall grass at night than when standing on a road at noon; noise draws investigators; dense smoke blocks sight.\\n\\n4. **Vertical slice, companions, three endings, saves, coverage.** Every contract row in PLAN.md §11 is playable, including both paymasters' jobs, a companion's alternate approach, the ceasefire breaking, the town burning, and the finale. The player chooses among three endings: burn the ledger, hold the mast while the names are read, or take the chair. A seeded bot, from a new game on normal difficulty, using only normal inputs and actions (bus travel allowed; no cheats and no teleport), reaches each ending. Across those runs, every coverage flag named in PLAN.md §15 is set by real play (union across runs is enough where one run cannot both save and lose the same companion or see every ending). A living companion rescues the downed player once per rest and the player stands at about half health; a downed companion can be patched or given a mercy overdose; a death stays dead across save and load. A save written from `file://` restores contract state, inventory and condition, discoveries, time, wind, fever timer, companions, post timers, the sparse burn map, diamonds, and infamy. Instance counts may follow PLAN.md §1.5's cut order (fewer caches, posts, or side jobs; tapes at least 4). Missing a system, a contract row, or an ending is a failure.\\n\\n5. **10-foot shell, code-only audio, attract, dev mode, and multiplayer.** Keyboard and mouse use the same screens as a standard-mapping gamepad, with prompts for the last device (PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox A B X Y in Settings). Mouse hover moves focus and click confirms. No screen has more than 7 focusable rows or a dense table. Touch-only shows the exact notice \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button, shows no touch controls, and still runs attract mode. After 10 seconds without input on the title or shell menus, a looping attract demo starts in the real world, reaches every scripted segment, writes no save, and any input exits it. Dev mode, opened from the pause menu and absent in a hub session, includes cheats, teleport, spawn, world controls, and live sliders for the balance variables in PLAN.md §8.7, each with reset to defaults. All music, sound effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no shipped wav, mp3, or ogg). Voices are syllable gibberish, not recorded speech. Music defaults to 10% volume (offline music-bus RMS at default ÷ RMS at full is 0.10 ± 0.01). Hub play follows the ai-hub session client: that script is pasted unmodified as the first script after the charset meta; the `hub:session*` metas from PLAN.md §7.1 are present; a session jumps straight into the shared 1–4 player depot contract (burn the tanks, carry the ledger, extract together) with host-authoritative fire, AI, and damage; the four harness cases in PLAN.md §14.4 pass, including server mode with no local player; when no hub is present the solo title game runs and shared-world code does not consume `Math.random`. The same round is playable on two local pages via BroadcastChannel and via pasted WebRTC codes, and no `RTCPeerConnection` is created while a hub session is active. One test command prints only failures and a final one-line count, and a full run ends with zero failures.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Confirm the HTML path, dev subfolder, `PROGRESS.md`, and title notes. Parse `exit-interview.json` and assert TASK.md's keys, array lengths (testing 4, engine 6, prompt 4), and `usage == \\\"added on import\\\"`. Search shipped game strings for the forbidden proper names and for TODO, TBD, placeholder, and lorem. Capture the command output to `{SCRATCH}/handin.log`. Pass only if the files exist, JSON parses, and the searches are clean outside the allowed source lines.\\n2. **gating:** Run the shipped native simulation tests (the game's own test binary, starting each case from a fresh boot) for look and movement, hitscan zones and wall blocking, jam rate, jam clear, weapon break, enemy jam, syrette and wound types, fever warning, pill clear, and no-pill blackout with a diamond fee. Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/sim-combat.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass and the log shows zero failures for this group.\\n20→3. **gating:** Run the shipped native tests for fire (8 m/s downwind ≥ 3× upwind at 60 s, breaks, regrowth, rain, chain ignition, sparse snapshot), vehicles (slope, boat shallows, repair, explode-and-ignite, NPC driver), detection (grass, night, posture, smoke, noise), and guard-post re-man rules including \\\"not while visible\\\" and town holster. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/sim-world.log`. Pass only if each of those outcomes is asserted by the run, not by a re-implementation.\\n4. **gating:** Run the shipped bot from a new game through each of the three endings on normal difficulty, then assert the PLAN.md §15 coverage-flag union, companion rescue, patch, overdose, permadeath across save and load, and a file-backed save round trip of the §5.21 fields. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/playthrough.log`. Pass only if the log shows all three endings reached and zero failures. This run must be deterministic enough that a second run of the same seeds also passes.\\n5. **gating:** Run the shipped UI and offline-audio tests: focus wrap and a max of 7 rows, glyphs follow device and the Xbox setting, attract starts at 10 s of idle and not before, any input exits, dev sliders change tunables and reset restores defaults, music RMS ratio 0.10 ± 0.01, voice output non-silent and different per speaker, no NaNs. Also search the dev tree for wav, mp3, and ogg files belonging to the game. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ui-audio.log`. Pass only if those checks pass and no game audio files are found.\\n6. **gating:** Launch the real HTML from `file://` with Playwright Chromium more than once (two separate runs), using the engine WebGPU flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader` (do not use `--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`). Each run: zero page errors; the canvas drawing size equals the viewport requested for that run; the frame is substantially filled (painted fraction high or a painted bbox covering most of the surface, not a clear buffer); the visible title or the published UI state equals `{GameTitle}`; keyboard input (the Enter, Space, Enter, Space solo path) changes that state. Save screenshots and console logs to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must succeed. A readback that returns a blank image is a failure to fix. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-fail.log` and treat criteria 2–5's native tests plus a static check that the page contains a browser entry (not a Node-only bundle) as the launch bar.\\n7. **gating:** On that same page, drive the shipped browser hooks: gamepad stub flips prompts to PlayStation glyphs, the Xbox setting changes them, pointer-lock mousemove changes yaw, losing the lock opens pause, a phone profile shows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and Settings with attract running and no touch controls, WebKit (no WebGPU) shows a clear message instead of a blank page, dev mode toggles a cheat and a slider visible in published status, save then Continue works from `file://`, published music gain is 0.10, and About text contains the Source Information fields and all three feedback sections. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\\n8. **gating:** Serve the real page with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, confirm the fetched `<title>` is this game, run PLAN.md §14.4's four harness URLs, and stop only that server PID. Pass only if `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true for each case. Then play the standalone round on two pages through BroadcastChannel and through a pasted WebRTC code to the results screen, and assert the solo page does not mark the hub client active and does not use `Math.random` for shared-world state. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n9. **gating:** Run the one full test command (expected path `{dev}/tests/run.sh all`) after the code it covers is finished. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/run-all.log`. Pass only if it prints failures (none) and one summary line whose failure count is 0.\\n10. **evidence:** 1920×1080 screenshots of golden hour on the plain, the depot fire, a weapon viewmodel, the title, and the dev balance page, written under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to judge couch-readable type and that the scene reads as the sun-bleached style. Absence of a shot does not fail the goal when steps 1–9 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n30→- Copying Far Cry 2's map, mission graph, 50 km² size, malaria journal, real factions, or proper names. The inspired systems are the ones PLAN.md specifies, not a scene-for-scene port.\\n- Hang gliders, player-versus-player modes, an in-game map editor, and character customisation beyond the four-merc pick.\\n- FPS targets, draw-call optimisation, and other performance work TASK.md defers.\\n- A physical DualSense, a real iPhone or iPad, a TV, a second human, or audible speakers. Stub the pad, use a phone profile and WebKit, judge 1920×1080 shots, use two local pages, and measure audio offline or with an analyser.\\n- Extra modes or systems not in PLAN.md §5–§11 (for example a cure for the fever, or a minimap).\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: the folder that contains `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`. All new files stay there. Do not read or write sibling agent folders or shared `/tmp` project files.\\n- Dev subfolder `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/`, containing a shardfall engine clone (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall) and the new game, plus downloaded ai-hub files from https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub (`session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`).\\n- Final page and `exit-interview.json` in the work root. `PROGRESS.md` in the work root. Title notes and feedback notes inside the dev folder.\\n40→- Toolchain already on this machine: Rust and wasm32 target, `wasm-bindgen` 0.2.129, Python Playwright 1.63, Chromium, Firefox, WebKit. `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS` at most 12. `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` stays inside the dev folder. No `playwright install`. No `pkill`, `killall`, or `fuser -k`.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md as the design (milestones M0–M11, data in TOML, pure seeded simulation separate from rendering, UI, and the page). Verifiers judge the criteria above, not a matching directory tree or identical type names. Keep rules `Clone`-able and free of hashmap iteration order and wall-clock randomness so two runs of the same seed agree. Build the campaign bot beside each contract so the playthrough test stays cheap. Put numbers in data files. Expose game status and a test-command queue on the page for browser checks, and a no-draw switch so browser tests are not limited by software-rendered frame rate. Pack one HTML that runs from `file://` and still loads the unmodified session client first. Track answers for the feedback questions while working so About and `exit-interview.json` are filled from real notes, not blanks.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Clone the engine into the dev subfolder, pick the title with 50 names plus a real RNG, save the notes, register the game scene, place unmodified `session.js` first, and make the single test command print a one-line count.\\n- [x] Add first-person look and movement, the controller and keyboard layouts, a held weapon with the wind rag, hitscan, and a sky, and pass the combat sim tests.\\n- [x] Add the 2048 m country, wind-driven fire, weapon condition and jams, healing and fever, stealth and guard posts, and drivable vehicles and the boat; pass the world sim tests.\\n- [x] Add companions, every PLAN.md §11 contract, three endings, saves, and a seeded bot playthrough that sets every §15 coverage flag.\\n50→- [x] Add code-only music, effects, and gibberish voices with music at 10%, plus the 10-foot shell, dev-mode sliders, attract mode, touch-only notice, and About/Credits.\\n- [x] Add the hub depot round and standalone two-page play; pass the four harness cases and the solo check.\\n- [x] Pack `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html` into the work root, write `exit-interview.json` and `PROGRESS.md`, run the full test command and the file:// launch twice, and store the captured logs.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Fire advances from the downwind tips and the calm extremes, not a full neighbor flood; the country is a seeded height and fuel field shown through a dressed overlook, not a streamed terrain mesh; the 10-foot menus are the page shell, not an egui panel.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- Software WebGPU on this host is slow, and the wrong Chromium flags (`--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`) leave a blank canvas that still reports frames. Use the flags in verification step 6. A blank screenshot from a successful readback is an app failure. If the browser process cannot start, step 6's written fallback applies. earlyoom kills the largest browser under memory pressure; keep a single browser and stop it by PID.\\n- PLAN.md §1.5 allows cutting instance counts, never systems. Criterion 4 follows that. It is not a license to drop fire, jams, fever, companions, a contract row, or an ending.\\n60→- TASK.md also asks for the Source Information and the three feedback sections in the implementer's chat reply. The checkable copies are About/Credits and `exit-interview.json`; the reply must repeat them, and verifiers do not fail the goal only because they cannot see the chat.\\n- The hub harness needs a local server. Solo play must still work from `file://`. Do not treat a server-only page as meeting criterion 1.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91%2Fwork/01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md's original first-person survival shooter inspired by Far Cry 2, and leave TASK.md's hand-ins in this folder.\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in and original identity.** The work folder (the one that holds `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`) contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML, and exactly one self-contained game page named `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html`. `{GameTitle}` is the title picked by brainstorming 50 playful riffs and then a real RNG (the 50 names and the RNG output are saved). A dev subfolder with that same stem holds the project. The page opens from `file://` (no silent blank from module CORS). The title screen shows that title. In-fiction names, places, factions, and dialogue do not copy the source: the strings Jackal, UFLL, APR, Pala, Mosate, Bowa-Seko, Leboa-Sako, Reuben Oluwagembi, and Mike's Bar, and the source's playable mercs or buddies, do not appear, and the words \\\"Far Cry 2\\\" appear only in the title-riff notes, the About/Credits source line, and the exit-interview source fields. Shipped text and behavior have no TODOs, TBDs, placeholders, lorem, or stubbed features. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in TASK.md's exact shape, with `usage` equal to `added on import` and every other value filled (`unknown` only where it cannot be confirmed). The in-game About/Credits screen (paged, large type) includes every Source Information field from TASK.md plus the Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback answers in question order.\\n\\n2. **First person, guns that fall apart, fever, and actions in the hands.** From a fresh simulation (not a mid-fight snapshot), the player looks and moves in first person on foot: stance changes eye and capsule height, stick move follows yaw, and mouse-look changes yaw and pitch. Hitscan shots from the camera apply head ×3, torso ×1, and limb ×0.6, and solid walls block them. The loadout is one sidearm, one primary, one special, plus throwables and a machete, with the kinds of weapons PLAN.md §5.3 names (including igniting flare, rocket, flamethrower, mortar, molotov, and a silent dart). Each weapon has condition: wear, wider spread as condition falls, jam chance 0 above 70 and otherwise `0.22 × ((70 − c) / 70)²` (about 1 in 18 at c = 35 over a large seeded sample), a clear-jam action that blocks firing until it finishes, and a break-apart below the low threshold. Enemy guns use the same jam rule and that enemy stops firing to clear it. Health, a syrette, and six distinct wound treatments restore the amounts PLAN.md §5.5 states, and the treatment matches the cause. A hidden fever warns, then impairs aim and movement until a pill clears it and resets the timer; with no pill the player blacks out, wakes at a safe house minus a diamond fee, and it is not a game over. The map, GPS, and monocular are physical first-person props (no HUD minimap); a waypoint is set on the map and read from the GPS.\\n\\n3. **Wind-driven fire, the country, vehicles, posts, and stealth.** The playable map is a 2048 m × 2048 m open country (dry grass, laterite roads, a river, settlements from PLAN.md §6.3) with a day-night cycle. Fire is deterministic and wind-driven: at 8 m/s, downwind reach after 60 s is at least 3× upwind reach; calm spread is roughly round; roads, water, rock, and cut cells stop spread except ember jumps; burnt cells do not re-burn until they regrow; rain extinguishes; barrels, fuel tanks, and burning people can ignite and chain; a large burn's saved state stays sparse. Vehicles (technical with a mounted gun, jeep, supply truck) pitch and roll on slopes, take damage stages, can be repaired, and explode into grass fires; a boat floats and stops in the shallows; an NPC can drive a road. Guard posts shoot on sight outside the ceasefire, stay clear for their re-man timer, and never re-man while the player can see them. Inside the ceasefire town, weapons cannot be raised, so civilians cannot be shot. Detection is lower when crouched in tall grass at night than when standing on a road at noon; noise draws investigators; dense smoke blocks sight.\\n\\n4. **Vertical slice, companions, three endings, saves, coverage.** Every contract row in PLAN.md §11 is playable, including both paymasters' jobs, a companion's alternate approach, the ceasefire breaking, the town burning, and the finale. The player chooses among three endings: burn the ledger, hold the mast while the names are read, or take the chair. A seeded bot, from a new game on normal difficulty, using only normal inputs and actions (bus travel allowed; no cheats and no teleport), reaches each ending. Across those runs, every coverage flag named in PLAN.md §15 is set by real play (union across runs is enough where one run cannot both save and lose the same companion or see every ending). A living companion rescues the downed player once per rest and the player stands at about half health; a downed companion can be patched or given a mercy overdose; a death stays dead across save and load. A save written from `file://` restores contract state, inventory and condition, discoveries, time, wind, fever timer, companions, post timers, the sparse burn map, diamonds, and infamy. Instance counts may follow PLAN.md §1.5's cut order (fewer caches, posts, or side jobs; tapes at least 4). Missing a system, a contract row, or an ending is a failure.\\n\\n5. **10-foot shell, code-only audio, attract, dev mode, and multiplayer.** Keyboard and mouse use the same screens as a standard-mapping gamepad, with prompts for the last device (PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox A B X Y in Settings). Mouse hover moves focus and click confirms. No screen has more than 7 focusable rows or a dense table. Touch-only shows the exact notice \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button, shows no touch controls, and still runs attract mode. After 10 seconds without input on the title or shell menus, a looping attract demo starts in the real world, reaches every scripted segment, writes no save, and any input exits it. Dev mode, opened from the pause menu and absent in a hub session, includes cheats, teleport, spawn, world controls, and live sliders for the balance variables in PLAN.md §8.7, each with reset to defaults. All music, sound effects, and voices are synthesized in code (no shipped wav, mp3, or ogg). Voices are syllable gibberish, not recorded speech. Music defaults to 10% volume (offline music-bus RMS at default ÷ RMS at full is 0.10 ± 0.01). Hub play follows the ai-hub session client: that script is pasted unmodified as the first script after the charset meta; the `hub:session*` metas from PLAN.md §7.1 are present; a session jumps straight into the shared 1–4 player depot contract (burn the tanks, carry the ledger, extract together) with host-authoritative fire, AI, and damage; the four harness cases in PLAN.md §14.4 pass, including server mode with no local player; when no hub is present the solo title game runs and shared-world code does not consume `Math.random`. The same round is playable on two local pages via BroadcastChannel and via pasted WebRTC codes, and no `RTCPeerConnection` is created while a hub session is active. One test command prints only failures and a final one-line count, and a full run ends with zero failures.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Confirm the HTML path, dev subfolder, `PROGRESS.md`, and title notes. Parse `exit-interview.json` and assert TASK.md's keys, array lengths (testing 4, engine 6, prompt 4), and `usage == \\\"added on import\\\"`. Search shipped game strings for the forbidden proper names and for TODO, TBD, placeholder, and lorem. Capture the command output to `{SCRATCH}/handin.log`. Pass only if the files exist, JSON parses, and the searches are clean outside the allowed source lines.\\n2. **gating:** Run the shipped native simulation tests (the game's own test binary, starting each case from a fresh boot) for look and movement, hitscan zones and wall blocking, jam rate, jam clear, weapon break, enemy jam, syrette and wound types, fever warning, pill clear, and no-pill blackout with a diamond fee. Capture stdout to `{SCRATCH}/sim-combat.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass and the log shows zero failures for this group.\\n3. **gating:** Run the shipped native tests for fire (8 m/s downwind ≥ 3× upwind at 60 s, breaks, regrowth, rain, chain ignition, sparse snapshot), vehicles (slope, boat shallows, repair, explode-and-ignite, NPC driver), detection (grass, night, posture, smoke, noise), and guard-post re-man rules including \\\"not while visible\\\" and town holster. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/sim-world.log`. Pass only if each of those outcomes is asserted by the run, not by a re-implementation.\\n4. **gating:** Run the shipped bot from a new game through each of the three endings on normal difficulty, then assert the PLAN.md §15 coverage-flag union, companion rescue, patch, overdose, permadeath across save and load, and a file-backed save round trip of the §5.21 fields. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/playthrough.log`. Pass only if the log shows all three endings reached and zero failures. This run must be deterministic enough that a second run of the same seeds also passes.\\n5. **gating:** Run the shipped UI and offline-audio tests: focus wrap and a max of 7 rows, glyphs follow device and the Xbox setting, attract starts at 10 s of idle and not before, any input exits, dev sliders change tunables and reset restores defaults, music RMS ratio 0.10 ± 0.01, voice output non-silent and different per speaker, no NaNs. Also search the dev tree for wav, mp3, and ogg files belonging to the game. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ui-audio.log`. Pass only if those checks pass and no game audio files are found.\\n6. **gating:** Launch the real HTML from `file://` with Playwright Chromium more than once (two separate runs), using the engine WebGPU flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader` (do not use `--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`). Each run: zero page errors; the canvas drawing size equals the viewport requested for that run; the frame is substantially filled (painted fraction high or a painted bbox covering most of the surface, not a clear buffer); the visible title or the published UI state equals `{GameTitle}`; keyboard input (the Enter, Space, Enter, Space solo path) changes that state. Save screenshots and console logs to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must succeed. A readback that returns a blank image is a failure to fix. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-fail.log` and treat criteria 2–5's native tests plus a static check that the page contains a browser entry (not a Node-only bundle) as the launch bar.\\n7. **gating:** On that same page, drive the shipped browser hooks: gamepad stub flips prompts to PlayStation glyphs, the Xbox setting changes them, pointer-lock mousemove changes yaw, losing the lock opens pause, a phone profile shows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and Settings with attract running and no touch controls, WebKit (no WebGPU) shows a clear message instead of a blank page, dev mode toggles a cheat and a slider visible in published status, save then Continue works from `file://`, published music gain is 0.10, and About text contains the Source Information fields and all three feedback sections. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\\n8. **gating:** Serve the real page with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, confirm the fetched `<title>` is this game, run PLAN.md §14.4's four harness URLs, and stop only that server PID. Pass only if `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true for each case. Then play the standalone round on two pages through BroadcastChannel and through a pasted WebRTC code to the results screen, and assert the solo page does not mark the hub client active and does not use `Math.random` for shared-world state. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n9. **gating:** Run the one full test command (expected path `{dev}/tests/run.sh all`) after the code it covers is finished. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/run-all.log`. Pass only if it prints failures (none) and one summary line whose failure count is 0.\\n10. **evidence:** 1920×1080 screenshots of golden hour on the plain, the depot fire, a weapon viewmodel, the title, and the dev balance page, written under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to judge couch-readable type and that the scene reads as the sun-bleached style. Absence of a shot does not fail the goal when steps 1–9 pass.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- Copying Far Cry 2's map, mission graph, 50 km² size, malaria journal, real factions, or proper names. The inspired systems are the ones PLAN.md specifies, not a scene-for-scene port.\\n- Hang gliders, player-versus-player modes, an in-game map editor, and character customisation beyond the four-merc pick.\\n- FPS targets, draw-call optimisation, and other performance work TASK.md defers.\\n- A physical DualSense, a real iPhone or iPad, a TV, a second human, or audible speakers. Stub the pad, use a phone profile and WebKit, judge 1920×1080 shots, use two local pages, and measure audio offline or with an analyser.\\n- Extra modes or systems not in PLAN.md §5–§11 (for example a cure for the fever, or a minimap).\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: the folder that contains `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`. All new files stay there. Do not read or write sibling agent folders or shared `/tmp` project files.\\n- Dev subfolder `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/`, containing a shardfall engine clone (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall) and the new game, plus downloaded ai-hub files from https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub (`session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`).\\n- Final page and `exit-interview.json` in the work root. `PROGRESS.md` in the work root. Title notes and feedback notes inside the dev folder.\\n- Toolchain already on this machine: Rust and wasm32 target, `wasm-bindgen` 0.2.129, Python Playwright 1.63, Chromium, Firefox, WebKit. `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS` at most 12. `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` stays inside the dev folder. No `playwright install`. No `pkill`, `killall`, or `fuser -k`.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md as the design (milestones M0–M11, data in TOML, pure seeded simulation separate from rendering, UI, and the page). Verifiers judge the criteria above, not a matching directory tree or identical type names. Keep rules `Clone`-able and free of hashmap iteration order and wall-clock randomness so two runs of the same seed agree. Build the campaign bot beside each contract so the playthrough test stays cheap. Put numbers in data files. Expose game status and a test-command queue on the page for browser checks, and a no-draw switch so browser tests are not limited by software-rendered frame rate. Pack one HTML that runs from `file://` and still loads the unmodified session client first. Track answers for the feedback questions while working so About and `exit-interview.json` are filled from real notes, not blanks.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Clone the engine into the dev subfolder, pick the title with 50 names plus a real RNG, save the notes, register the game scene, place unmodified `session.js` first, and make the single test command print a one-line count.\\n- [x] Add first-person look and movement, the controller and keyboard layouts, a held weapon with the wind rag, hitscan, and a sky, and pass the combat sim tests.\\n- [x] Add the 2048 m country, wind-driven fire, weapon condition and jams, healing and fever, stealth and guard posts, and drivable vehicles and the boat; pass the world sim tests.\\n- [x] Add companions, every PLAN.md §11 contract, three endings, saves, and a seeded bot playthrough that sets every §15 coverage flag.\\n- [x] Add code-only music, effects, and gibberish voices with music at 10%, plus the 10-foot shell, dev-mode sliders, attract mode, touch-only notice, and About/Credits.\\n- [x] Add the hub depot round and standalone two-page play; pass the four harness cases and the solo check.\\n- [x] Pack `{GameTitle}-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html` into the work root, write `exit-interview.json` and `PROGRESS.md`, run the full test command and the file:// launch twice, and store the captured logs.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Fire advances from the downwind tips and the calm extremes, not a full neighbor flood; the country is a seeded height and fuel field shown through a dressed overlook, not a streamed terrain mesh; the 10-foot menus are the page shell, not an egui panel.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- Software WebGPU on this host is slow, and the wrong Chromium flags (`--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`) leave a blank canvas that still reports frames. Use the flags in verification step 6. A blank screenshot from a successful readback is an app failure. If the browser process cannot start, step 6's written fallback applies. earlyoom kills the largest browser under memory pressure; keep a single browser and stop it by PID.\\n- PLAN.md §1.5 allows cutting instance counts, never systems. Criterion 4 follows that. It is not a license to drop fire, jams, fever, companions, a contract row, or an ending.\\n- TASK.md also asks for the Source Information and the three feedback sections in the implementer's chat reply. The checkable copies are About/Credits and `exit-interview.json`; the reply must repeat them, and verifiers do not fail the goal only because they cannot see the chat.\\n- The hub harness needs a local server. Solo play must still work from `file://`. Do not treat a server-only page as meeting criterion 1.\\n\",\"total_lines\":61}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"aa21dbd2-0e15-47a7-b91f-41c1c76c32d6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_396","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a large verification task.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The contract is a full Far Cry 2-style slice with a packed HTML hand-in. I'll read the captured test logs and the plan's numbered requirements before judging the code."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a05df810-a283-4768-9940-3dfe90236305-5","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/tmp/grok-goal-8215da6fd761/implementer/run-all.log","limit":200}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a05df810-a283-4768-9940-3dfe90236305-6","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/PROGRESS.md","limit":150}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a05df810-a283-4768-9940-3dfe90236305-7","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"^## |^### |§1[1-5]|coverage|M0|acceptance","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/PLAN.md","head_limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a05df810-a283-4768-9940-3dfe90236305-8","name":"list_dir","input":{"target_directory":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8023,"output_tokens":549,"cache_read_input_tokens":26112,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aad8-e6b0-7c02-9fba-a7e5c5a4c558","uuid":"7df9f44e-2e2b-48eb-b4b2-98bb9f512fe1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-a05df810-a283-4768-9940-3dfe90236305-5","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→PASS 48 FAIL 0\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→PASS 48 FAIL 0\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/tmp/grok-goal-8215da6fd761/implementer/run-all.log\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":200,\"raw_output\":\"PASS 48 FAIL 0\\n\",\"total_lines\":1}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-a05df810-a283-4768-9940-3dfe90236305-6","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Jam Cry progress\\n\\nOriginal first-person contract shooter on the shardfall engine. The playable page is `Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html` in this folder. The dev folder of the same name holds the engine, the hub client, and `tests/run.sh`.\\n\\n- Title chosen from 50 riffs plus a real RNG. Notes are in the dev folder `notes/title.md`. Feedback notes are in `notes/feedback.md`.\\n- The `merc` scene is a headless country sim behind a first-person overlook. Look, hits, jams, fever, wind-driven fire, vehicles, companions, the contract list, and three endings are covered by `cargo test -p pav_core --test merc`. The seeded bot sets every coverage flag.\\n- Menus are a 10-foot shell on the page. Music and voices are synthesized. The hub depot round uses the unmodified session client as the first script.\\n- `tests/run.sh all` runs the sim, audio, browser, and hub checks and prints one failure count. The finished run printed `PASS 48 FAIL 0`.\\n\\n10→## Deviations\\n\\n- Fire advances from the downwind tips and the calm extremes, not a full neighbor flood.\\n- The country is a seeded height and fuel field. The window is a small dressed overlook, not a streamed terrain mesh.\\n- The 10-foot menus are the page shell, not an egui panel.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Jam Cry progress\\n\\nOriginal first-person contract shooter on the shardfall engine. The playable page is `Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html` in this folder. The dev folder of the same name holds the engine, the hub client, and `tests/run.sh`.\\n\\n- Title chosen from 50 riffs plus a real RNG. Notes are in the dev folder `notes/title.md`. Feedback notes are in `notes/feedback.md`.\\n- The `merc` scene is a headless country sim behind a first-person overlook. Look, hits, jams, fever, wind-driven fire, vehicles, companions, the contract list, and three endings are covered by `cargo test -p pav_core --test merc`. The seeded bot sets every coverage flag.\\n- Menus are a 10-foot shell on the page. Music and voices are synthesized. The hub depot round uses the unmodified session client as the first script.\\n- `tests/run.sh all` runs the sim, audio, browser, and hub checks and prints one failure count. The finished run printed `PASS 48 FAIL 0`.\\n\\n10→## Deviations\\n\\n- Fire advances from the downwind tips and the calm extremes, not a full neighbor flood.\\n- The country is a seeded height and fuel field. The window is a small dressed overlook, not a streamed terrain mesh.\\n- The 10-foot menus are the page shell, not an egui panel.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/PROGRESS.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":150,\"raw_output\":\"# Jam Cry progress\\n\\nOriginal first-person contract shooter on the shardfall engine. The playable page is `Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503.html` in this folder. The dev folder of the same name holds the engine, the hub client, and `tests/run.sh`.\\n\\n- Title chosen from 50 riffs plus a real RNG. Notes are in the dev folder `notes/title.md`. Feedback notes are in `notes/feedback.md`.\\n- The `merc` scene is a headless country sim behind a first-person overlook. Look, hits, jams, fever, wind-driven fire, vehicles, companions, the contract list, and three endings are covered by `cargo test -p pav_core --test merc`. The seeded bot sets every coverage flag.\\n- Menus are a 10-foot shell on the page. Music and voices are synthesized. The hub depot round uses the unmodified session client as the first script.\\n- `tests/run.sh all` runs the sim, audio, browser, and hub checks and prints one failure count. The finished run printed `PASS 48 FAIL 0`.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Fire advances from the downwind tips and the calm extremes, not a full neighbor flood.\\n- The country is a seeded height and fuel field. The window is a small dressed overlook, not a streamed terrain mesh.\\n- The 10-foot menus are the page shell, not an egui panel.\\n\",\"total_lines\":14}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-a05df810-a283-4768-9940-3dfe90236305-7","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,97,116,32,108,101,97,115,116,32,56,48,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,53,45,48,49,51,53,48,51,95,95,120,98,100,55,100,57,49,47,119,111,114,107,47,80,76,65,78,46,109,100,10,57,58,45,32,42,42,82,101,97,100,105,110,103,58,42,42,32,114,101,97,100,32,194,167,49,32,116,111,32,194,167,51,32,119,104,111,108,101,32,98,101,102,111,114,101,32,121,111,117,32,115,116,97,114,116,46,32,82,101,97,100,32,116,104,101,32,114,101,115,116,32,97,115,32,121,111,117,32,114,101,97,99,104