{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_402","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a significant architectural shortcut.\n\nThe simulation is headless and abstract. A tiny overlook room with a fixed third-person body sits on a 2048m map. The camera eye stays locked in first person at a fixed position. Only yaw and pitch rotate. The rifle and rag are static boxes. Menus render through an HTML shell. The player never moves through the country visually.\n\nThe abstract World tracks movement, stance, and yaw. Tests validate these underlying coordinates. The rendered camera eye is fixed at [0.15, 1.55, 4.2]. Stance changes World.eye but aim_overlook only copies yaw and pitch. Eye height is not transferred.\n\nThe `aim_overlook` function only updates yaw and pitch. The eye height is set once at initialization and never changes again. Tests validate the abstract World eye coordinates, not the rendered camera position.\n\nThe core objective is a playable first-person game. The visual first-person movement through the country is the primary flow. World.x and World.z update correctly. The third-person body resets to its spawn position every frame.\n\nThe HTML shell might only overlay menus on a static diorama. I need to inspect the page shell and world.tick to confirm whether gameplay actually drives the world or merely displays a frozen scene.\n\nCriterion 6 confirms that keyboard input changes UI state. The visual may remain the overlook.\n\nThe plan's acceptance criteria target simulation behavior rather than a full terrain mesh. The deviations section explicitly documents this shortcut and the implementer updated the plan accordingly.\n\nThe verifier instructions state that a weakened, deleted, or self-serving criterion is grounds to refute.\n\nThe acceptance criteria still require a 2048 m × 2048 m open country with dry grass, laterite roads, a river, settlements, and a day-night cycle. The bot walks the abstract numbers, but the human player sees only the same fixed overlook regardless of where they are in the world. This creates a direct contradiction between the stated requirements and the actual visual output.\n\nThis is a legitimate core objective failure, not a minor nitpick. PLAN.md §6 describes a visual terrain with grass, trees, and time of day. A static diorama with abstract state flags cannot demonstrate fire spread, vehicle driving, or country exploration. The vertical slice must be visually playable so a player can understand the game feel.\n\nThe anti-overreach rule forbids refuting for missing non-goals or extra scope. It also requires returning Not Refuted when every criterion is met, even if more could be added. The code-correctness floor covers unreachable flows and placeholders, but the anti-overreach rule prevents extending the verdict to polish or fidelity.\n\nThe rendered camera is a first-person view, but it is fixed inside the overlook room looking at a third-person mannequin and box trees. The eye position sits at [0.15, 1.55, 4.2], while the character spawns near z=0. The player sees the character from behind or in front, not through the character's own eyes. This confirms the visual perspective is not the player's own first-person view.\n\nThe character faces the camera, confirming a title-screen diorama rather than a first-person view. Yaw changes rotate the fixed eye around the static mannequin and boxes. WASD updates World.x and World.z but never moves the camera position.\n\nI need to inspect the HTML shell and world status to see if a HUD displays position or if gameplay is purely menu-driven. If the 3D viewport is decorative and only a state machine beneath it, the game would be a menu-driven abstract simulation.\n\nCriterion 2 explicitly requires the player to look and move in first person on foot. The tests assert this behavior on the World state. If World.tick implements stance changes, stick movement, and mouse-look correctly, the test assertions pass. The visual disconnect remains a real defect if the criterion demands a player-facing experience.\n\nCriterion 2 is judged by simulation tests, not the visual camera. Step 6 concerns the title screen launch. Step 7 checks that browser pointer-lock mousemove updates yaw in the published status JSON. The browser test likely validates this abstract state rather than rendered pixels.\n\nThe verifier instructions require the launch gate to assert the deliverable's primary observable is correct. The title screen is the primary observable for launch, but the primary observable for gameplay is first-person movement through the country. This distinction matters for the final verdict.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The page paints a title over a fixed overlook. I'll check whether movement, fire, and contracts are real simulation or flags the bot sets by script."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eb0504e6-3401-47e6-a27b-31a30cc084e8-32","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/merc/world.rs","offset":250,"limit":450}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eb0504e6-3401-47e6-a27b-31a30cc084e8-33","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/merc/fire.rs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eb0504e6-3401-47e6-a27b-31a30cc084e8-34","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-013503__xbd7d91/work/Jam Cry-Far Cry 2-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261105-013503/engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/merc/gun.rs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7189,"output_tokens":2582,"cache_read_input_tokens":67968,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sess