e neighbor-killinga tank in that brief window. The transition logic that sets HP  to mounted is loggedto zero without firing after the first full tick of gameplay, so a shot is intentional the goal change occurs chain reaction behavior. during that initial The `doomed_enough` tick. function is dead code; the real patch path uses `matches!` instead, so this is not a bug.\n\nContract flags are stamped at step start and marked done immediately. This is by design for contract markers and was accepted in prior rounds.\n\nThe Better step sets its flag when the player interacts with the companion. Walking there first satisfies the prior gap requirement that the flag be set only after the player takes\n\nThe log timestamp  the offered approach.is rounded to the nearest integer second. With\n\nI still need to verify whether the packed  a half-second tick HTML matches the simulation.interval, two ticks can share the same displayed second. The step advance from ignite11 to Tanks occurs during the tick simulation, so the transition is logged\n\nI am now checking  at the end of that whether the packed HTMLsame tick. is stale. If the Rust code was changed but the WASM binary was not rebuilt, the playable game would still contain the old bugs. The objective is the game in the folder, so a stale packed page would be a real defect.\n\nA single tick of shooting is possible, but the aim logic prevents instant kills. The bot only fires when yaw error is below 0.2 radians; otherwise it turns and defers firing. The nearest tank sits exactly at the player's xz position.\n\nThe packed HTML is the human-playable game, so a stale WASM binary would ship old behavior. The Drive cheat set seat=1 in the bot script, which only runs during tests; fixing it does not affect human play. The Tanks chain reaction is new behavior for human shooting, and the packed page would still show the old bug if the binary was not rebuilt.\n\nI am checking whether `begin()` runs for human play or only the campaign 